const COURSE = {"title":"Robotics — one small step","version":"balancewise-robotics-2026-10-11-v1","weeks":[{"number":1,"title":"Introduction to Autonomous Mobile Robots","note":"Follows the supplied Week 1 lecture pages 1–33, then demonstration pages 1–37. Assessment figures are attributed to the Week 1 lecture. Older desktop images and statistics remain labelled historical.","sections":[{"id":"w1-s1","title":"What this course teaches and how we will follow it","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 1 Student.pdf","pages":"1–11"},"cards":[{"title":"Your first small idea","text":["A robot is a machine that senses something, computes what to do and produces an action. Autonomous means it can carry out a task using its own decisions, within the limits people set.","This game focuses on mobile robots: robots that travel. Our main examples move on a flat, two-dimensional surface. Two-dimensional means we describe the floor using two directions, x and y."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"What each tool does","text":["Ubuntu is the operating system: it manages programs and hardware. ROS 2 connects robot software parts. Gazebo simulates a robot and its physical surroundings.","The lecturer specifies Ubuntu 22.04 with ROS 2 and Gazebo for practical work. A different host operating system can use a dedicated Ubuntu 22.04 environment. A container is a separate software environment sharing the host computer’s Linux kernel."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Five learning outcomes in plain words","text":["Goal 1: understand robot systems and the software used to build them. Goal 2: consider effects on people, including safety, privacy and fairness.","Goal 3: judge how well a robot design meets its purpose. Goal 4: choose and analyse sensors and algorithms; an algorithm is a sequence of steps for solving a problem. Goal 5: investigate independently and question evidence.","Example of ethics: a road camera may capture people. Explain what data the project needs and avoid collecting more than the task requires."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"The exact learning route","text":["Lecture explains ideas; demonstration shows tools; workshop lets you practise; individual research project brings the parts together. Feedback during workshops helps you improve before final assessment.","Follow the week slots in the supplied outline: Week 1 introduction; Week 2 locomotion; Week 3 kinematics and control; Week 4 sensing; Week 5 vision; Week 6 enhancement; Weeks 7–8 localisation; Week 9 maps and SLAM; Weeks 10–11 planning and navigation; Week 12 recap and assessment support. The tentative outline calls Week 11 behaviour trees, but the actual Week 11 lecture supplied here teaches graph path planning. We follow that actual lecture within the same week slot.","SLAM means simultaneous localisation and mapping: estimating where the robot is while building its map. Behaviour trees organise decisions into small tasks; they appear in the supplied Nav2 overview. We will explain these when the relevant materials introduce them. Timetable rooms and times printed in the slides are source information, not a freshly verified timetable."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Finding support and useful reading","kind":"friendly","text":["Use the explanations, worked examples and research links in this game to support your study. For a deeper treatment, a useful textbook is Introduction to Autonomous Mobile Robots by Siegwart, Nourbakhsh and Scaramuzza.","The lecture connects these skills to factory automation, agriculture, healthcare, autonomous driving and Industry 4.0: industry using connected digital systems. Its Monday lecture and Tuesday workshop schedule is printed source information; check your current timetable for attendance."]}],"practice":{"prompt":"Explain Ubuntu, ROS 2 and Gazebo using one simple sentence each.","expected":"Ubuntu runs software; ROS 2 connects robot components; Gazebo simulates physical behaviour."},"quiz":[{"prompt":"Which tool models physical robot movement and sensors?","choices":["Ubuntu","Gazebo","A research report"],"answer":1,"why":"Gazebo runs the physical simulation; Ubuntu runs programs and the report describes evidence.","remediate":"Picture a toy robot inside a pretend room. Gazebo supplies that pretend room and its physics."},{"prompt":"Your system works, but records unnecessary images of people. Which outcome asks you to think about that?","choices":["Goal 2: ethical and social implications","Goal 3: evaluate the design only","No course outcome"],"answer":0,"why":"Ethical reasoning asks how a robot affects people as well as whether it functions.","remediate":"Ethics means considering effects on people. Privacy is one example."},{"prompt":"Which learning order matches the lecturer’s plan?","choices":["Vision before locomotion","SLAM before Week 1","Introduction → locomotion → kinematics/control"],"answer":2,"why":"The first three scheduled theory topics establish what the robot does, how it moves and how to calculate/control motion.","remediate":"Read the first three week names again: introduction, locomotion, kinematics/control."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w1-s2","title":"Your road inspection project and the evidence it needs","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 1 Student.pdf","pages":"12–16"},"cards":[{"title":"The task has several parts","text":["Survey means cover and inspect the driving area. A pothole is a damaged depression in a road surface. Detection means deciding that a pothole is present.","Localisation means finding its location. Geometry estimation means measuring its shape or size. Reporting means presenting the results clearly; visualisation means showing them on a map."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"A complete small example","text":["Imagine the robot sees one road defect. It reports a map position, a measured area with units, and an image showing why it classified that region as a pothole.","If the camera sees that same defect in ten frames, that is ten observations of one pothole. A frame is one camera image. The final count must describe unique potholes, rather than every image containing one."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"What the supplied Week 1 lecture says","text":["The lecture specifies an individual 3500-word research report worth 65%, and a robot demonstration worth 35%. It states five minutes per student plus questions and submission of a video demonstration.","These figures are from the supplied Week 1 lecture, pages 12–15. Use the current project assessment brief for deadlines, detailed marking criteria and any later changes; those were not supplied as a separate brief here."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"What proves progress","text":["A simulator opening shows setup progress. A successful route shows navigation progress. Accurate pothole locations and sizes show measurement progress. A report needs evidence for each claim.","A sensible record says what was tested, what happened, what failed and what uncertainty remains. Uncertainty means how much the estimate could differ from the true value.","The supplied detector contains a placeholder for your detection code. A red marker from that unfinished example is not evidence that it detected a pothole."],"kind":"danger"}],"practice":{"prompt":"Write the six project verbs in order: survey, capture, detect, locate, measure, report.","expected":"You can explain why each step is needed and why a simulator screenshot alone cannot prove all six."},"quiz":[{"prompt":"The camera sees the same pothole in ten successive frames. What should the unique-pothole count be?","choices":["Ten","Zero automatically","One"],"answer":2,"why":"Several images can describe the same physical defect; observations must be associated before counting.","remediate":"One apple photographed ten times remains one apple."},{"prompt":"Which weighting is stated in the supplied Week 1 lecture?","choices":["Report 65%, demonstration 35%","Report 35%, demonstration 65%","Report 100%"],"answer":0,"why":"Those percentages belong to the supplied lecture’s assessment description.","remediate":"The report has the larger share:65 out of 100."},{"prompt":"Gazebo opens and a robot appears. What does this establish?","choices":["The whole assignment is finished","The simulator setup has reached a useful checkpoint","Pothole area estimates are correct"],"answer":1,"why":"Startup is one step. It does not show autonomous coverage, recognition or measurement accuracy.","remediate":"Separate opening the lab from completing the experiment."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w1-s3","title":"Mobile robots and their three questions","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 1 Student.pdf","pages":"17–20"},"cards":[{"title":"A fixed robot and a travelling robot","text":["A manipulator is a robot arm that moves objects within its reach, such as a welding arm in a factory. Its base may stay fixed.","A mobile robot travels through an environment. Locomotion means the physical method it uses to move: wheels, legs, propellers or another mechanism."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"The three questions","text":["Where am I? The robot needs its current position and direction. Where am I going? It needs a destination or task. How do I get there? It needs a route it can follow.","Pose means position plus orientation. Orientation is which way the robot faces. On a floor, a pose can be written as(x, y,θ), where θ (theta) is the heading angle."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Use a familiar example","text":["You are in a library and want a particular shelf. You need to identify where you stand, choose the shelf and find a route around the furniture.","A robot does similar jobs using a model of the environment, sensor readings, an estimated pose and movement decisions. A model is a simplified description, such as a floor map."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"See → think → act, then repeat","text":["See: sensors measure the surroundings. Think: software interprets the readings and chooses what to do. Act: motors move the robot.","The loop repeats so the robot can respond to changes. If a chair blocks the route, a new reading should affect the next movement decision. This repeated use of results is feedback."],"kind":"friendly"}],"practice":{"prompt":"Use walking to a bus stop to explain the three questions and the see–think–act loop.","expected":"You name your start, destination, route and one change that new observations could cause."},"quiz":[{"prompt":"Which list gives the three mobile-robot questions?","choices":["Where am I? Where am I going? How do I get there?","What colour? What brand? What price?","Which laptop? Which screen? Which keyboard?"],"answer":0,"why":"Autonomous navigation needs a current state, a goal and a way to reach the goal.","remediate":"Imagine finding a room in a building: start location, destination, route."},{"prompt":"A pose contains which information?","choices":["Only colour","Position and orientation","Only speed"],"answer":1,"why":"Facing direction matters as well as location, because it affects the motion the robot can take next.","remediate":"Two robots can stand on the same spot while facing opposite directions."},{"prompt":"A new obstacle appears. What should a feedback loop do?","choices":["Ignore it","Replay the old motor commands unchanged","Use the new reading in its next decision"],"answer":2,"why":"The point of feedback is to update behaviour using what actually happened.","remediate":"See again before choosing the next action."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w1-s4","title":"Motion and perception: moving and measuring","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 1 Student.pdf","pages":"21–24"},"cards":[{"title":"Kinematics means describing motion","text":["Kinematics studies motion using geometry, position and speed. At this point we only need the words; detailed wheel equations belong to Week 3.","Linear motion changes position along a direction. Angular motion changes orientation by turning. Metres measure distance; radians are a unit for angle."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"A tiny movement calculation","text":["If a robot really travels at 0.10 metres per second for 2 seconds, distance=speed×time=0.20 metres. That is 20 centimetres.","This calculation assumes the speed holds and the robot can move freely. A command asking for 0.10 m/s does not prove the physical robot actually achieved it."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Sensors provide measurements","text":["A sensor measures a property of the world or the robot. A laser scanner estimates distances. A camera produces images.","A laser can time a light pulse travelling out and back; this is time of flight. A camera requires software to extract useful information from its pixels. A pixel is one small element of an image."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Perception gives measurements meaning","text":["A range reading may say something is 0.8 metres away. Perception asks what that reading means for the task: is there an obstacle in front?","A dark patch in an image may be a pothole, a shadow or a stain. Recognition needs evidence and testing. We will study sensing in Week 4 and robot vision in Week 5."],"kind":"readmore"}],"practice":{"prompt":"Say which is measurement and which is interpretation: “0.8 m ahead” and “an obstacle blocks my route”.","expected":"The distance is a measurement; the obstacle decision is an interpretation."},"quiz":[{"prompt":"Which change is angular motion?","choices":["Moving 0.2 metres straight ahead","Turning to face left","Changing a file name"],"answer":1,"why":"Turning changes the robot’s heading angle.","remediate":"Linear: travel along. Angular: turn around."},{"prompt":"At a steady 0.10 m/s for 2 s, how far does a robot travel?","choices":["0.20 m","2 m","20 m"],"answer":0,"why":"Multiplying metres per second by seconds leaves metres:0.10×2=0.20.","remediate":"Imagine 10 centimetres each second. Two seconds gives 20 centimetres."},{"prompt":"Why is a camera image not yet a pothole report?","choices":["Images contain no information","The screen must be red","Software must interpret and validate what the pixels represent"],"answer":2,"why":"Measurements require interpretation, location and reliable classification before reporting a defect.","remediate":"A picture of a patch does not by itself explain what caused the patch."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w1-s5","title":"Localisation, maps and routes","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 1 Student.pdf","pages":"25–33"},"cards":[{"title":"Where am I?","text":["Localisation means estimating the robot’s pose in its environment. Odometry estimates movement from information such as wheel motion.","Wheel slip can make odometry drift: the estimate gradually differs from reality. Sensor observations of known features, such as a pillar, can help correct the estimate. A belief is an estimate with uncertainty, rather than guaranteed truth."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"What does the place look like?","text":["Mapping means building a model of the surroundings. You can draw a map by hand or let a robot construct one from measurements.","An occupancy grid divides the floor into small cells. Each cell describes whether space is free, occupied or unknown. Unknown means the robot lacks enough information; it does not mean safely empty."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"How do I get there?","text":["Global path planning chooses a route across the map. Graph search is one way to compare connected places and find a route. A graph is a set of points joined by links.","Local planning and collision avoidance respond to nearby obstacles while travelling. Collision means physical contact with an obstacle. The global route and local decisions must work together."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"One small story","text":["The robot estimates it is beside a pillar, finds a destination on its map and plans around a wall. While driving, a new box appears, so nearby obstacle avoidance adjusts the movement.","A good map alone is insufficient: the robot needs to know where it is in that map. The suggested reading is Chapter 1 of Introduction to Autonomous Mobile Robots. This section closes the Week 1 lecture before the Linux demonstration begins."],"kind":"friendly"}],"practice":{"prompt":"Explain localisation and mapping using “me on the map” and “the map itself”.","expected":"Localisation finds your pose; mapping creates or updates the description of the surroundings."},"quiz":[{"prompt":"A perfect map is available but the robot does not know its location. What is missing?","choices":["More file names","A bigger monitor","Localisation"],"answer":2,"why":"A route needs a meaningful starting pose in the map.","remediate":"A building map cannot guide you from your current spot until you identify that spot."},{"prompt":"An unknown grid cell means what?","choices":["Enough evidence of free space","Insufficient information about that cell","Definitely a wall"],"answer":1,"why":"Unknown is its own state; assuming it is clear could lead to a collision.","remediate":"Unknown means “I have not established what is there”."},{"prompt":"What role checks a new obstacle close to the moving robot?","choices":["Local planning/collision avoidance","Only a printed global map","A package name"],"answer":0,"why":"Fresh nearby observations affect immediate movement decisions.","remediate":"Global: the broad route. Local: what to do near the robot now."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w1-s6","title":"Demonstration: Linux and your desktop","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 1 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"1–9"},"cards":[{"title":"Linux and Ubuntu","text":["Linux is the core of a family of operating systems. A distribution combines that core with applications and supporting software. Ubuntu is the distribution used in this course.","Open source means the source code is available under licences that permit specified kinds of reuse and modification. Source code is the readable instructions programmers write."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Find and open an application","text":["Use your application launcher or search to find Terminal, Files or VS Code. Terminal accepts typed commands; Files lets you browse folders; VS Code is an editor for program text.","A pinned launcher icon is a shortcut to an application. Focus means the window that currently receives your keyboard input. Clicking a window brings it forward and usually gives it focus."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Read older screenshots with care","text":["The demonstration shows an older Ubuntu desktop: window buttons at top left, a global menu and triangle markers. A newer desktop can place these controls differently.","Learn the purpose of search, launchers, notifications and window controls rather than expecting the old screenshot to match every pixel. The course concept remains the same."],"kind":"readmore"},{"title":"Your two environments","text":["The host is your main Ubuntu desktop. The course’s Humble container is the separate Ubuntu 22.04 software environment. Editing the files in VS Code on the host is compatible with running course ROS commands inside the container.","The historical setup note says host ROS is Jazzy and container ROS is Humble. Check the active terminal before course commands; a window looking familiar does not tell you which environment it uses."],"kind":"danger"}],"practice":{"prompt":"Find Terminal and Files using your own application launcher. Bring each window to the front.","expected":"You recognise launching versus focusing an already-open application."},"quiz":[{"prompt":"Ubuntu is best described as what?","choices":["A robot wheel","A Linux distribution","A ROS topic"],"answer":1,"why":"Ubuntu combines the Linux kernel with the software needed for an operating environment.","remediate":"Linux is the underlying family; Ubuntu is a particular packaged distribution."},{"prompt":"The old slide has buttons on the left, but yours has them on the right. What should you do?","choices":["Identify controls by function on your desktop","Reinstall everything immediately","Assume all course files are wrong"],"answer":0,"why":"The demonstration’s interface images are historical. Moving a button does not change what closing or maximising does.","remediate":"Learn the purpose of the control, then find its current position."},{"prompt":"Where should Humble course ROS commands run on your saved setup?","choices":["In any browser tab","Only in an image viewer","Inside ros-humble-limo"],"answer":2,"why":"The container holds the software baseline used by the supplied simulator.","remediate":"Host desktop edits files; Humble container runs the course robotics software."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w1-s7","title":"Demonstration: folders and terminal commands","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 1 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"10–14"},"cards":[{"title":"A path tells you where something lives","text":["Linux starts its folder tree at /, called the filesystem root. Folder names in a path are separated by /. A typical user home is /home/your-username; $HOME names your own home folder.","A path beginning with / is absolute: it starts at the root. A relative path begins from the current directory. A directory is another word for a folder. Names beginning with a dot, such as .git, are hidden from ordinary listings."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Read-only first steps","text":["pwd prints the current directory. ls lists its contents. ls -a includes dot names. cd changes directory; it does not move your files.","Tab can complete names. Ctrl+C interrupts a running foreground command. A simulator may keep occupying a terminal while it works; that is normal. Ctrl+Shift+T commonly opens a new terminal tab."],"kind":"friendly","code":"pwd\nls\nls -a\ncd \"$HOME/Documents/Robotics Study\"\npwd\nls"},{"title":"Understand the other slide commands","text":["cp copies a source to a destination. mv moves a file or renames it. rm removes a file. These commands can affect real files; this lesson explains their meaning without asking you to delete anything.","ps displays running processes. A process is a running program, identified by a process ID, or PID. kill sends a signal to a process. Use Ctrl+C in its own terminal first when ending your course simulator."],"kind":"danger"},{"title":"Spaces need quotes","text":["The course folder name has a space. Quotes keep “Robotics Study” together as one path instead of two separate command arguments.","An argument is extra information passed to a command. In cd \"$HOME/Documents/Robotics Study\", $HOME expands to your home directory and the quotes protect the whole path. Container HOME refers to its dedicated home; use the document path from the host."],"kind":"friendly"}],"practice":{"prompt":"From the host, use pwd and ls to locate the flat course documents folder. Do not move or delete anything.","expected":"You can recognise the original lecture PDFs, the ZIP, study companion and three screenshots."},"quiz":[{"prompt":"Which command answers “Which folder am I in?”","choices":["pwd","mv","rm"],"answer":0,"why":"pwd reports your working directory without changing files.","remediate":"pwd means print working directory."},{"prompt":"Why quote a path containing Robotics Study?","choices":["Quotes make the folder hidden","Quotes let the shell treat the space-containing path as one argument","Quotes install ROS"],"answer":1,"why":"Without quotes, the shell normally splits at spaces.","remediate":"A folder name can contain a space; the command still needs one complete folder name."},{"prompt":"Which slide command removes a file?","choices":["ls","pwd","rm"],"answer":2,"why":"rm performs removal, so inspect the path before ever using it.","remediate":"Listing is ls. Reading your location is pwd. Removing is rm."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w1-s8","title":"Demonstration: what ROS adds","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 1 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"15–22"},"cards":[{"title":"Why reusable parts matter","text":["Before shared robot frameworks, teams often repeated device drivers, message handling and algorithms. A driver is software that helps a program communicate with hardware.","ROS supplies conventions and tools so components can cooperate. Middleware means software between applications and the operating system that helps those applications communicate."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"The name can be misleading","text":["ROS means Robot Operating System, but it does not replace Ubuntu. A useful picture is Ubuntu underneath, ROS communication in the middle, and robot programs above.","C++ and Python programs can exchange agreed message types. A robot application can reuse a sensor driver and a navigation component instead of rebuilding both from scratch."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Distributions are versions with a baseline","text":["A ROS distribution is a named release family. The demonstration recommends ROS 2 Humble with Ubuntu 22.04. Match that baseline when following these practical examples.","The slide’s table calls Noetic and Iron active even though their listed dates already ended. Treat it as a historical slide. Official release/platform information confirms Humble’s Ubuntu 22.04 baseline; keep course commands on Humble."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"History is background","text":["The slides introduce ROS’s 2007 origins, Willow Garage and later Open Robotics stewardship. They show older ROS 1 distribution names and a 2023 download chart.","These explain how the ecosystem developed; they are not current statistics or a requirement to learn every old release name. Open source licences vary across packages: do not assume every dependency uses one identical licence."],"kind":"readmore"}],"practice":{"prompt":"Explain why installing ROS does not mean replacing the operating system.","expected":"You can distinguish the computer platform from the robot middleware."},"quiz":[{"prompt":"ROS runs in place of Ubuntu: true or false?","choices":["True, Ubuntu is unnecessary","False, ROS provides robot software tools on an operating system","True whenever a camera is connected"],"answer":1,"why":"The robot programs and ROS still need an operating system underneath them.","remediate":"Ubuntu is the platform running programs. ROS helps robot programs work together."},{"prompt":"What is a driver?","choices":["A person steering the simulator only","The final report","Software communicating with a device"],"answer":2,"why":"Drivers adapt hardware interfaces so applications can use sensors or motors.","remediate":"A camera driver gets camera data into software."},{"prompt":"Which baseline matches this course’s saved environment?","choices":["Humble inside Ubuntu 22.04 container","Mix Jazzy and Humble libraries in one terminal","Any old release with the same logo"],"answer":0,"why":"The supplied simulator uses the Humble/Gazebo Classic environment.","remediate":"A distribution is a compatible set of tools. Use the matched set."}],"research":[{"title":"Official ROS2 platform policy","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ros-infrastructure/rep/master/rep-2000.rst","addition":"Checked distribution support and Ubuntu targets. Added a historical-table warning and the Humble/Ubuntu 22.04 baseline.","anchor":"Week 1 demonstration 19–20"}]},{"id":"w1-s9","title":"Demonstration: the ROS ecosystem and communication","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 1 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"23–34"},"cards":[{"title":"Break a big job into small jobs","text":["A robot can have a camera component, an image-processing component and a wheel-control component. Their agreed interfaces tell each one what data to send or receive.","Distributed software can run components on several computers. Multilingual software can combine different programming languages. Reuse means using an existing suitable part again."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"ROS1 and ROS2 are different","text":["The demonstration contrasts ROS 1’s master with ROS 2’s discovery between participants. A master is a central manager in that older design; ROS 2’s graph does not require that ROS 1 master.","A parameter is a named setting, such as an update rate. In ROS 2 parameters belong to nodes. A node is a participant doing a particular job in the robot system; we will inspect nodes in Week 2."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Tools help you see what is happening","text":["A log records messages such as errors or progress. Plotting draws measurements over time. Diagnostics report component health. Visualisation shows robot data in a useful form.","RViz displays ROS data such as maps, scans and robot models. Gazebo simulates physics and sensors. A problem in the RViz view is different from a robot failing to move in Gazebo."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Where to read and ask","text":["The slides point to official ROS documentation and tutorials, ROS Discourse, Robotics Stack Exchange and ROSCon. Use Humble documentation for this course.","The ecosystem includes community packages for mapping, planning, vision and more. The 2023 repository counts and visitor charts in the lecture are historical examples of growth, not current counts. Read a package’s description, compatibility and licence before reusing it."],"kind":"readmore"}],"practice":{"prompt":"Name one measurement you would plot and one thing you would show in RViz.","expected":"For example: speed over time in a plot, and the robot’s scan or map in RViz."},"quiz":[{"prompt":"What does a ROS 2 parameter describe?","choices":["A named setting belonging to a node","A physical pothole automatically","A new Ubuntu login"],"answer":0,"why":"Parameters configure how a node behaves.","remediate":"Think of a setting such as camera update rate."},{"prompt":"Which pairing is correct?","choices":["RViz simulates physics; Gazebo writes reports","RViz visualises ROS data; Gazebo simulates robot physics","Both are only text editors"],"answer":1,"why":"They serve different parts of the robotics workflow.","remediate":"Gazebo is the pretend physical world. RViz is a display of robot data."},{"prompt":"Do the slide’s 2023 repository counts establish today’s numbers?","choices":["Yes, numbers never change","Only if the slide is blue","No, they are historical statistics"],"answer":2,"why":"The source labels them with a past measurement date.","remediate":"Always keep a statistic attached to its date."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w1-s10","title":"Demonstration: installing a package versus building source","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 1 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"35–37"},"cards":[{"title":"Two ways to obtain software","text":["A binary package contains software already prepared for a supported platform. Ubuntu’s apt package manager can install Debian .deb packages and declared dependencies.","Source installation means obtaining the readable project files, then building and installing them in a workspace. A dependency is another library or package required by the software."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Read the installation pattern","text":["The lecture pattern is sudo apt install ros-humble-package. sudo requests administrator privileges; apt manages packages; install asks to add software; replace “package” with the actual package name.","Debian ROS package names use hyphens, such as ros-humble-turtlesim. ROS package identifiers may use underscores, such as nav2_map_server. The slide’s old /opt/ros/kinetic example belongs to a different historical distribution."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Match the task to the method","text":["Prepared packages are usually easier for standard tools. Source is useful for lecturer code and your own changes, but needs a compatible build environment.","If your host uses another ROS distribution, use a dedicated Humble environment for this course. The practical examples assume an existing container named ros-humble-limo. They are not instructions to replace your host installation."],"kind":"danger"},{"title":"Your next practical step","text":["Week 2 introduces the workspace and build process. Your downloaded ZIP is a source archive, not a running robot and not a generated build folder.","A source archive stores project files together. The separate course documents folder keeps your teaching material together; the live ROS workspace keeps code inside src with build outputs beside it. Both structures serve their own purpose."],"kind":"friendly"}],"practice":{"prompt":"Identify the course ZIP and the live src folder without extracting or moving either.","expected":"You can say which contains an archived baseline and which is the current working code."},"quiz":[{"prompt":"What is a dependency?","choices":["A screen colour","Software another package requires","Only a folder shortcut"],"answer":1,"why":"A package may need libraries and tools supplied by other packages.","remediate":"If a camera program needs a camera library, that library is its dependency."},{"prompt":"What is the downloaded course ZIP?","choices":["An archive of project source files","A verified running simulator","The generated install/setup.bash from your live workspace"],"answer":0,"why":"The archive stores source; running and building use the separate compatible environment.","remediate":"Downloading the ingredients is different from running the program."},{"prompt":"Which pair shows the naming distinction?","choices":["Both must use spaces","Both must use capital letters","ROS nav2_map_server and Debian ros-humble-nav2-map-server"],"answer":2,"why":"The ROS identifier and Debian package name use different naming conventions.","remediate":"Underscores can be in the ROS name; apt uses hyphens in this pattern."}],"research":[]}]},{"number":2,"title":"Locomotion","note":"Follows the supplied Week 2 lecture pages 1–35, then demonstration pages 1–27. Formal differential-drive equations belong to Week 3. Workspace paths and seven packages were inspected read-only.","sections":[{"id":"w2-s1","title":"Locomotion: choosing a way to move","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 2 2026 lecture.pdf","pages":"1–7"},"cards":[{"title":"We are now studying Act","text":["Locomotion means interacting physically with the environment to travel. In the see–think–act loop this week focuses on Act: producing motion.","An actuator turns energy into physical action. A motor is an actuator. Mechanisms such as wheels or legs turn actuator movement into useful travel."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Three lenses","text":["Stability asks whether the robot stays upright: consider contact positions, centre of gravity and slope. Centre of gravity is the point where we represent the weight acting.","Contact asks how the robot touches the surface: shape, area, angle and friction. Friction is resistance at touching surfaces; enough traction helps the robot push against the ground. Environment asks about terrain and medium: hard floor, soft ground, air or water."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Nature suggests options, not automatic answers","text":["Animals walk, swim or fly using bodies adapted to their environment. Copying them can require complicated mechanisms and control. Engineering also uses wheels and propellers.","The lecture’s general observation about smaller animals flying is a trend discussion, not a rule applying to every animal. The design question is what matches the robot’s mission."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Wheels and legs have different costs","text":["On a smooth warehouse floor, rolling avoids repeatedly lifting the body and can be efficient. On steps or rubble, legs can select where each foot lands.","Energy efficiency means using less energy for the task. Legs usually require more actuators and coordination. Neither choice wins for every surface; compare terrain, energy, mechanical complexity and control."],"kind":"friendly"}],"practice":{"prompt":"Choose wheels or legs for a smooth warehouse and for broken rubble, and give a reason for each.","expected":"You connect the choice to the terrain rather than treating one mechanism as universally best."},"quiz":[{"prompt":"Which three lenses organise the lecture?","choices":["Price, logo, colour","File, folder, screen","Stability, contact, environment"],"answer":2,"why":"They cover balance, surface interaction and the setting in which motion happens.","remediate":"Ask: will it stay upright, how does it touch, where does it move?"},{"prompt":"Why are wheels often suitable for a flat warehouse floor?","choices":["Rolling can be efficient with simpler mechanisms","They can step over every obstacle","They do not need actuators"],"answer":0,"why":"The floor supports continuous rolling with relatively simple control and hardware.","remediate":"Smooth floor plus rolling is a good match."},{"prompt":"What does an actuator do?","choices":["Stores lecture PDFs","Converts energy into physical action","Describes a map cell"],"answer":1,"why":"Motors and similar devices create the physical movement that mechanisms use.","remediate":"The motor makes motion; the wheel turns it into travel."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w2-s2","title":"Legs, joints and balance","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 2 2026 lecture.pdf","pages":"8–12"},"cards":[{"title":"What legs let you do","text":["A foot makes a discrete contact: the robot can choose one place to put it, then another. A foothold is a place where a foot can safely support the robot.","This can help on rough ground or steps, but requires terrain perception, enough power and coordination of several moving joints."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Degrees of freedom, one at a time","text":["A degree of freedom, or DOF, is an independent movement available to a mechanism. A joint connects body parts and allows a particular relative movement.","The slide uses two DOF as a simple lift-and-swing minimum for moving a leg forward, and three DOF as a common practical design. A fourth ankle DOF can add flexibility. These are lecture design examples, not a claim that every possible leg must have the same joints."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"A tiny counting example","text":["A quadruped has four legs. With three actuated joints per leg,4×3=12 leg joints. A hexapod has six legs:6×3=18.","More joints allow more movement choices, but add motors, power demand and control decisions. DOF counts independent motions; it is not always identical to the count of motors."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Static and dynamic balance","text":["Static stability means the configuration can stay balanced without continually correcting a falling motion. In slow quadruped walking, three feet can stay down while the fourth steps.","Their contact points form a support triangle. The downward projection of the centre of gravity must remain inside that support region for static balance under the simplified assumptions. Dynamic stability uses continuing motion and active control; the lecture uses biped walking as its example. A biped can also stand statically in suitable poses, so do not turn the gait example into “all bipeds always fall”."],"kind":"friendly"}],"practice":{"prompt":"Draw three supporting feet as a triangle. Mark a centre of gravity projection inside, then outside.","expected":"You can explain which drawing represents the simplified stable static stance."},"quiz":[{"prompt":"A four-legged robot with three actuated joints per leg has how many such joints?","choices":["7","12","18"],"answer":1,"why":"Multiply the four legs by three joints on each leg.","remediate":"Draw four groups of three dots:3+3+3+3=12."},{"prompt":"What is a foothold?","choices":["A place where a foot can support the robot","A ROS topic","A battery percentage"],"answer":0,"why":"Legged locomotion chooses discrete contact positions on the terrain.","remediate":"Think of choosing where to place your foot on stepping stones."},{"prompt":"In the simplified static balance picture, where should the gravity projection lie?","choices":["Anywhere outside the contacts","Above the screen title","Inside the support region"],"answer":2,"why":"Inside the contact region, the supporting feet can balance the weight without tipping under those assumptions.","remediate":"Imagine a stool tipping when your weight goes beyond its supporting legs."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w2-s3","title":"Flying and swimming robots","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 2 2026 lecture.pdf","pages":"13–16"},"cards":[{"title":"Four flight concepts","text":["A quadrotor uses four powered rotors and can hover: stay approximately in place in the air. A helicopter also uses rotating lift.","A fixed-wing aircraft uses wings for lift while moving forward. A blimp floats using buoyancy in air. Flapping-wing designs imitate changing wing motion. Each has different endurance and control needs."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Match the flight to the task","text":["A rotorcraft can inspect a point while hovering, but continuous powered lift uses substantial energy. A fixed-wing aircraft can suit long surveys but cannot simply stop and hover like a quadrotor.","A blimp can use little energy to stay aloft but wind and size limit precise manoeuvres. Flapping wings introduce mechanical and control complexity. The slide’s “under 20 minutes” and “hours” are example comparisons, not guaranteed endurance for every product."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Underwater changes the problem","text":["An AUV is an autonomous underwater vehicle. An ROV is a remotely operated vehicle, controlled by a person through its operating system, often a tether.","Buoyancy is the upward force from displaced fluid. Drag resists movement through water. Pressure increases with depth. Normal GPS signals and ordinary radio links are unsuitable underwater; robots use other sensing and communication methods."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Compare three missions","text":["For parcels on a flat warehouse floor, wheels are a reasonable starting choice. For searching rubble, legs can offer useful foothold choices. For inspecting a bridge underside from air, hovering rotorcraft may help.","These are justified starting choices, not universal guarantees: access, weather, power, payload, safety and terrain can change the decision. An underwater inspection needs a vehicle designed for that medium."],"kind":"friendly"}],"practice":{"prompt":"Choose a flight concept for a long open-area survey and another for a close inspection while hovering.","expected":"You can justify fixed-wing for suitable long surveys and rotorcraft for suitable hovering tasks."},"quiz":[{"prompt":"Which design can normally hover for a detailed airborne inspection?","choices":["A conventional fixed-wing aircraft","A wheeled LIMO","A quadrotor"],"answer":2,"why":"Its rotors can provide lift while the vehicle stays approximately in place.","remediate":"Hover means hold an airborne position instead of needing continuous forward flight."},{"prompt":"What does AUV mean?","choices":["Autonomous underwater vehicle","Angular Ubuntu version","Aerial user video"],"answer":0,"why":"It identifies an underwater vehicle carrying out tasks autonomously.","remediate":"A: autonomous. U: underwater. V: vehicle."},{"prompt":"Why might a fixed-wing design suit a long aerial survey?","choices":["It can hover without energy","Continuous wing-supported forward flight can offer endurance advantages","It works without moving air"],"answer":1,"why":"Its flight concept can be more efficient for covering distance, with different manoeuvring limits.","remediate":"Long forward travel and hovering are different mission needs."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w2-s4","title":"Wheels and their movement freedoms","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 2 2026 lecture.pdf","pages":"17–20"},"cards":[{"title":"Why wheels are common","text":["Wheels can move efficiently on hard flat surfaces with relatively few actuators. Wheel choice still depends on terrain, speed and the directions the robot needs to travel.","The lecture’s statement that three wheels provide stability assumes a suitable contact triangle and centre of gravity; we examine that condition next."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Standard and castor wheels","text":["A standard wheel rolls about its axle and resists sideways sliding in the ideal rolling model. It can be fixed or steered. The lecture counts two contact/rolling freedoms for this wheel type.","A castor is a passive support wheel with an offset swivel axis: it turns to follow the travel direction. The lecture counts three DOF, including this swivel. A castor does not actively command the robot to drive sideways."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Swedish and spherical wheels","text":["A Swedish wheel has rollers around its edge. Omni and Mecanum wheels use roller geometries that allow extra movement components; the lecture shows Swedish 90° and 45° variants.","A spherical wheel is ball-shaped and can rotate in different directions. The slide counts three DOF for Swedish and spherical types. These contact freedoms do not mean all three motions have separate motors."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"A whole vehicle is more than one wheel","text":["Rollers can permit lateral movement, meaning movement to the side. A correctly arranged and controlled set of wheels can make a robot omnidirectional.","Omnidirectional means the vehicle can command movement in arbitrary planar directions. Having one loose roller wheel is not enough: wheel arrangement, actuators and controller must work together."],"kind":"friendly"}],"practice":{"prompt":"Identify the four wheel names and explain one distinguishing feature of each.","expected":"Standard rolls in its plane; castor swivels; Swedish uses rollers; spherical uses a ball."},"quiz":[{"prompt":"Which wheel has a passive offset swivel?","choices":["Castor","Fixed standard wheel only","A bookcase"],"answer":0,"why":"The castor swivel lets its rolling direction follow the vehicle’s motion.","remediate":"Picture a trolley wheel turning itself into line as you push."},{"prompt":"What feature distinguishes a Swedish wheel?","choices":["A built-in map","Small rollers around its edge","No contact with the ground"],"answer":1,"why":"The roller directions change the wheel’s sideways movement constraints.","remediate":"Look for the extra small rollers on the main wheel."},{"prompt":"Does a wheel’s three DOF mean three independently powered motors?","choices":["Always","Only if it is blue","No, some freedoms can be passive"],"answer":2,"why":"The lecture’s wheel DOF include passive contact or swivel motions.","remediate":"Independent possible movement and powered movement are different counts."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w2-s5","title":"Stability, manoeuvrability and motion constraints","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 2 2026 lecture.pdf","pages":"21–23"},"cards":[{"title":"How many supporting contacts?","text":["A self-balancing two-wheel example uses active control to remain upright. Three separated floor contacts can give static stability when the gravity projection is inside their triangle.","Four or more contacts can be useful, but on uneven ground a rigid chassis may not keep every wheel loaded. Suspension allows parts to adjust. Bigger wheels can climb larger obstacles but may require more torque; torque is turning effort."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"What movements can the robot make?","text":["Manoeuvrability describes the movements available. Car-like Ackermann steering follows curved turns. Differential drive can turn using a speed difference between its sides and can rotate on the spot.","An omnidirectional robot can command sideways travel as well as forward travel and rotation. The lecture compares the extra mechanical/control effort of these choices; it is a design trade-off, not a universal theorem that more manoeuvrability always makes a system uncontrollable."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Non-holonomic in everyday words","text":["A standard differential-drive robot cannot instantly move sideways while keeping its current heading. That no-sideways rolling constraint is called non-holonomic in this lecture.","It can still reach a place to its side by turning and travelling. “Can eventually reach” differs from “can move that way immediately”. A car parallel-parks using a sequence of permitted movements."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Holonomic at vehicle level","text":["The lecture uses holonomic for a platform that can command independent planar translation and rotation at an instant. Suitable Mecanum, omni or spherical-wheel arrangements can provide this behaviour.","The exact constraint equations come in Week 3. For now, explain the difference using an immediate sideways movement test, while remembering that actual friction and slipping affect the real result."],"kind":"friendly"}],"practice":{"prompt":"Draw one immediate allowed and one disallowed velocity direction for a standard differential-drive robot.","expected":"Forward/turning are allowed; pure sideways translation at fixed heading is constrained."},"quiz":[{"prompt":"Why can a differential-drive robot reach a point to its left without being able to move directly sideways?","choices":["It can manoeuvre through turns and forward/backward motion","It secretly has wings","The point cannot be reached"],"answer":0,"why":"The constraint limits immediate velocity directions, not every eventual destination.","remediate":"Think of parking a car using several allowed movements."},{"prompt":"When are three contacts statically stable in the lecture’s simplified model?","choices":["Every time, regardless of weight location","When the gravity projection is within their support triangle","Only while flying"],"answer":1,"why":"Support positions and weight placement jointly determine tipping tendency.","remediate":"A triangle of feet supports the weight when it acts inside that triangle."},{"prompt":"What does torque mean?","choices":["Map resolution","How bright the camera is","Turning effort"],"answer":2,"why":"Motors need sufficient turning effort to drive wheels against loads and obstacles.","remediate":"Think of the twist you apply with a spanner."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w2-s6","title":"Wheel arrangements and robot examples","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 2 2026 lecture.pdf","pages":"24–35"},"cards":[{"title":"Two, three, four and six wheel arrangements","text":["A wheel configuration means how wheels and steering joints are arranged on the body. Two driven wheels plus support castors can provide differential steering.","Four or six wheels can provide load support, traction or articulated terrain contact. The count alone does not identify whether the platform can move sideways; look at wheel types and steering arrangement."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Differential and skid-steer examples","text":["The Roomba example has two independently driven wheels and a castor. Equal side speeds give approximately straight motion; different side speeds turn the robot.","Four-wheel differential and tracked skid-steer platforms also turn using side-speed differences. Some contact patches must skid during turns. Skidding can waste energy and make wheel-based odometry less reliable: wheel motion then does not fully describe body motion."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Omnidirectional and hybrid examples","text":["The Tribolo example uses three spherical wheels so translation and rotation can be combined independently with suitable control. That flexibility costs mechanical and control complexity.","The hybrid examples combine rolling with an articulated body that can lift or step over terrain. A bogie is a linked wheel-support assembly; it can help wheels adjust to uneven ground. The SpaceCat/Mars concept is a lecture example, not this course’s LIMO configuration."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"What to carry into the demonstration","text":["Choose locomotion by terrain, energy, stability and control needs. Standard-wheel vehicles have rolling constraints; appropriately arranged omni mechanisms add motion freedoms.","The reading is Chapter 2, Locomotion, in the 2011 second edition of Introduction to Autonomous Mobile Robots. Tomorrow’s demonstration explores ROS 2 workspaces, nodes/topics and LIMO drive modes. Week 3 adds differential-drive equations and control."],"kind":"friendly"}],"practice":{"prompt":"Compare Roomba-style differential drive, skid-steer tracks and an omni platform for a tight smooth room.","expected":"You explain turning, slipping and sideways capability using mechanism constraints."},"quiz":[{"prompt":"How does a differential-drive robot turn?","choices":["Only by a steering wheel at the front","By changing the relative left/right wheel-side speeds","By renaming its package"],"answer":1,"why":"Unequal side velocities create rotation of the body.","remediate":"Imagine one side rolling faster than the other."},{"prompt":"Why can skid steering degrade odometry?","choices":["Tyre slip breaks the ideal relationship between wheel rotation and body motion","Because it has four folders","Because ROS 2 has no messages"],"answer":0,"why":"Odometry based on wheel motion can over- or underestimate movement when contacts slip.","remediate":"Turning wheels do not guarantee the same amount of ground travel."},{"prompt":"Can you decide holonomic behaviour from wheel count alone?","choices":["Yes, four wheels always means holonomic","Yes, six wheels always means holonomic","No, wheel types, arrangement and control matter"],"answer":2,"why":"Four standard wheels and four Mecanum wheels impose different constraints.","remediate":"Count wheels, then inspect how each wheel can roll and how the controller combines them."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w2-s7","title":"Demonstration: build tools and the exact workspace tree","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 2 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"1–6"},"cards":[{"title":"Messages connect small robot jobs","text":["ROS provides communication, robot-specific features and tools. Message passing means one component sends typed data that another component can understand.","An interface is an agreed contract for the data’s fields and types. A publish/subscribe system sends messages on named channels. IDL means Interface Description Language: a way to specify those contracts."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Workspace and package","text":["A workspace is the project folder from which you build a group of related packages. A package is a smaller organisational unit of code and resources.","colcon coordinates package builds. Packages can use ament_cmake for CMake-based code or ament_python for Python. CMake describes build instructions for many compiled projects. The lecturer simplifies the build overview; every Python package is not itself compiled by CMake."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Keep exactly this layout","text":["Use robotics_ws as an example workspace name, and substitute your own workspace path. Source packages go inside src; build, install and log are sibling folders created by the build process. The example packages shown here must be supplied separately for practical work.","build holds build outputs and intermediate work. install holds installed package resources and environment setup scripts. log holds build records. README.md introduces the project; wiki_images stores supporting images. Do not move package source into build or flatten the live workspace."],"kind":"friendly","code":"robotics_ws/\n├── src/\n│   ├── example_codes/\n│   ├── limo_description/\n│   ├── limo_gazebosim/\n│   ├── limo_msgs/\n│   ├── trajectory_referee/\n│   └── trajectory_skeleton/\n├── build/\n├── install/\n├── log/\n├── wiki_images/\n└── README.md"},{"title":"Build then make packages visible","text":["From the workspace root, colcon build prepares packages. source install/setup.bash updates that terminal’s search paths so ROS can find the built workspace. Source means execute the setup instructions in the current shell.","A shell is the command interpreter running inside a terminal. A new terminal needs the ROS environment and workspace overlay again. An overlay adds your packages on top of the base ROS installation. Read the sequence here before executing a later practical build."],"kind":"friendly","code":"# On the Ubuntu host\ndistrobox enter ros-humble-limo\n# Now inside the Humble container\nsource /opt/ros/humble/setup.bash\ncd \"$HOME/robotics_ws\"\nsource install/setup.bash\n# Build only when practising a build from this workspace root:\n# colcon build --symlink-install\n# source install/setup.bash"}],"practice":{"prompt":"Compare the actual workspace tree with the screenshot and name the source folder and three generated folders.","expected":"src contains source; build/install/log sit beside src. Workspace naming can differ without changing the required structure."},"quiz":[{"prompt":"Where do package source directories belong?","choices":["Inside src/","Inside log/","Inside an image file"],"answer":0,"why":"The workspace’s src directory contains source packages; generated outputs live beside it.","remediate":"src means source. Put readable project packages there."},{"prompt":"Where should you run colcon build for this workspace?","choices":["Within any package’s test folder","At the robotics_ws workspace root","Inside Gazebo’s screen"],"answer":1,"why":"The root lets colcon discover and coordinate the packages below src.","remediate":"Stand one level above src, build, install and log."},{"prompt":"A fresh terminal cannot find a built package. What first environment step matters?","choices":["Delete src","Move it into Documents","Enter Humble and source the built workspace overlay"],"answer":2,"why":"Each shell needs the paths that locate the installed packages.","remediate":"The build exists on disk, but a new shell needs to be told where it is."}],"research":[{"title":"Official Humble package tutorial","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ros2/ros2_documentation/humble/source/Tutorials/Beginner-Client-Libraries/Creating-Your-First-ROS2-Package.rst","addition":"Verified workspace/src organisation and the ament versus colcon distinction. Added Python build-type clarification.","anchor":"Week 2 demonstration 4–6"}]},{"id":"w2-s8","title":"Demonstration: packages and the supplied seven","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 2 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"7–10"},"cards":[{"title":"One package can hold several kinds of resource","text":["A package can contain nodes, reusable libraries, configuration files, datasets, launch files and message/service definitions. A launch file starts a related set of components with settings.","package.xml is a manifest: it records package identity and declared dependencies. setup.py installs a Python package and can register executable entry points. An entry point connects a command name to a Python function."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"The actual course packages","text":["limo_description stores the robot model: links, joints and geometry. limo_gazebosim supplies simulation worlds, launchers and integration. limo_msgs defines robot-specific messages.","trajectory_referee supplies reference routes and waypoint commands. trajectory_skeleton is the controller exercise. example_codes contains teaching examples. Inspect the packages in your own workspace to find the navigation and detector programs available to you."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Inspect before creating","text":["ros2 pkg list shows packages visible in the sourced environment. ros2 pkg executables PACKAGE shows registered executable commands for a package.","The lecture example creates mypkg with ament_python and dependency rclpy. rclpy is the ROS 2 Python client library: it lets Python code create ROS nodes and use communication. If practising package creation later, start in workspace/src. Do not create a duplicate package just to follow this reading.","The exact slide exercise is shown below as a one-time package-creation example. Inside the sourced Humble container, first enter workspace/src. If mypkg already exists, inspect it rather than create it again. --build-type chooses Python packaging; --dependencies records the library the package needs."],"kind":"friendly","code":"# On the Ubuntu host\ndistrobox enter ros-humble-limo\n# Now inside the Humble container\nsource /opt/ros/humble/setup.bash\ncd \"$HOME/robotics_ws\"\nsource install/setup.bash\nros2 pkg list\nros2 pkg executables example_codes\nros2 pkg executables trajectory_skeleton\n# One-time lecture exercise,only if mypkg does not already exist:\n# cd \"$HOME/robotics_ws/src\"\n# ros2 pkg create mypkg --build-type ament_python --dependencies rclpy"},{"title":"A file is not automatically a ros2 run command","text":["In this baseline, example_codes/setup.py has no registered console scripts. Its Python files are teaching source, so do not expect ros2 run example_codes FILE to work automatically.","A correctly configured exercise package can register the intended entry point, then rebuild. The assessment package does register example_opencv_detector and navigation example commands. Registration means discoverable; it does not prove the unfinished logic solves the assessment."],"kind":"danger"}],"practice":{"prompt":"Find each of the seven package names in src and explain what two of them provide.","expected":"You identify seven packages, including example_codes, and keep them within src."},"quiz":[{"prompt":"What is package.xml for?","choices":["Camera pixels","Package metadata and declared dependencies","Wheel torque measurements"],"answer":1,"why":"The manifest identifies the package and what other packages it declares it needs.","remediate":"Think of a package’s label and dependency list."},{"prompt":"Which command lists a package’s registered executables?","choices":["ros2 pkg executables example_codes","pwd only","ros2 topic echo /scan"],"answer":0,"why":"Executable registration and topics are different parts of ROS.","remediate":"Ask the package tools for executables, not the topic tools."},{"prompt":"Why may a Python file fail to appear under ros2 run?","choices":["Every Python file is forbidden","The file must be an image","Its executable entry point may not be registered/installed"],"answer":2,"why":"ROS searches installed executable entries; simply storing a file does not create one.","remediate":"The source file exists, but the run command needs installation instructions pointing to it."}],"research":[{"title":"Official Humble package tutorial","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ros2/ros2_documentation/humble/source/Tutorials/Beginner-Client-Libraries/Creating-Your-First-ROS2-Package.rst","addition":"Checked setup files and executable discovery. Added the source-file versus installed entry-point distinction.","anchor":"Week 2 demonstration 7–10"}]},{"id":"w2-s9","title":"Demonstration: nodes, topics, services and actions","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 2 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"11–19"},"cards":[{"title":"Nodes do jobs and form a graph","text":["A node is a participant that performs a focused robot job. Several nodes can run at once and exchange data. The ROS graph is the network of participants and their connections.","The lecture calls a node a ROS executable program. More precisely, one executable program can contain one or more nodes. An executable is a program that can be launched; a node is a ROS participant within it."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Three communication choices","text":["A topic carries streams of typed messages: one sender can publish repeated sensor readings to several subscribers. A publisher sends; a subscriber receives.","A service is a request and response, such as asking a node to perform a short operation. An action handles a goal that takes time, gives feedback and can be cancelled, such as navigating to a destination."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Topic name versus message type","text":["/scan is a topic name, like a channel label. sensor_msgs/msg/LaserScan is its message type, like the agreed form of each item sent on that channel.","/cmd_vel carries geometry_msgs/msg/Twist, which has linear and angular velocity fields. For a differential-drive command, linear.x is forward speed and angular.z is turning rate. Different channels may carry the same message type. Multiple publishers can exist, but conflicting driving commands are a practical problem."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Observe the active system","text":["ros2 node list finds active node names; ros2 node info NODE shows a node’s connections. ros2 topic list finds topics; type/info explain them; echo prints data; hz estimates received frequency.","With a simulator already running, inspect before commanding movement. A topic existing does not prove new messages are arriving. rqt_graph displays node/topic connections; some items may be hidden by its display filters."],"kind":"friendly","code":"# On the Ubuntu host\ndistrobox enter ros-humble-limo\n# Now inside the Humble container\nsource /opt/ros/humble/setup.bash\ncd \"$HOME/robotics_ws\"\nsource install/setup.bash\nros2 node list\nros2 topic list -t\nros2 topic type /scan\nros2 topic info /scan\nros2 topic echo /scan --once\n# Optional graph view, if the tool is installed:\n# ros2 run rqt_graph rqt_graph"}],"practice":{"prompt":"For camera stream, short state request and navigation goal, choose topic, service and action.","expected":"You associate continuous data with topic, quick request/response with service, and longer goals with action."},"quiz":[{"prompt":"A sensor sends fresh ranges repeatedly. Which communication type fits?","choices":["A topic","A text-editor window","Only an action"],"answer":0,"why":"Streaming readings suit publish/subscribe data channels.","remediate":"Repeated observations are a stream: think topic."},{"prompt":"A robot goal may take a minute and needs progress and cancellation. Which type fits?","choices":["A file extension","An action","Only a one-way log"],"answer":1,"why":"Actions represent ongoing work with feedback and result handling.","remediate":"A long task benefits from knowing progress and being able to stop it."},{"prompt":"A /scan topic exists. Which check helps establish messages are actually arriving?","choices":["Count package folders","Change the desktop wallpaper","Read a message or measure the received frequency"],"answer":2,"why":"Discovery tells you a channel exists; echo/hz observe its data.","remediate":"A named radio channel may still be silent. Listen for a message."}],"research":[{"title":"Official Humble nodes tutorial","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ros2/ros2_documentation/humble/source/Tutorials/Beginner-CLI-Tools/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst","addition":"Verified graph inspection and clarified that one executable may contain several nodes.","anchor":"Week 2 demonstration 11–12"}]},{"id":"w2-s10","title":"Demonstration: message fields, editing and source builds","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 2 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"20–27"},"cards":[{"title":"An interface describes fields","text":["A message definition lists data fields and their types. A field may be a number, text, or another message nested inside it. Nested means one structured object contains another.","PoseStamped combines a header with a pose. A header carries context such as time and coordinate-frame name. A pose includes position and orientation. A coordinate frame is a named set of axes used to interpret numbers."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Read a message before using it","text":["JointState describes joint states; JointTrajectory describes a sequence of desired joint movements; Image describes image data; Log describes logging data. Their ROS 2 identifiers include /msg/.","ros2 interface show TYPE prints the definition. Custom message source is commonly in a package’s msg folder; interface generation creates language-specific types. CMakeLists.txt configures generation for the CMake interface package."],"kind":"friendly","code":"# On the Ubuntu host\ndistrobox enter ros-humble-limo\n# Now inside the Humble container\nsource /opt/ros/humble/setup.bash\ncd \"$HOME/robotics_ws\"\nsource install/setup.bash\nros2 interface list\nros2 interface packages\nros2 interface package limo_msgs\nros2 interface show geometry_msgs/msg/Twist\nros2 interface show geometry_msgs/msg/PoseStamped"},{"title":"Edit source with context","text":["An IDE is an integrated development environment: an editor with programming aids. VS Code and PyCharm are examples. Read package.xml, setup.py and launch files to see how a program is installed and started.","In the example Distrobox setup, edit the workspace on the host at ~/distrobox/ros-humble-limo/robotics_ws. Inside the dedicated container it is $HOME/robotics_ws. Adapt host paths to your own setup; do not create a second workspace by accident."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Source installation has a sequence","text":["The slide teaches finding a compatible repository, placing its packages in workspace/src, building from the workspace root, then sourcing the overlay. Git is a tool for tracking source changes; clone downloads a repository copy.","Your current workspace already contains the course source. Do not clone another full copy into src or mix host Jazzy tools into this Humble build. The PDF extraction has stray apt text mixed into the source-install slide; it is not a valid command to copy."],"kind":"danger"}],"practice":{"prompt":"Inspect Twist and name its two major groups of fields. Then say which two fields steer differential drive.","expected":"Twist contains linear and angular vectors; forward linear.x and turning angular.z are the usual planar differential-drive commands."},"quiz":[{"prompt":"What does the “Stamped” part of PoseStamped add?","choices":["An instruction to move the robot automatically","A header with context such as time and frame","A package installation"],"answer":1,"why":"Context lets other nodes interpret where and when a pose applies.","remediate":"Coordinates need a frame, and observations need a time."},{"prompt":"Which command shows the fields of Twist?","choices":["ros2 interface show geometry_msgs/msg/Twist","ros2 pkg create Twist","ls -a only"],"answer":0,"why":"The interface tool reads the agreed structure of the message type.","remediate":"To understand message fields, ask ros2 interface show."},{"prompt":"What is the correct source-build sequence?","choices":["Build inside log, then move src","Run random PDF fragments","Put packages in src → build from root → source install/setup.bash"],"answer":2,"why":"Build discovery and runtime discovery are separate steps with specific locations.","remediate":"First source files, then build outputs, then tell the shell where installed packages live."}],"research":[]}]},{"number":3,"title":"Mobile Robot Kinematics/Geometry & Control","note":"Slide references use the physical slide position in the supplied 29-slide PowerPoint; some printed slide numbers repeat. Lecture first, then its20-page demonstration.","sections":[{"id":"w3-s1","title":"Part A · From motor turns to a position","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 3 2026_v3.pptx","pages":"1–5"},"cards":[{"title":"One bridge, two directions","text":["The robot vacuum wants to reach its dock. Its motors can only turn wheels. We need a bridge between “turn these wheels” and “be at the dock.”","Forward: wheel speeds tell us how the robot moves. Backward: the movement we want tells us the required wheel speeds."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Three words, one at a time","text":["Locomotion = the physical way of moving: wheels, legs or propellers. Kinematics = the geometry of motion, ignoring forces and mass. Odometry = adding up small measured movements to estimate where we are.","Like counting your steps: each step gives a little movement. Add the steps to estimate your location. If some steps slip, your estimate becomes wrong."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Where this sits in the loop","text":["This lecture concentrates on ACT. A controller needs a movement model, an estimate of the current pose, a goal, and a rule turning error into commands. Pose means position plus the direction the robot faces.","We will first model motion, then estimate pose, then control it. These are related jobs, but they answer different questions."],"kind":"friendly"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Which question belongs to kinematics?","choices":["How much does the robot weigh?","How does this wheel speed move the robot?","What colour is a pothole?"],"answer":1,"why":"Kinematics connects speeds and geometric movement. It deliberately leaves forces and mass out.","remediate":"Start with a toy car: changing its wheel turns changes its movement. That relation is kinematics."},{"prompt":"You add up wheel movements to estimate your location. What are you doing?","choices":["Odometry","Camera segmentation","Path search"],"answer":0,"why":"Odometry integrates, meaning adds up, the motion over time.","remediate":"Picture counting steps from your front door. The step total becomes a position estimate."},{"prompt":"What does a pose contain?","choices":["Only speed","Only x and y","Position and orientation"],"answer":2,"why":"A robot at the dock but facing sideways has the right position and the wrong pose.","remediate":"Draw a dot for the location and an arrow for its direction. Together they represent pose."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w3-s2","title":"Radians · A different way to count a turn","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 3 2026_v3.pptx","pages":"6"},"cards":[{"title":"Degrees and radians measure the same thing","text":["A full circle is 360 degrees. It is also 2π radians, about 6.283. π is about 3.14159. A radian is an angle whose arc length equals the circle radius.","Radius means distance from centre to edge. Arc means a curved piece of the edge. Angle θ = arc length s / radius r."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"The smallest worked calculation","text":["Arc length s = r × θ. Use radians. If r = 0.6 metres and θ = 0.5 radians, s = 0.6 × 0.5 = 0.30 metres. That is 30 centimetres.","Convert degrees to radians: degrees × π / 180. So 90° = π/2 ≈ 1.571 rad. Convert back: radians × 180 / π. So 0.5 rad ≈ 28.65°."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Keep the units together","text":["Python math.sin, math.cos and math.atan2 use radians. The ROS turn rate uses radians per second. Convert a display in degrees before calculating."],"kind":"danger","code":"import math\ntheta = math.radians(90)\nprint(math.cos(theta))  # approximately 0\nprint(math.sin(theta))  # 1"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"A half-turn, 180°, equals how many radians?","choices":["π","180π","π/180"],"answer":0,"why":"A whole turn is 2π rad, so half is π rad.","remediate":"Draw half a circle. It is half of 360° and half of 2π rad."},{"prompt":"r = 0.6 m and θ = 0.5 rad. What is the arc length?","choices":["1.2 m","0.30 m","17.2 m"],"answer":1,"why":"Multiply the radius by the angle in radians: 0.6 × 0.5.","remediate":"The formula is s=rθ. Put the two numbers side by side and multiply them."},{"prompt":"What should Python math.sin receive for a 90° heading?","choices":["90","360","π/2, about 1.571"],"answer":2,"why":"Python trigonometry expects radians. 90 × π/180 = π/2.","remediate":"A degree number is a display unit. Convert it to radians before using sin or cos."}],"practice":{"prompt":"Convert 45° to radians without a calculator first.","expected":"45 is one quarter of 180, so 45° = π/4 ≈ 0.785 rad."},"research":[]},{"id":"w3-s3","title":"Differential drive · Average moves, difference turns","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 3 2026_v3.pptx","pages":"7–9"},"cards":[{"title":"Two independently driven wheels","text":["Left and right wheels share an axle. A passive caster supports the body. Equal forward speeds go straight. Different speeds produce a curve. Equal opposite speeds spin on the spot.","An ordinary wheel cannot roll sideways without slipping. So this robot must turn and drive to reach a point beside it."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Read the symbols with their units","text":["r = wheel radius in metres. b = distance between wheels in metres. φ̇_l and φ̇_r are wheel spin rates in rad/s; the dot means rate of change. v_l and v_r are wheel rim speeds in m/s.","Robot speed v is in m/s. Robot turn rate ω, pronounced omega, is in rad/s. Positive ω turns anticlockwise, or left. R is distance from the turning centre to the robot centre."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"The lecture example, one calculation at a time","text":["r = 0.05 m, b = 0.20 m, left spin = 5 rad/s, right spin = 7 rad/s. Rim speeds: v_l = rφ̇_l = 0.25 m/s; v_r = 0.35 m/s.","Average: v = (v_r + v_l)/2 = (0.35 + 0.25)/2 = 0.30 m/s. Difference: ω = (v_r − v_l)/b = 0.10/0.20 = 0.50 rad/s. Radius R = v/ω = 0.60 m."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Run the bridge backwards","text":["For a desired v and ω, choose v_r = v + ωb/2 and v_l = v − ωb/2. Divide each rim speed by wheel radius to obtain motor spin rate.","If ω=0, the straight-line turning radius is infinite; do not divide by zero. If equal opposite speeds give v=0 and ω≠0, R=0: turn in place."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Both wheels roll forward at 0.2 m/s. What happens?","choices":["It spins","It moves sideways","It moves straight"],"answer":2,"why":"The difference is zero, so ω=0; the average forward speed is 0.2 m/s.","remediate":"Imagine both sides of a shopping trolley moving equally: neither side swings ahead."},{"prompt":"v_l=0.25, v_r=0.35 m/s, b=0.20 m. What is ω?","choices":["0.50 rad/s","0.30 m/s","0.02 rad/s"],"answer":0,"why":"Difference divided by separation is 0.10/0.20 = 0.50 rad/s.","remediate":"Average gives forward speed; difference divided by wheel spacing gives turn rate."},{"prompt":"Equal opposite wheel speeds produce what centre speed?","choices":["Maximum speed","Zero","Infinite"],"answer":1,"why":"One side moves forward while the other moves backward equally. Their average is zero.","remediate":"Add +0.2 and −0.2. The sum is zero, so the centre does not translate."}],"practice":{"prompt":"With v=0.30, ω=0.50 and b=0.20, reconstruct both rim speeds.","expected":"ωb/2 = 0.05. Right = 0.35 m/s; left = 0.25 m/s."},"research":[]},{"id":"w3-s4","title":"Straight odometry · Split one movement into x and y","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 3 2026_v3.pptx","pages":"10"},"cards":[{"title":"A position is two numbers","text":["x measures movement along one ground axis. y measures movement along the axis at a right angle. Heading θ measures the direction from the positive x-axis.","A straight journey of length s can be split into x and y parts. Cosine gives the x part; sine gives the y part. At heading 0°, all movement is along x."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"First leg of the lecture example","text":["Start (0,0). Speed 0.30 m/s for 2 seconds: s=vt=0.60 m. Heading 30°. x=0+0.60 cos30°≈0.520 m. y=0+0.60 sin30°=0.300 m.","In Python convert 30° to radians. The displayed degree formulas are human-readable; the numeric computation still uses radians."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Carry the old answer into the next leg","text":["Leg 2: turn to 100°, then drive at 0.30 m/s for 1.5 s. Distance =0.45 m. New x=0.520+0.45 cos100°≈0.442 m; new y=0.300+0.45 sin100°≈0.743 m.","Final pose is (0.442 m,0.743 m,100°). An ideal turn on the spot changes the heading, not the centre position. New position = old position + this movement."],"kind":"friendly"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"0.30 m/s for 2 s gives what distance?","choices":["0.15 m","0.60 m","2.30 m"],"answer":1,"why":"Distance=speed×time. Metres per second times seconds leaves metres.","remediate":"Think “30 centimetres each second.” Two seconds gives 60 centimetres."},{"prompt":"Heading 0°, travel 1 m from (0,0). End position?","choices":["(0,1)","(1,1)","(1,0)"],"answer":2,"why":"cos0=1 and sin0=0. Only x changes.","remediate":"The positive x-axis is the reference for heading zero."},{"prompt":"Why add the previous x and y in leg 2?","choices":["To continue from where leg 1 ended","To double the distance","To remove orientation"],"answer":0,"why":"Odometry tracks the whole journey; each displacement starts at the previous endpoint.","remediate":"Draw two connected arrows. The second arrow starts at the tip of the first."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w3-s5","title":"Turning odometry · A tiny curved step","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 3 2026_v3.pptx","pages":"11–13"},"cards":[{"title":"Inside wheel, outside wheel","text":["During a turn, both wheels sweep the same change in robot heading Δθ. Δ means “change in.” The outside wheel follows a larger arc, so it travels further.","Wheel travel distances are d_l and d_r, in metres. Robot centre distance d_c=(d_l+d_r)/2. Heading change Δθ=(d_r−d_l)/b, in radians."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Use the direction halfway through the turn","text":["For a small step, approximate the curved arc by a straight step pointing at θ+Δθ/2. New x=x+d_c cos(θ+Δθ/2). New y=y+d_c sin(θ+Δθ/2). New heading θ′=θ+Δθ.","This is a midpoint approximation. It is very useful for short time steps, but the arc and its chord are not exactly the same length."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"The original numerical example","text":["d_l=0.25 m; d_r=0.35 m; b=0.20 m; start(0,0,90°). Δθ=0.50 rad=28.65°. d_c=0.30 m. Halfway heading=104.32°.","x′≈−0.074 m; y′≈0.291 m; θ′≈118.65°. The lecture gives the exact arc endpoint about(−0.073,0.288), a difference of roughly 3 mm."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"A unit mistake makes a big position mistake","text":["Convert the starting heading to radians before adding Δθ. Do not add 90 degrees to 0.50 radians. Also remember that d_l is travelled distance; r is physical wheel radius."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"d_l=0.25 m and d_r=0.35 m. What is d_c?","choices":["0.10 m","0.60 m","0.30 m"],"answer":2,"why":"The centre travels the average of the wheel distances:0.60/2.","remediate":"The centre is halfway between the wheels, so average their travel."},{"prompt":"Which direction does the midpoint approximation use?","choices":["θ+Δθ/2","Only the final heading","Always zero"],"answer":0,"why":"The chord direction lies halfway between initial and final headings.","remediate":"If you start at 90° and finish120°, the halfway direction is 105°."},{"prompt":"Starting heading is in degrees, Δθ in radians. What first?","choices":["Add them immediately","Convert to a common unit","Discard Δθ"],"answer":1,"why":"Angle addition is meaningful only when both values use the same units.","remediate":"You would not add metres to centimetres without conversion. Angles need the same care."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w3-s6","title":"Odometry drift · Counting is an estimate","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 3 2026_v3.pptx","pages":"14"},"cards":[{"title":"Small errors keep being added","text":["An encoder measures wheel rotation. We multiply by an assumed radius to estimate distance. A wrong radius, wheel slip, uneven ground or coarse counts makes each estimate imperfect.","An error in direction is particularly costly: the robot then projects later travel into the wrong x and y directions."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"The lecture’s wheel mismatch","text":["Assume a 0.0500 m wheel but the real right radius is 0.0505 m:1% bigger. For an intended metre, the right side moves about 1.01 m while the left moves1.00 m.","With b=0.20 m, heading mismatch is 0.01/0.20=0.05 rad≈2.9° per metre. The lecture’s two-lap square simulation gives about 31.7 cm position error and 26.5° heading error. These are that simulation’s results, not universal guarantees."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"How to help the estimate","text":["Calibration means measuring and correcting assumed wheel radius and separation. External observations, such as LiDAR landmarks, can correct accumulated pose error.","Odometry remains useful between observations. Perception and later localisation help it stay tied to the world."],"kind":"friendly"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Does repeating wheel odometry automatically remove old errors?","choices":["No, errors can accumulate","Yes, at every turn","Yes, every metre"],"answer":0,"why":"Integration adds motion, including its errors. There is no automatic absolute reset.","remediate":"Counting footsteps on a slippery floor can keep adding mistaken steps."},{"prompt":"A0.01 m distance difference with b=0.20 m gives what angle error?","choices":["0.20 rad","0.05 rad","5 rad"],"answer":1,"why":"Δθ=distance difference/separation=0.01/0.20.","remediate":"Put the difference on top and the wheel spacing below. Both lengths cancel, leaving radians."},{"prompt":"Which observation could correct drifting odometry?","choices":["Another identical count of wheel rotations","A prettier robot icon","A measured landmark in the environment"],"answer":2,"why":"A landmark supplies an external reference instead of only repeating the same wheel estimate.","remediate":"Compare your counted steps with a signpost you recognise."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w3-s7","title":"Part B · Why a sideways command does not work","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 3 2026_v3.pptx","pages":"15–16"},"cards":[{"title":"Three things we want, two controls we have","text":["The desired pose contains x, y and θ. But the differential-drive controller commands only forward speed v and turn rate ω. It cannot independently push along x, y and rotate.","This is a multi-input multi-output problem: more than one control input and more than one state output."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Read a dotted symbol as “how fast this changes”","text":["ẋ=v cosθ. ẏ=v sinθ. θ̇=ω. These are the unicycle kinematic equations: move in the direction of the nose, and change heading at the commanded turn rate.","No sideways velocity is expressed by ẋ sinθ−ẏ cosθ=0. Substitute the first two equations and the terms cancel. A car parks sideways by a sequence of turns and drives, not an instantaneous sideways slide."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Numbers from the lecture","text":["θ=30°, v=0.30 m/s,ω=0.50 rad/s. ẋ≈0.260 m/s, ẏ=0.150 m/s. Constraint check:0.260×0.500−0.150×0.866≈0.","These equations assume ideal rolling. Forces, motor delay and slip are outside this simple kinematic model."],"kind":"friendly"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What inputs does the ideal differential controller command?","choices":["x, y,θ independently","v and ω","Only wheel colour"],"answer":1,"why":"The inputs are forward speed and rotation rate. Their combined effect changes the pose.","remediate":"Think of two remote controls: move forward/back and turn left/right."},{"prompt":"In ẋ, what does the dot mean?","choices":["Rate of change over time","Multiplication by x","A map landmark"],"answer":0,"why":"ẋ says how quickly x changes, measured in m/s.","remediate":"Distance is x; speed along that axis is x-dot."},{"prompt":"How can this robot reach a position directly beside it?","choices":["Instantly set sideways wheel velocity","Ignore its wheel constraints","Turn and translate in a feasible sequence"],"answer":2,"why":"It can reach many poses even though its instantaneous sideways speed is constrained.","remediate":"Parallel parking is a sequence of allowed movements that produces a sideways overall change."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w3-s8","title":"Open loop and closed loop · Check what happened","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 3 2026_v3.pptx","pages":"17"},"cards":[{"title":"Open loop: decide, then execute","text":["An open-loop controller chooses commands in advance. Example: drive for 2 seconds, then stop. It does not use measured error to correct this action.","Like closing your eyes and counting steps. Easy to plan, but a slip changes the result without being noticed."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Closed loop: measure, compare, correct","text":["A closed-loop controller repeatedly measures the state, compares it with the target, and adjusts the command. Sensor information is fed back into the decision.","Like walking toward a chair while looking at it. If you drift left, you can adjust right. This does not make sensors perfect; it gives the system a way to respond."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"The lecture’s examples","text":["Open-loop paths may combine straight segments, arcs and clothoids. A clothoid is a curve whose curvature changes smoothly with distance. Feedback control is the main approach for the following workshop.","A closed loop needs sensible sensing and timing. Bad or stale feedback can still produce a bad command."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Drive2 s without measuring position: which control?","choices":["Open loop","Closed loop","Bayes filter"],"answer":0,"why":"No measured result is fed back to adjust the action.","remediate":"A fixed recipe executed without checking its result is open loop."},{"prompt":"What closes the loop?","choices":["A larger motor","A screenshot","Measurements returning to the controller"],"answer":2,"why":"Feedback connects the measured outcome back to the next command.","remediate":"Draw controller→robot→sensor→controller. The last arrow closes the loop."},{"prompt":"Does feedback guarantee perfect motion?","choices":["Yes, even with broken sensors","No, sensor errors and delays still matter","Yes, if the map is blue"],"answer":1,"why":"A controller can correct only as well as its model and timely measurements allow.","remediate":"If the driver sees an old picture of the road, steering from it can still go wrong."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w3-s9","title":"Bang-bang and proportional control · How much to correct","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 3 2026_v3.pptx","pages":"18–19"},"cards":[{"title":"Start with one distance","text":["We want to stop r=0.5 m from a wall. Measured distance is d. In THIS example r means reference distance, not wheel radius. Error e=r−d.","If d=1.0 m, e=−0.5 m: too far away. Positive forward speed drives toward the wall and makes d smaller. The sign depends on this chosen geometry."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Bang-bang: full one way or full the other","text":["If e<0, command+v_max. If e>0, command−v_max. At e=0, stop. This uses only the sign of the error, so it treats a tiny error and a large error similarly.","With v_max=0.5 m/s, it still commands 0.5 m/s at d=1.0 m and d=0.6 m. Repeated full-speed corrections can overshoot and chatter: keep switching back and forth."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Proportional: a small error gets a small push","text":["Use v=−k_p e. The positive gain k_p sets the response strength. With k_p=0.4 and d=1.0 m, e=−0.5, so v=0.20 m/s. At d=0.6 m, e=−0.1, so v=0.04 m/s.","For the lecture start d=1.55 m, e=−1.05 m, v=0.42 m/s. The robot slows as it approaches the target. Speed limits clip commands that are too large."],"kind":"friendly"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"With r=0.5 m, d=1.0 m, what is e=r−d?","choices":["+0.5 m","−0.5 m","1.5 m"],"answer":1,"why":"Subtract measurement from reference:0.5−1.0. Negative means too far for this setup.","remediate":"Write the order first: r−d. Do not swap it halfway through."},{"prompt":"k_p=0.4, e=−0.5 m. What speed does v=−k_p e give?","choices":["0.20 m/s","−0.20 m/s","0.5 m/s"],"answer":0,"why":"The two negatives make a positive:−0.4×−0.5=0.2.","remediate":"The robot is too far away, so this example needs a positive toward-wall speed."},{"prompt":"Why can bang-bang chatter near a target?","choices":["It reads image pixels","It has no target","It keeps using full corrections and overshoots"],"answer":2,"why":"It uses error direction without shrinking the command with the error size.","remediate":"Imagine trying to park using only full throttle forward and full throttle backward."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w3-s10","title":"Choose a gain · Fast enough, gentle enough","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 3 2026_v3.pptx","pages":"20"},"cards":[{"title":"A sampled controller updates in little time steps","text":["Δt is the time between updates, in seconds. For the simple ideal distance model, d_next=d−vΔt. Combining e=r−d with v=−k_p e gives e_next=(1−k_pΔt)e.","This equation says each update multiplies the old error by a factor. A positive factor smaller than 1 shrinks it gently. A negative factor flips its sign."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Use the lecture’s Δt=0.2 seconds","text":["k_p=0.5 gives factor0.90: slow shrink. k_p=2.5 gives 0.50: half the error per step. k_p=9.5 gives−0.90: alternating overshoot.","In this ideal model stability needs|1−k_pΔt|<1, so0<k_p<2/Δt=10. A positive non-oscillating factor needs0<k_p<1/Δt=5; the boundary5 gives an ideal one-step response."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"A mathematical limit is not a robot setting","text":["These bounds assume the simplified sampled model and no delays. A real robot has speed limits, sensor noise and lag. Start conservatively in simulation, observe and tune.","The slide includes an embedded equation with a different sign. The lecture prose and recurrence consistently use v=−k_p e for the defined error e=r−d."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"For k_p=2.5 and Δt=0.2, what is the multiplier?","choices":["−0.5","1.5","0.5"],"answer":2,"why":"1−2.5×0.2=1−0.5=0.5.","remediate":"Multiply gain by time first, then subtract from1."},{"prompt":"What does a negative error multiplier do?","choices":["Flips the error sign on each update","Removes sensing","Means the robot has no wheels"],"answer":0,"why":"The error crosses to the other side of the target: alternating overshoot.","remediate":"A positive error times a negative number becomes negative; on the next step it flips back."},{"prompt":"Are the ideal gain limits enough to choose a physical robot setting?","choices":["Yes, no checks needed","No, delays and limits also matter","Only at midnight"],"answer":1,"why":"Stability depends on the real system, not just the simplified lecture recurrence.","remediate":"The model is a useful starting point. The actual motor and sensor timing must still be checked."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w3-s11","title":"Go to a pose · Distance, direction, final facing","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 3 2026_v3.pptx","pages":"21–22"},"cards":[{"title":"A goal is a dot and an arrow","text":["Current pose=(x, y,θ). Goal pose=(x_g, y_g,θ_g). Position errors Δx=x_g−x and Δy=y_g−y tell us how far the goal lies along each axis.","A straight x/y correction cannot command sideways motion. Describe the goal in polar coordinates instead: distance and angles."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Three small questions become three symbols","text":["ρ, pronounced rho, asks “how far?” ρ=√(Δx²+Δy²). Squaring and adding makes the right-triangle distance. α, alpha, asks “where is the goal relative to my nose?” α=atan2(Δy,Δx)−θ.","β, beta, asks “if I arrive along that line, how far from the desired facing will I be?” β=θ_g−θ−α. If goal heading θ_g=0, this simplifies to−θ−α."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"The lecture example","text":["Start(−1,−1,0°), goal(0,0,0°). Δx=1 m,Δy=1 m. ρ=√2≈1.414 m. atan2(1,1)=45°:α=45°≈0.785 rad. β=−45°≈−0.785 rad.","Use atan2(y, x), which knows which quadrant the goal is in. atan(y/x) loses that information and may divide by zero."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Wrap angles to the short turn","text":["Use atan2(sin a, cos a) to keep an angle near−π toπ. Otherwise a 350° turn may be chosen when a−10° turn reaches the same orientation. At the exact goal, atan2 of zero displacement does not define a useful goal direction."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Δx=1,Δy=1. What is ρ?","choices":["2 m","√2≈1.414 m","0 m"],"answer":1,"why":"Right-triangle distance is square root of 1²+1².","remediate":"Draw one horizontal metre and one vertical metre. The direct diagonal is longer than 1 and shorter than 2."},{"prompt":"What does α measure?","choices":["Direction to the goal relative to the current heading","Wheel radius","Map area"],"answer":0,"why":"Subtracting heading from world goal bearing makes it robot-relative.","remediate":"A goal straight in front of the nose has α=0, regardless of the world compass direction."},{"prompt":"For a general desired θ_g, which β formula is correct?","choices":["β=ρ/b","β=θ+α","β=θ_g−θ−α"],"answer":2,"why":"The final desired heading must be included;−θ−α assumes θ_g=0.","remediate":"Compare the requested final arrow with the direction of the straight arrival line."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w3-s12","title":"The pose controller · Turn these errors into motion","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 3 2026_v3.pptx","pages":"23–26"},"cards":[{"title":"Two control rules","text":["Command v=k_ρρ and ω=k_αα+k_ββ. The first makes forward speed proportional to distance. The second combines turning toward the goal with lining up the final heading.","Lecture gains are k_ρ=3, k_α=8, k_β=−1.5. For the previous example, v=3×1.414=4.24 m/s and ω=8×0.785+(−1.5)×(−0.785)=7.46 rad/s."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Why we check stability","text":["The lecture’s local linearised conditions are k_ρ>0, k_β<0, k_α−k_ρ>0. The example satisfies all three. Local means near the goal under the model assumptions, not an unconditional guarantee with obstacles.","Distance change is ρ̇=−k_ρρcosα. Angle change is α̇=k_ρsinα−k_αα−k_ββ. β̇=−k_ρsinα. A dot means rate of change. At the example start,ρ̇≈−3.00 m/s: distance is shrinking."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"A goal behind you needs an explicit strategy","text":["For a goal ahead,|α|≤π/2, the lecture example drives forwards. For a goal behind, it chooses a reverse branch, uses heading θ+π when computing the angular errors, and commands a negative forward speed.","The illustrated12-start simulation converges with those branch choices. A basic forward-only sketch does not automatically implement that reverse branch."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Turn the equation into a careful node","text":["A timer repeats the control step. Read the latest pose and goal, compute wrapped errors, clip speed, and publish Twist.linear.x=v and Twist.angular.z=ω. Use a position tolerance and a final-heading phase to stop.","The lecture numbers4.24 m/s and 7.46 rad/s are mathematical examples. They are not safe commands to copy onto a real LIMO. There is no obstacle avoidance in this simple controller."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What does v=k_ρρ do as distance ρ shrinks?","choices":["Increases the speed","Makes sideways motion","Reduces the commanded forward speed"],"answer":2,"why":"A positive constant times a smaller distance is a smaller speed.","remediate":"Like easing off the accelerator as you approach a parking spot."},{"prompt":"Which gain conditions match the lecture’s local result?","choices":["k_ρ>0, k_β<0, k_α>k_ρ","All gains must be negative","Only k_ρ=0 works"],"answer":0,"why":"Those inequalities make the local linearised error dynamics converge under the stated assumptions.","remediate":"Read each role: close the distance, align the goal heading, and turn toward the target fast enough."},{"prompt":"What is missing from a plain go-to-pose controller?","choices":["Wheel averaging","Obstacle avoidance and a complete stopping strategy","A goal coordinate"],"answer":1,"why":"A geometric target controller can otherwise drive into an obstacle or keep correcting at the goal.","remediate":"Knowing the destination is not the same as knowing a collision-free route."}],"practice":{"prompt":"Read the workshop sketch on original slide 26. Name four pieces still needed.","expected":"Pose subscriber or TF feedback, a timer, valid pose/goal checks, and position/final-heading stopping logic with speed limits."},"research":[]},{"id":"w3-s13","title":"Demonstration · Publish and subscribe in Python","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 3 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"2–6"},"cards":[{"title":"One program speaks; another listens","text":["A publisher sends messages on a named topic. A subscriber receives them and calls your callback function. A callback is a function ROS runs when the event happens.","Both ends must agree on the topic and message type. Here the type is std_msgs.msg. String and the topic is topic."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Read the publisher building blocks","text":["rclpy is the Python ROS library. Initialise it, create a node, create its publisher, fill a String message and publish. A timer can call the publishing function repeatedly.","Queue depth10 controls how many messages may wait. It is not a speed setting and does not mean ten messages per second."],"kind":"friendly","code":"import rclpy\nfrom std_msgs.msg import String\n\nrclpy.init()\nnode = rclpy.create_node(\"small_publisher\")\npub = node.create_publisher(String, \"topic\", 10)\n\ndef send():\n    msg = String()\n    msg.data = \"Hello robot\"\n    pub.publish(msg)\n\nnode.create_timer(0.5, send)\ntry:\n    rclpy.spin(node)\nexcept KeyboardInterrupt:\n    pass\nfinally:\n    node.destroy_node()\n    if rclpy.ok():\n        rclpy.shutdown()"},{"title":"Now the receiving side","text":["create_subscription takes the message type, topic name, callback and queue depth. spin keeps processing callbacks until stopped. The subscriber should keep running while the publisher speaks.","This is a complete separate subscriber example. Save it as small_subscriber.py when practising the package exercise; do not append it to the continuously running publisher."],"kind":"friendly","code":"import rclpy\nfrom std_msgs.msg import String\n\nrclpy.init()\nnode = rclpy.create_node(\"small_subscriber\")\n\ndef heard(msg):\n    node.get_logger().info(msg.data)\n\nsub = node.create_subscription(String, \"topic\", heard, 10)\ntry:\n    rclpy.spin(node)\nexcept KeyboardInterrupt:\n    pass\nfinally:\n    node.destroy_node()\n    if rclpy.ok():\n        rclpy.shutdown()"},{"title":"A typo to repair before running","text":["Demo page 4 prints rclpy.init(args=args): with a trailing colon. A normal function call has no trailing colon. The timer example here is a researched adaptation of the same publisher task."],"kind":"danger"},{"title":"Try the two programs together","kind":"friendly","text":["After the Week 2 mypkg exercise, save these files at src/mypkg/mypkg/small_publisher.py and small_subscriber.py. For this first experiment, run them directly with python3; you do not need to change existing package entry points.","Use two separate sourced Humble terminals at the workspace root. Run the publisher in one and subscriber in the other. The subscriber should print Hello robot about twice a second. Stop each with Ctrl+C. Later, a proper installed executable maps a console script to a main() function in setup.py; the small direct-run examples here deliberately keep that first experiment simple."],"code":"# Terminal A,inside the sourced Humble container at workspace root:\npython3 src/mypkg/mypkg/small_publisher.py\n# Terminal B,also sourced and at workspace root:\npython3 src/mypkg/mypkg/small_subscriber.py"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"When does the callback run?","choices":["Once a year","When a subscribed message is processed","Before the node exists"],"answer":1,"why":"ROS invokes it when incoming data is handled during spinning.","remediate":"The callback is like answering the doorbell: the arrival triggers your function."},{"prompt":"Does queue depth10 mean10 Hz?","choices":["Yes","Only for strings","No"],"answer":2,"why":"Queue capacity and timer frequency are different settings. A0.5 s timer aims for 2 calls per second.","remediate":"A mailbox can hold10 letters without receiving10 letters every second."},{"prompt":"What must publisher and subscriber agree on?","choices":["Topic and compatible message type","The same Python variable names","Their window colours"],"answer":0,"why":"The topic routes communication; the message type defines its structure.","remediate":"Two people must tune to the same channel and use the same language."}],"research":[{"title":"Official Humble Python publisher/subscriber tutorial","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ros2/ros2_documentation/humble/source/Tutorials/Beginner-Client-Libraries/Writing-A-Simple-Py-Publisher-And-Subscriber.rst","addition":"Added the timer-and-spin pattern and clarified callback processing, while repairing the slide’s trailing colon.","anchor":"Week 3 demonstration pp3–6"}]},{"id":"w3-s14","title":"Demonstration · Services and node parameters","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 3 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"7–11"},"cards":[{"title":"A service is a short request and a reply","text":["A topic is a stream. A service is like asking “please reset this counter” and receiving a reply. A .srv definition has request fields, then---, then response fields.","Service clients can call asynchronously in ROS2: they need not freeze the entire program waiting. The demonstration’s sequential diagram shows a simple request/reply flow, not a universal blocking rule."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Inspect before calling","text":["List services, find their type, and inspect that type before constructing a request. These commands inspect what is currently running."],"kind":"friendly","code":"ros2 service list\n# Replace SERVICE_NAME with a listed name:\nros2 service type SERVICE_NAME\n# Inspect the actual returned type:\nros2 interface show PACKAGE/srv/TYPE"},{"title":"A parameter is a node setting","text":["A parameter can configure a node at startup or, if that node allows it, while running. It belongs to a node and lives with that node. Example: use_sim_time tells a node to use simulator time.","get reads a value; set changes it. load reads settings from YAML, a structured text file."],"kind":"friendly","code":"ros2 param list\n# Replace NODE and PARAMETER with actual names:\nros2 param get NODE PARAMETER\nros2 param set NODE PARAMETER VALUE\nros2 param load NODE settings.yaml"},{"title":"Repair the command on demo page 11","text":["The slide repeats ros2 param get for setting a value. The correct verb is set. A parameter change can affect behaviour immediately, so understand it before applying it."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What separates a .srv request from its response?","choices":["---","A camera pixel","A wheel encoder"],"answer":0,"why":"The interface file uses three hyphens to divide the two field lists.","remediate":"Think of a form with a question area and an answer area, with a divider between them."},{"prompt":"Which command changes a parameter?","choices":["ros2 param get NODE PARAM VALUE","ros2 param set NODE PARAM VALUE","ros2 topic hz"],"answer":1,"why":"set changes a value; get only reads one.","remediate":"Remember get=look, set=change."},{"prompt":"Do all ROS2 services require freezing the whole program?","choices":["Yes","Only on Tuesday","No, clients can use asynchronous calls"],"answer":2,"why":"Asynchronous clients request a reply and let the executor handle other work.","remediate":"The request and reply are a pair, even when the program does other work while waiting."}],"research":[{"title":"Official Humble parameter tutorial","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ros2/ros2_documentation/humble/source/Tutorials/Beginner-CLI-Tools/Understanding-ROS2-Parameters/Understanding-ROS2-Parameters.rst","addition":"Verified get versus set and the node-owned parameter model; added the exact correction for demonstration page 11.","anchor":"Week 3 demonstration pp10–11"}]},{"id":"w3-s15","title":"Demonstration · Launch a collection of nodes","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 3 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"12–18"},"cards":[{"title":"One recipe starts several programs","text":["A launch file describes a system: which nodes to start, their settings, names and remappings. Launch descriptions may use Python, XML or YAML. The supplied course commonly uses .launch.py.","An argument is an input to the launch recipe. A parameter is a setting passed to a node. A remapping redirects a topic or name."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Read a minimal Python launch file","text":["generate_launch_description returns a LaunchDescription. Each Node action says which package and executable to run. This little example starts turtlesim; it is an explanatory adaptation, not the entire mimic slide."],"kind":"friendly","code":"from launch import LaunchDescription\nfrom launch_ros.actions import Node\n\ndef generate_launch_description():\n    return LaunchDescription([\n        Node(package=\"turtlesim\",\n             executable=\"turtlesim_node\",\n             name=\"small_turtle\")\n    ])"},{"title":"Why the mimic example needs namespacing","text":["Two turtlesim nodes have the same normal topic names unless namespaced or remapped. Naming a node turtle1 does not automatically put its topics under /turtlesim1.","The lecture’s page 13 listing omits namespaces while its mimic remaps refer to namespaced topics. Read it as an incomplete illustration; verify the actual graph with ros2 node list and ros2 topic list."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Use the course launch pattern","text":["ros2 launch PACKAGE FILE starts an installed launch recipe. Keep its terminal open. Ctrl+C in that terminal stops it. Launching two simulator copies accidentally can conflict."],"kind":"danger","code":"ros2 launch limo_gazebosim limo_gazebo_diff.launch.py\n# Illustrative course command; prepare the Humble terminal first.\n# Keep this launch terminal open while using a second terminal."}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What is a launch file for?","choices":["Starting/configuring a group of nodes","Compiling every mesh","Counting pixels"],"answer":0,"why":"It makes the system startup repeatable rather than typing every process separately.","remediate":"Think of a recipe that starts the camera, the robot and the visualiser together."},{"prompt":"Does merely naming a node turtle1 namespace all its topics?","choices":["Always","No","Only for images"],"answer":1,"why":"Node name and node namespace are different concepts.","remediate":"A person’s name is not their street address. The node name is not automatically its topic prefix."},{"prompt":"What should you do with a simulator launch terminal?","choices":["Close it immediately","Type into it while the server is running","Leave it running; use another terminal for inspection"],"answer":2,"why":"The foreground launch process owns the terminal until stopped.","remediate":"A running simulator is doing its job even though you have not got a shell prompt back."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w3-s16","title":"Demonstration · Twist and the actual controller skeleton","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 3 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"19–20"},"cards":[{"title":"Translate your intention into a message","text":["Twist has two3D vectors: linear velocity and angular velocity. For ordinary differential ground motion, set linear.x=v and angular.z=ω. Other components remain zero.","Topic /cmd_vel carries the command. Publishing there can move the simulated or physical robot. Learn in the course simulator."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Position-only versus full-pose control","text":["Demo page 19 uses v=k_ρρ and ω=k_αα. This aims at position but does not include the final-facing term β. The lecture’s full-pose version adds k_ββ.","Do not expect a position-only controller to automatically finish at the requested heading."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Find the existing teaching files","text":["In src/trajectory_skeleton/trajectory_skeleton/trajectory_tracking_controller.py, the node receives /waypoint_cmd, looks up odom→base_footprint, and publishes /cmd_vel. Its dummy command is 0.1 forward and 0.1 turning: it is a starter, not a completed tracker.","Reference versions live in src/example_codes/example_codes/trajectory_tracking_controller_on_off.py, trajectory_tracking_p_controller.py, and trajectory_tracking_kinematic_controller.py. Inspect before executing; the provided examples need valid pose/waypoint guards and careful stopping logic."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Write a safe control sequence on paper first","text":["Check that pose and goal exist and are recent. Compute errors. Wrap angles. Clip commands. If within position tolerance, handle final orientation or stop. On sensor failure or shutdown, publish an appropriate stop command.","The installed skeleton has a console entry point, but example_codes has none. A ros2 run command copied for an unregistered example will fail. We are inspecting code here, not executing its dummy motion."],"kind":"danger","code":"# Inside the prepared Humble container,inspect only:\nros2 pkg executables trajectory_skeleton\nros2 interface show geometry_msgs/msg/Twist\n# Starter executable exists; do not run until its control logic is ready."}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Which Twist fields control an ordinary differential base?","choices":["linear.y and linear.z","linear.x and angular.z","Only angular.x"],"answer":1,"why":"linear.x is body-forward speed; angular.z is yaw rate about the vertical axis.","remediate":"Point forward for x, and turn around an upright pencil for z rotation."},{"prompt":"What does leaving β out lose?","choices":["Final goal-orientation correction","All distance information","The publisher topic"],"answer":0,"why":"The two-term distance/bearing controller aims at the point without a separate desired arrival heading term.","remediate":"A dot is the position; the goal’s arrow still needs to be matched."},{"prompt":"Is the existing0.1/0.1 skeleton already an autonomous tracker?","choices":["Yes","Yes if renamed","No, it sends a dummy motion command"],"answer":2,"why":"A constant forward-and-turn command does not use goal error to track a target.","remediate":"A starter file gives the structure. You still have to replace the placeholder with measured-error control."}],"practice":{"prompt":"Without running the controller, trace sensor/TF data→error calculation→Twist publication in the skeleton.","expected":"You can identify where current pose is read, where goal is stored, and where the placeholder command must become a control law."},"research":[]}]},{"number":4,"title":"Perception I — sensors for mobile robots","note":"Week 4 duplicate lecture and demonstration PDFs contain identical extracted text. We teach the original once, in its page order. Historical sensor specifications remain labelled as lecture examples.","sections":[{"id":"w4-s1","title":"What perception means","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 4.pdf","pages":"2–5"},"cards":[{"title":"The robot needs clues","text":["A mobile robot is a machine that moves around. A sensor is a device that measures something. Perception means turning those measurements into useful information about the world.","Imagine walking into a room: seeing a chair is useful because you can avoid it. A robot starts with measurements, not the word “chair”."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"See → think → act","text":["See: sensors collect raw data, meaning measurements before interpretation. Think: extract information, estimate location, make a map, and plan a path. Act: send commands to motors and move.","The real world changes after movement, so the cycle repeats. Localization means estimating where the robot is. Cognition means deciding what to do. Path execution means carrying out the route."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"From numbers to meaning","text":["The page 4 diagram rises through raw data → features → objects → places or situations. A feature is a useful pattern such as a corner. An object is something such as a door. A situation gives context, such as a person standing in a doorway.","Semantics means meaning: a door is not just a rectangle; it may be somewhere you can pass through. Models are descriptions or rules that help interpret the measurements."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked example: the doorway","text":["Raw data: the camera shows coloured pixels and the laser reports distances. Features: two upright edges. Object: a doorway. Situation: somebody is blocking the opening. Decision: wait or choose another route.","Measurements can be uncertain or incomplete. A hidden person, poor lighting, or noisy range reading can change the interpretation. Treat an estimate as an estimate."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"A laser reports 0.4 m. Which step turns this into “an obstacle is close”?","choices":["Acting","Perception","Wheel rotation"],"answer":1,"why":"Perception interprets a measurement into useful information.","remediate":"Start with a thermometer: the number is the measurement; “the room is cold” is its interpretation."},{"prompt":"What happens after the robot acts?","choices":["It senses the changed world again","It never needs sensors again","All uncertainty disappears"],"answer":0,"why":"Movement changes what the sensors see, so the control cycle repeats.","remediate":"Say the cycle slowly: see, think, act, then see again."},{"prompt":"Why can a doorway estimate be wrong?","choices":["Robot data is always complete","Features cannot be measured","Part of the scene may be hidden or noisy"],"answer":2,"why":"The lecture stresses partial information and uncertainty.","remediate":"Cover half a photograph. You still have clues, but you cannot see everything."}],"research":[],"practice":{"prompt":"Explain “see, think, act” using a robot approaching a chair.","expected":"Sensor reads chair; robot chooses a safe route; motors move along it."}},{"id":"w4-s2","title":"Sensor jobs and the two classification questions","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 4.pdf","pages":"6–9"},"cards":[{"title":"What each sensor helps with","text":["Bumpers detect physical contact. GPS estimates a global location. An IMU measures motion quantities such as acceleration and turning. Wheel encoders measure wheel rotation. Laser scanners measure distances. Cameras capture images.","Odometry is a position estimate built by adding up small movements. Scene interpretation means finding useful things in the surroundings, such as pedestrians or free space."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Question one: inside or outside?","text":["Proprioceptive means measuring the robot’s own state: wheel speed or battery level. Exteroceptive means measuring the world around it: distance to a wall or a camera image.","These are two useful categories. A sensor’s job and physical design matter; classification is not a magic rule that tells you whether its reading is accurate."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Question two: send energy or receive it?","text":["An active sensor sends energy and measures the response: a laser sends light, and sonar sends sound. A passive sensor receives energy already present: an ordinary camera receives light from the scene.","The two classification questions are independent. A laser looking at a wall is exteroceptive and active. An ordinary camera is exteroceptive and passive."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Use the slide table carefully","text":["Page 9 also lists compasses, gyroscopes, inclinometers, accelerometers, radio or acoustic beacons, Doppler sensors, structured light, and time-of-flight sensors. A gyroscope measures turning; an inclinometer measures tilt; an accelerometer measures acceleration; a beacon provides a known reference. Doppler sensing uses a signal change associated with motion.","The table calls optical encoders active because their own light source is part of the measurement mechanism. GPS classification can depend on whether you mean the complete transmitting system or only the receiving device. First state what device and definition you mean."],"kind":"readmore"},{"title":"Worked example: choose a clue","text":["A robot must know whether its wheel turned: choose an encoder. It must know whether a wall is near: choose a range sensor. It must read a coloured sign: choose a camera.","Environment affects the result. Darkness harms a passive camera; wheel slip harms odometry; a missing reflection harms a range reading. No single sensor promises complete understanding."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Which measures an internal property?","choices":["A camera seeing a pedestrian","A laser seeing a wall","An encoder measuring wheel rotation"],"answer":2,"why":"Wheel rotation belongs to the robot itself.","remediate":"Internal means about the robot; external means about its surroundings."},{"prompt":"Which is an active external sensor?","choices":["A laser measuring a wall","A passive camera","A battery-level measurement"],"answer":0,"why":"It sends light outward and measures the returning signal.","remediate":"Active asks whether it sends measuring energy. External asks what it measures."},{"prompt":"A wheel spins on a slippery floor. What is the likely problem?","choices":["The camera must turn off","Encoder-based travel can disagree with actual travel","GPS becomes a wheel sensor"],"answer":1,"why":"The wheel can rotate without carrying the robot the expected distance.","remediate":"Imagine pedalling a bicycle with its rear wheel lifted off the ground."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w4-s3","title":"Range sensors and ultrasound","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 4.pdf","pages":"10–12"},"cards":[{"title":"Distance is a length","text":["A range sensor measures how far away something is. Robots use distances to avoid obstacles, estimate location, and describe their environment.","The slides name three methods: measure travel time; triangulate using angles and known geometry; or infer distance from reflected signal strength. These methods have different limitations."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"An ultrasonic echo","text":["Ultrasonic means sound above normal human hearing. The emitter sends a sound pulse. The detector receives its reflection, called an echo. Time of flight is the time the signal takes to travel.","The lecture gives typical frequencies of 40–180 kHz. A kilohertz is 1,000 cycles per second. The beam spreads in a cone, often around 20–40 degrees wide in the examples."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked example: divide by two","text":["For an echo, distance d = v × t / 2. Here v is signal speed and t is the round-trip time. Divide by two because the signal travels to the wall and back.","Use the lecture approximation v = 330 m/s. An echo arrives after 0.006 s. Total sound travel = 330 × 0.006 = 1.98 m. Distance to the wall = 1.98 / 2 = 0.99 m. Actual sound speed depends on conditions."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Where sonar works and struggles","text":["Sonar can work without visible light and is used for short-distance parking assistance. The lecture also contrasts vision with sound in underwater settings; underwater sonar requires suitable hardware.","Soft materials may absorb sound. Angled smooth surfaces can reflect sound away. Crosstalk means one sensor hears another’s pulse. A broad cone makes exact direction unclear, and sound travel limits update speed."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Why divide an echo travel distance by two?","choices":["The signal made an outward and return journey","The sensor has two wheels","Sound has no speed"],"answer":0,"why":"Time of flight for an echo includes both halves of the path.","remediate":"Draw sensor → wall → sensor. Only one arrow-length is the wall distance."},{"prompt":"Using 330 m/s, a 0.002 s round trip means what distance?","choices":["0.66 m","0.33 m","330 m"],"answer":1,"why":"330 × 0.002 = 0.66 m total, then divide by two.","remediate":"Multiply speed by time first. Then halve the answer."},{"prompt":"What does a wide sound cone make harder?","choices":["Measuring whether any echo exists","Working in darkness","Pinpointing the object’s angle"],"answer":2,"why":"An echo can originate from several directions inside the cone.","remediate":"A narrow torch spot shows a direction more clearly than a wide floodlight."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w4-s4","title":"Laser scans, endpoints, 3D scanners and scan matching","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 4.pdf","pages":"13–17"},"cards":[{"title":"Laser distance measurements","text":["A laser rangefinder sends a narrow light beam and measures its return. The slide describes a time-of-flight design with transmit and receive beams sharing an axis. A scanning mechanism sweeps the beam to collect many distances.","A 2D scan describes one plane; a 3D scanner samples multiple heights too. Precision means how closely repeated measurements agree. Some scanners are certified for safety functions; this claim does not apply to every laser."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"One array, many directions","text":["A scan is a list of ranges, in metres, together with angles. Index i means the position of one reading in the list; the first index is 0. Its angle is θᵢ = angle_min + i × angle_increment.","Convert a valid reading rᵢ to a point: xᵢ = rᵢ cos θᵢ; yᵢ = rᵢ sin θᵢ. Cosine supplies the forward component; sine supplies the sideways component. ROS scan angles use radians: π radians equals 180 degrees."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked example: three rays","text":["Assume three rays at −90°, 0°, and +90° with ranges [1, 2, 1]. At −90°, the endpoint is (0, −1) m. At 0°, it is (2, 0) m. At +90°, it is (0, +1) m.","The lecture assumes 180° field of view in this example. Your actual simulated laser configuration uses 360 samples from −2 to +2 radians, range 0.12–8 m, and update rate 8 Hz. 360 samples does not automatically mean a 360° view."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Typical scans and scan matching","text":["Page 15 shows many rays forming outlines of walls and obstacles. Scan matching compares two scans and finds a movement that makes their shared structure align. This helps localization and mapping.","Page 16 gives a historical Velodyne HDL-64E example: 64 laser emitters, up to 15 Hz turns, 360° horizontal and 26.8° vertical field of view, approximately 0.09°/0.4° angular resolution, over 1.3 million points/s, and the lecture’s range/accuracy/price figures. These illustrate a device class; they are not the LIMO sensor specifications or current shopping advice."],"kind":"readmore"},{"title":"Discard invalid measurements","text":["A missing echo is not proof of empty space. Reject non-finite values such as NaN and infinity, and readings outside the message’s valid range. NaN means “not a number”.","Use the message’s angle and frame fields. Do not assume every scan starts at −90°, every range is valid, or every sensor is located at the robot centre."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"A valid reading is 3 m at angle 0. What is its endpoint?","choices":["(0, 3) m","(3, 0) m","(3, 3) m"],"answer":1,"why":"At zero angle the ray points along positive x.","remediate":"Zero angle means forward: all of the distance goes into x."},{"prompt":"Your scan contains infinity. What should nearest-point logic do?","choices":["Treat it as a nearby wall","Replace every range with zero","Exclude it from valid candidates"],"answer":2,"why":"A non-finite number cannot describe a finite obstacle point.","remediate":"Before comparing distances, keep only usable numbers inside the sensor limits."},{"prompt":"What does scan matching try to find?","choices":["The movement that aligns common structures","The name of every pedestrian","The wheel colour"],"answer":0,"why":"The same wall looks shifted after the sensor moves; matching estimates that shift.","remediate":"Think of sliding two transparent drawings until the walls overlap."}],"research":[{"title":"ROS 2 Humble LaserScan message","url":"https://github.com/ros2/common_interfaces/blob/humble/sensor_msgs/msg/LaserScan.msg","addition":"Verified metres, radians, first-ray timestamp, angle fields and valid-range filtering so the endpoint example maps correctly to ROS data.","anchor":"Week 4 lecture p14; demo p24"}]},{"id":"w4-s5","title":"Cameras and wheel encoders","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 4.pdf","pages":"18–21"},"cards":[{"title":"A camera records a grid","text":["A pixel is one tiny sample of an image. Rows run down the picture; columns run across it. A camera can provide texture and appearance information for identification and tracking.","The lecture example has 192 rows × 256 columns = 49,152 pixels. A monocular camera uses one view. A stereo pair uses two views whose geometric difference can help estimate depth."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"An encoder counts movement","text":["An encoder converts shaft position or movement into an electrical signal. A wheel encoder can count repeated marks or slots as a wheel turns. Rotation counts can estimate wheel speed and distance.","Adding wheel movements over time is odometry. Counts accumulate, and wheel slip or imperfect dimensions can make the position estimate drift away from truth."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked example: wheel travel","text":["One wheel revolution travels a circumference C = 2πr. Here r is wheel radius; π is approximately 3.1416. Assume no slip, radius 0.05 m, and 100 counts per revolution.","One turn travels 2 × π × 0.05 ≈ 0.314 m. Twenty-five counts are 25/100 = one quarter-turn. Travel ≈ 0.314 × 0.25 = 0.0785 m, or 7.85 cm. Counts and pulses depend on the decoder setting."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Direction and the reference mark","text":["A single repeated signal can count movement but does not by itself distinguish forward from reverse. Quadrature uses two signals offset by a quarter-cycle; their order tells direction. Counting both rising and falling edges of both channels gives four count events per cycle.","The slide says “four sensors”; ordinary quadrature terminology describes two channels and four decoded edges, so do not equate four-times counting with a requirement for four sensors. A separate reference pulse marks one location per revolution. The lecture gives 64–2048 increments/revolution as typical examples."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"How many pixels are in 192 rows by 256 columns?","choices":["448","49,152","192"],"answer":1,"why":"Multiply the row count by the column count.","remediate":"A grid with 2 rows and 3 columns has 6 cells; scale the same idea."},{"prompt":"A quarter-turn moves what fraction of the circumference?","choices":["One quarter","Two circumferences","None"],"answer":0,"why":"Without slip, wheel travel follows the fraction of a full turn.","remediate":"Imagine rolling a wheel along a straight line and stopping after one quarter-turn."},{"prompt":"Why can odometry drift?","choices":["Wheel counts are colours","Counts make GPS perfect","Wheel slip and small errors accumulate"],"answer":2,"why":"Odometry adds estimated movements, so each error contributes to later position error.","remediate":"If each estimated step is a little too long, the total becomes increasingly too long."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w4-s6","title":"IMU and GPS: motion clues and global location","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 4.pdf","pages":"22–29"},"cards":[{"title":"The IMU measures motion quantities","text":["IMU means Inertial Measurement Unit. Its accelerometers measure acceleration-related signals and gyroscopes measure angular velocity, meaning rate of turning. Software can use these to estimate motion.","Roll is tilt around the forward axis, pitch around the sideways axis, and yaw around the upright axis. Position x, y, z describes location; orientation describes which way the robot points."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked example: acceleration","text":["In a simplified straight-line example, acceleration a = 0.2 m/s² for 3 s changes velocity by a × t = 0.6 m/s. With starting velocity zero, travel under constant acceleration is ½at² = 0.5 × 0.2 × 9 = 0.9 m.","Real inertial estimation must account for gravity, sensor orientation, starting velocity and measurement bias. Bias is a systematic offset. Repeated integration can amplify error; an IMU is not a direct global-position sensor."],"kind":"danger"},{"title":"GPS timing and trilateration","text":["GPS uses satellite radio signals carrying time and orbital information. Orbital information, called ephemeris, helps determine where the satellites were. Trilateration means estimating a position from distances to known locations.","The receiver estimates signal travel time. Its clock is imperfect, so the usual basic solution needs four satellite measurements for four unknowns: x, y, z, and clock correction ΔT. This is a one-way signal path, unlike a sonar echo."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"A useful GPS reading needs conditions","text":["Ground stations monitor satellite positions and the system updates orbital information. Buildings and other local surroundings can block or reflect signals; interference and timing errors affect the result.","The slide’s “below one metre” accuracy is conditional, not a guarantee for every receiver. Indoors, GPS is often unsuitable. Read accuracy statements together with receiver type, correction services and surroundings."],"kind":"danger"},{"title":"The week’s original reading and workshop","text":["Page 26 points to Introduction to Autonomous Mobile Robots chapter 4.1, Elements of Robotics chapter 2, Robotics, Vision and Control chapter 1.4, and Introduction to Intelligent Robot System Design chapter 1.3. These are reading references; their full chapters were not supplied.","The Friday outline is LIMO sensor introduction, Gazebo sensor models and ROS simulations, then subscribing to sensors and processing their values. Next week follows with computer vision and image processing, exactly as page 27 states."],"kind":"readmore"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Which directly belongs to a basic IMU?","choices":["A printed street map","Satellite ephemeris","Gyroscope and accelerometer readings"],"answer":2,"why":"These sensors provide turning and acceleration-related measurements.","remediate":"Think “how am I moving?” rather than “which street am I on?”"},{"prompt":"Why does the basic GPS solution normally use four satellite measurements?","choices":["Three position coordinates plus receiver clock correction","Because the robot has four wheels","To measure four image colours"],"answer":0,"why":"Position gives three unknowns and clock offset gives a fourth.","remediate":"List the unknowns: x, y, z, time correction."},{"prompt":"Why can an IMU-only position estimate get worse over time?","choices":["Gravity cannot affect sensors","Small biases accumulate during integration","Its pixels become larger"],"answer":1,"why":"Integration adds measurements over time; persistent offsets accumulate.","remediate":"Add a small extra amount every second and watch the total grow."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w4-s7","title":"Demonstration: Gazebo, its interface and ROS sensor plugins","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 4 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"2–9"},"cards":[{"title":"Simulation is a practice world","text":["ROS has communication infrastructure, robot-specific features, and tools. Communication carries messages; robot features handle things such as coordinates; tools help inspect and debug.","Gazebo Classic simulates a 3D world with physics, robots and sensors. A noise model deliberately makes simulated measurements imperfect. Gaussian noise means a bell-shaped distribution of measurement errors."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Find your way around Gazebo","text":["The scene is the main 3D area. The World tab lists models and lets you inspect properties such as pose, meaning position plus orientation. The Insert tab adds models. The toolbars provide interaction and time information.","The Building Editor helps make walls and rooms. Mouse navigation changes your viewing camera; changing your viewpoint does not move the robot. Use the original page 6 mouse diagram as the visual reference.","Page 6 mouse diagram: left-drag pans, Shift+left-drag rotates, double-click moves the view to a point, middle-drag rotates, right-drag vertically zooms, and scroll zooms. These control the view, not the robot’s motors."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Plugins connect simulation to ROS","text":["A plugin is extra software attached to the simulator. gazebo_ros_pkgs provides the ROS 2 integration. gazebo_dev supplies build configuration; gazebo_msgs supplies interfaces; gazebo_ros supplies helpers; gazebo_plugins supplies plugins such as camera and differential-drive interfaces.","A simulated camera plugin publishes image messages. A differential-drive plugin reads movement commands and reports motion. The simulator and ROS are separate systems linked by these integrations."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Your actual course simulation","text":["These are instructions to run later in the course container; they were not executed during content research. On the host, enter the container; inside it, source ROS and the existing workspace before launching the course differential-drive simulation.","The launch file defaults to simulation_table_with_obstacles.world. Sensor definitions are in src/limo_description/urdf/limo_gazebo.xacro; differential-drive definitions are in src/limo_description/urdf/limo_diff/limo_diff.gazebo."],"kind":"friendly","code":"distrobox enter ros-humble-limo\n# Now inside the container:\nsource /opt/ros/humble/setup.bash\ncd \"$HOME/robotics_ws\"\nsource install/setup.bash\nros2 launch limo_gazebosim limo_gazebo_diff.launch.py"},{"title":"Version note","text":["This course uses Gazebo Classic and its plugins. Gazebo Classic reached end of life in January 2025. Keep the course environment for these lessons, and recognise that instructions for newer Gazebo versions can use different commands and plugins."],"kind":"readmore"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Which tab adds a model to the simulated world?","choices":["Insert","World time","Console log"],"answer":0,"why":"Insert is the model-adding panel in the lecture interface.","remediate":"World lists what is already there; Insert adds something new."},{"prompt":"What joins a simulated camera to ROS messages?","choices":["A wheel encoder","A camera plugin","A webpage font"],"answer":1,"why":"The plugin publishes simulated measurements through ROS interfaces.","remediate":"A bridge takes data from one system and presents it to the other."},{"prompt":"What does a sensor noise model do?","choices":["Guarantees perfect measurements","Deletes robot packages","Introduces measurement variation"],"answer":2,"why":"It helps algorithms practise with imperfect readings.","remediate":"Real measurements wobble; simulated noise imitates some of that wobble."}],"research":[{"title":"Gazebo Classic project page","url":"https://classic.gazebosim.org/","addition":"Checked the January 2025 end-of-life notice and different-plugin warning. This explains why newer tutorials may not match the course commands.","anchor":"Week 4 demo pp3–8"}]},{"id":"w4-s8","title":"Demonstration: coordinate frames and the TF tree","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 4 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"10–16"},"cards":[{"title":"Every coordinate needs a reference","text":["A frame is a set of axes attached to a reference, such as the robot base, a camera or the world. “One metre ahead” means nothing until you say ahead of what.","TF keeps track of relationships between frames over time. In ROS 2 the library is tf2. A transform describes how to translate and rotate coordinates from one frame to another."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"The body axes","text":["For ordinary robot body frames: x is forward, y is left, z is up. These form a right-handed coordinate system. Lengths use metres and angles use radians.","A camera optical frame has another convention: z forward, x right, y down. Always inspect the frame named by the message instead of treating every camera frame as a body frame."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"A tree of relationships","text":["A TF tree links frames through parent–child relationships. A child has one parent in that tree. Dynamic transforms change over time, for example odom to base_link as the robot moves. Static transforms describe fixed mounting relationships.","The topics /tf and /tf_static carry TFMessage messages containing transforms. A TransformStamped includes time, parent reference, child frame, translation, and rotation. A quaternion is a four-number rotation representation.","Inspect the exact type with ros2 interface show tf2_msgs/msg/TFMessage. header.frame_id names the reference frame; child_frame_id names the child. The translation locates the child origin in the reference frame, and the rotation describes the child’s orientation there. Some page 16 arrows describe the direction loosely; rely on the fields and conversion convention."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked example: a sensor offset","text":["Assume the laser is mounted 0.2 m forward of the robot base and faces the same way. A point is (1, 0) m in the laser frame. In the base frame, it is (1.2, 0) m because the mounting offset must be added.","If the sensor also turns, rotation must be applied before adding translation. In general p_target = R × p_source + t, where R rotates the point and t shifts it. Changing frame changes the numbers describing the same physical point."],"kind":"friendly"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"For a normal robot body frame, positive y points where?","choices":["Right","Down","Left"],"answer":2,"why":"The lecture convention uses x forward, y left and z up.","remediate":"Face forward as the robot: your left side is positive y."},{"prompt":"A laser is 0.2 m forward of the base and sees a point 1 m forward. Where is it relative to the base?","choices":["1.2 m forward","0.2 m forward","1 m behind"],"answer":0,"why":"The mounting offset and measured forward distance add when axes align.","remediate":"Base → sensor is 0.2 m; sensor → point is 1 m."},{"prompt":"Why does a coordinate value need a frame name?","choices":["Frame names are image colours","Different reference axes give different numbers for the same point","Coordinates never change"],"answer":1,"why":"A number describes location relative to a chosen origin and axes.","remediate":"A chair can be beside your desk and behind the door at the same time."}],"research":[{"title":"ROS REP 103 coordinate conventions","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ros-infrastructure/rep/master/rep-0103.rst","addition":"Verified SI units, body-axis directions, and the camera optical-frame exception to prevent mixing camera and robot coordinates.","anchor":"Week 4 demo p11"}]},{"id":"w4-s9","title":"Demonstration: inspect TF and look up a transform","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 4 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"17–23"},"cards":[{"title":"Look before calculating","text":["rqt_tf_tree displays the frame relationships. RViz displays robot data in 3D and has a TF display. tf2_echo prints a transform between frames. These are inspection tools.","Use frame IDs without leading slashes in ROS 2. The slide’s /odom /base_link command is better written with odom and base_link. A topic such as /tf can still have a slash."],"kind":"friendly","code":"ros2 run rqt_tf_tree rqt_tf_tree\nros2 run tf2_ros tf2_echo odom base_link\nros2 topic echo /tf_static --once"},{"title":"Read the printed result","text":["Translation [0.463, 0.002, 0.010] means offsets along three axes, in metres. The slide quaternion [0, 0, 0.642, 0.766] is a rotation representation, not four distances.","RPY means roll, pitch, yaw. The slide’s yaw is about 1.395 radians, or about 80°. Radians × 180/π converts to degrees. The transform timestamp states when the relationship applies."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"The listener and buffer","text":["A buffer stores known transforms. A TransformListener fills that buffer from TF messages. lookup_transform(target, source, time) asks for the transform that converts source coordinates into target coordinates.","A time argument of rclpy.time. Time() means request the latest available transform. To connect a measurement to its acquisition time, request that measurement’s timestamp instead, with appropriate transform availability handling."],"kind":"friendly","code":"from tf2_ros import Buffer, TransformListener\nself.tf_buffer = Buffer()\nself.tf_listener = TransformListener(self.tf_buffer, self)\ntransform = self.tf_buffer.lookup_transform(\n    'odom', 'base_link', rclpy.time.Time())\nrobot_x = transform.transform.translation.x\nrobot_y = transform.transform.translation.y"},{"title":"Direction is a real calculation","text":["To transform a camera-frame point into base_link, target must be base_link and source must be the point’s camera frame. Swapping arguments requests the inverse relationship.","Your src/example_codes/example_codes/tf_listener.py looks up depth_camera_link ← base_link but then applies it to a camera-frame pose. That direction is inconsistent with the intended “camera to base” comment. Treat this as a teaching correction, not a robot-workspace change."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"For a point in laser_link that you want in odom, what lookup order is correct?","choices":["lookup_transform('laser_link','odom', time)","lookup_transform('odom','laser_link', time)","Either order gives identical numbers"],"answer":1,"why":"The first argument is the destination frame and the second is the input frame.","remediate":"Say “into odom, from laser_link”. Destination first."},{"prompt":"What does an empty rclpy.time. Time() request in this lookup?","choices":["A random timestamp","Exactly five seconds ago","The latest available transform"],"answer":2,"why":"The zero-time request has a special “latest” meaning.","remediate":"The buffer can answer about past times; a zero request asks for its latest answer."},{"prompt":"What unit applies to TF translation?","choices":["Metres","Pixels","Degrees"],"answer":0,"why":"Translation is spatial length; rotation is represented separately.","remediate":"A shift is a distance. A turn is an angle."}],"research":[{"title":"ROS 2 Humble tf2 Buffer source","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ros2/geometry2/humble/tf2_ros_py/tf2_ros/buffer.py","addition":"Confirmed target/source order and zero-time latest lookup; used this to identify the supplied listener’s inverted intended transform.","anchor":"Week 4 demo pp22–23"}]},{"id":"w4-s10","title":"Demonstration: nearest obstacle and an RViz marker","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 4 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"24–25"},"cards":[{"title":"What the exercise builds","text":["The exercise subscribes to /scan, finds a nearest obstacle point in the laser frame, converts it into odom, and publishes a marker on /closest_obstacle_marker. A marker is a drawing instruction RViz can display.","Your starter is src/example_codes/example_codes/closest_lidar_point.py. Its zeros are placeholders for your work, so running it unchanged does not calculate a nearest point. The answer file is closest_lidar_point_answer.py.","Page 25 also shows broadcasting a detected_obstacle frame under base_footprint. A broadcaster sends the relationship; a listener receives it. TF composes odom←robot with robot←object to obtain odom←object. In a simple aligned example, robot at x=2 m in odom plus object at x=1 m in robot gives object x=3 m in odom. With rotation, compose full transforms rather than simply adding coordinates."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked example: keep only real candidates","text":["For ranges [infinity, 0.7, 0.2, NaN] with valid limits 0.12–8 m, keep 0.7 and 0.2. The nearest valid reading is 0.2 at original index 2. Preserve the original index so its angle is correct.","If angle_min = −1 rad and angle_increment = 0.5 rad, θ₂ = −1 + 2 × 0.5 = 0. The nearest point is (0.2, 0) m in the scan frame. If no valid candidates remain, skip the marker instead of inventing a point."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Minimum core calculation","text":["This is a calculation pattern for the starter’s scan callback. It filters first, calculates angle from the original index, and converts polar distance/angle into x and y. The surrounding node and TF setup remain necessary."],"kind":"friendly","code":"valid = [(i, r) for i, r in enumerate(msg.ranges)\n         if math.isfinite(r) and msg.range_min <= r <= msg.range_max]\nif not valid:\n    return\ni, closest_range = min(valid, key=lambda pair: pair[1])\nclosest_angle = msg.angle_min + i * msg.angle_increment\nx = closest_range * math.cos(closest_angle)\ny = closest_range * math.sin(closest_angle)\nz = 0.0"},{"title":"Transform first, label afterward","text":["Use the scan message’s frame_id as the source, and only publish an odom marker after a successful conversion to odom. If lookup fails, return from this callback. Otherwise the marker would contain laser-frame numbers falsely labelled as odom.","The supplied answer uses min(msg.ranges) without filtering and continues after failed TF lookup. Its declared output_frame/min_range/max_range parameters are not actually applied. These are concrete code-review findings, not assumptions about a running robot.","The page 25 C++ diagram is conceptual: a usable rotation needs a valid quaternion, such as w=1 for no rotation. Do not copy a partial transform message with an all-zero quaternion as a complete working broadcaster."],"kind":"danger"},{"title":"Inspect the result later","text":["Inside the sourced course container, the source-only example can be run with Python. Its setup.py has no executable registrations, so do not invent a ros2 run example_codes closest_lidar_point command.","The answer file is for study; understand and repair the invalid-data/TF issues before relying on the marker. In RViz add a Marker display, choose /closest_obstacle_marker, and choose odom as the fixed reference frame."],"kind":"friendly","code":"python3 src/example_codes/example_codes/closest_lidar_point.py\n# In a separate sourced terminal:\nros2 topic info /scan --verbose\nros2 topic echo /closest_obstacle_marker --once"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"With [infinity, 0.7, 0.2, NaN], which original index belongs to the nearest valid range?","choices":["0","1","2"],"answer":2,"why":"Filtering must retain index 2 because that index determines the beam angle.","remediate":"Cross out invalid readings without renumbering the remaining ones."},{"prompt":"A TF lookup fails. What should this callback do before publishing an odom marker?","choices":["Skip this marker until conversion succeeds","Label the unchanged point odom anyway","Set its range to zero"],"answer":0,"why":"The coordinate label must describe the coordinates actually calculated.","remediate":"A label cannot perform a coordinate conversion."},{"prompt":"How do you run this unregistered source-only Python starter?","choices":["ros2 run example_codes nonexistent_executable","python3 with its actual source file path","By renaming the workspace"],"answer":1,"why":"The setup file contains no console-script entry for it.","remediate":"A source file can be given directly to Python; ROS executable registration is a separate step."}],"research":[]}]},{"number":5,"title":"Perception II — image processing and computer vision","note":"Lecture first, then demonstration, in the original page order. Research clarifies a few printed mistakes; it does not add a different course.","sections":[{"id":"w5-s1","title":"Vision starts with numbers in a grid","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 5.pdf","pages":"2–12"},"cards":[{"title":"What computer vision does","text":["Computer vision automatically extracts useful information from images or videos. Useful means useful for the task: locate the robot, recognise an object, estimate shape or pose, track motion, or classify a scene.","A camera senses reflected light. Pictures contain many details, so software needs enough processing power. Cameras can be compact and relatively inexpensive, but an image does not explain itself."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Grayscale: one value per pixel","text":["Grayscale uses a brightness value at each pixel. In the lecture’s unsigned 8-bit example, values run from 0 to 255. Unsigned means no negative numbers; 8 bits provide 256 possible values.","An array is an organised collection of numbers. Shape (851, 1280) means 851 rows and 1280 columns, so there are 851 × 1280 = 1,089,280 grayscale values. A black pixel is 0; white is 255."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Colour: three values per pixel","text":["Shape (426, 640, 3) means 426 rows, 640 columns and three colour channels. A channel is one component of colour. That image has 272,640 pixels and 817,920 channel values.","Indexing uses image[row, column]. Many drawing functions instead take (x, y) = (column, row). The slide describes RGB order with blue third; OpenCV normally works in BGR order with blue first. Know your encoding before choosing a channel."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Applications and ambiguity","text":["The lecture names reconstruction, recognition, motion capture, augmented reality, games, tele-operation, navigation, automotive systems and medical imaging. Reconstruction means estimating shape; augmented reality overlays information; tele-operation means remote control.","Viewpoint, lighting and within-class variation all change appearance. Two different 3D scenes can produce similar 2D images. An image alone often cannot tell whether something is small and near or large and far away."],"kind":"danger"},{"title":"Worked example: useful information","text":["For a self-driving task, useful detections include obstacles, pedestrians, traffic signs or lights, lanes, and drivable free space. A label without a position may not be enough to act.","The lecture’s brain-size and “AI-complete” remarks motivate the difficulty of perception; they are not facts you need to memorise to process a camera frame. Start with the grid and the task."],"kind":"readmore"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What does shape (426, 640, 3) tell you?","choices":["426 objects in 640 frames","426 rows, 640 columns, 3 channels","3 metres of depth"],"answer":1,"why":"Image shape describes its stored dimensions.","remediate":"Picture a stack of three same-sized grids."},{"prompt":"In a uint8 grayscale image, what does 0 mean?","choices":["Maximum white","An unknown metre value","Black"],"answer":2,"why":"The lecture intensity scale starts with black at 0.","remediate":"Brightness increases as the number increases toward 255."},{"prompt":"Why can one photograph leave distance unclear?","choices":["Different 3D arrangements can create similar 2D pictures","Every image stores exact range","Pixels contain GPS coordinates"],"answer":0,"why":"Projection loses some spatial information.","remediate":"A tiny model close to a camera can resemble a large object far away."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w5-s2","title":"Feature extraction and Gaussian smoothing","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 5.pdf","pages":"13–15"},"cards":[{"title":"Build meaning one step at a time","text":["A feature is a useful pattern or measurement. The lecture’s pipeline is conditioning → labelling → grouping → extracting → matching. A pipeline is a sequence of operations whose output becomes the next input.","Conditioning reduces noise or irrelevant background variation. Labelling identifies structure. Grouping collects related pixels. Extracting measures properties such as area or shape. Matching compares those descriptions with known patterns."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Smoothing uses neighbours","text":["Noise is unwanted variation in the image. A smoothing filter combines nearby pixel values so random small changes have less influence. Gaussian smoothing uses a weighted average that gives nearby pixels greater influence.","Convolution means sliding a small pattern of weights, called a kernel, over the image and computing an output at each position. The slide writes Ĩ = G ⊗ I: filtered image equals kernel convolved with original image."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"The exact slide kernel","text":["The lecture gives G = (1/16) × [[1,2,1],[2,4,2],[1,2,1]]. The weights sum to 16, so dividing by 16 keeps a constant image at the same brightness.","Worked example: the nine pixels are all 0 except a centre value 160. At that centre, the weighted sum is 4 × 160 = 640. Divide by 16: new centre value 40. Some brightness is shared into neighbouring output positions."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Edges are rapid changes","text":["An edge is a place where brightness changes sharply. A derivative measures change. A large image derivative suggests a possible edge; noise can also create large changes.","Smooth before interpreting gradients. Too much smoothing can erase fine detail, so the strength of the filter is a choice you test against the task. The supplied example’s cv2.blur is a box average, while this slide explains a Gaussian-weighted average."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Why divide this kernel’s weighted sum by 16?","choices":["The weights total 16","Every picture is 16 metres wide","There must be 16 objects"],"answer":0,"why":"Normalising the total weights preserves constant brightness.","remediate":"Add the nine weights: 1+2+1+2+4+2+1+2+1 = 16."},{"prompt":"With only centre brightness 160 and centre weight 4, what is the filtered centre?","choices":["640","40","1600"],"answer":1,"why":"4 × 160 / 16 = 40.","remediate":"Multiply by the centre weight, then divide by the total weight."},{"prompt":"Why smooth a noisy image before detecting edges?","choices":["To guarantee recognition of all objects","To convert metres into pixels","To reduce false rapid changes caused by noise"],"answer":2,"why":"A gradient responds to noise as well as object boundaries.","remediate":"A scratchy brightness pattern can look like many tiny edges."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w5-s3","title":"Sobel, Canny and thin edges","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 5.pdf","pages":"16–20"},"cards":[{"title":"A line drawing from an image","text":["Edge detection tries to retain important scene contours: boundaries such as the outside of a box. A contour is a connected boundary curve. An edge image is useful evidence, but it is not automatically an object label.","Sobel filters estimate brightness changes horizontally and vertically. The resulting gradient tells you how strong the change is and its direction."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"The complete Canny sequence","text":["Canny combines several stages: smooth noise; estimate gradients; thin the edges using non-maximum suppression; and use two thresholds with connectivity to decide which edges survive.","Non-maximum suppression keeps a local strongest response along the gradient direction and removes weaker neighbours. This gives a thin contour. Here “suppression” means discarding a value that does not qualify."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked example: two thresholds","text":["Suppose the low threshold is 50 and high threshold is 100. A gradient strength 120 is strong. A strength 30 is discarded. A strength 70 is kept only if connected to a strong edge. This connected weak-edge rule is hysteresis.","For thinning, imagine strengths [3, 9, 4] along the gradient direction. Keep 9 and suppress its smaller neighbours. The goal is approximately one-pixel-wide evidence, not three thick parallel responses."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Run the image stages in order","text":["An explicit smoothing step makes the lesson clear; a simple cv2.Canny call does not substitute for understanding the stages. Edges are usually shown as white on black in OpenCV, although a diagram may choose the opposite display convention."],"kind":"friendly","code":"gray = cv2.cvtColor(bgr_image, cv2.COLOR_BGR2GRAY)\nsmooth = cv2.GaussianBlur(gray, (5, 5), 0)\nedges = cv2.Canny(smooth, 50, 100)"},{"title":"The thresholds have a job","text":["Low thresholds can keep more noise. High thresholds can lose faint true boundaries. Compare results on the actual image. Do not treat one pair of values as perfect for every scene."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What is non-maximum suppression trying to create?","choices":["Thinner edge responses","Satellite positions","More colour channels"],"answer":0,"why":"It removes nearby weaker responses along the gradient direction.","remediate":"Of nearby values [3,9,4], the middle peak is the strongest candidate."},{"prompt":"With low/high thresholds 50/100, what happens to an isolated strength 70?","choices":["It is certainly strong","It is not retained without connection to a strong edge","It becomes a GPS measurement"],"answer":1,"why":"Intermediate-strength edges need a connection to accepted strong evidence.","remediate":"There are three groups: low rejects, high accepts, middle needs support."},{"prompt":"Does an edge image by itself prove “this is a person”?","choices":["Yes, every edge names an object","Yes, if the image is black and white","No, it marks brightness boundaries"],"answer":2,"why":"Recognition needs further interpretation beyond detecting change.","remediate":"A line drawing gives shape clues; it does not assign names to them."}],"research":[{"title":"OpenCV Canny tutorial","url":"https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/d22/tutorial_py_canny.html","addition":"Completed the lecture’s abbreviated Canny explanation with two-threshold connectivity and an explicit smoothing example.","anchor":"Week 5 lecture pp17–20"}]},{"id":"w5-s4","title":"Classical detection: features, HoG, SVM, SIFT and BRIEF","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 5.pdf","pages":"21–28"},"cards":[{"title":"Where and what are different questions","text":["Detection finds where an object is; classification decides what category it belongs to. A bounding box is a rectangle around a detected region. Recognition may ask whether a particular known book appears in a new scene.","Classical methods use deliberately designed features. The lecture goals are discrimination, invariance to distractions such as scale or lighting, and efficient computation. Discrimination means separating relevant cases; invariance means keeping useful descriptions when an irrelevant change happens."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"HoG + SVM: describe then decide","text":["HoG means Histogram of Oriented Gradients. It summarises how edge directions are distributed in small image regions. A histogram is a tally of values in groups. SVM means Support Vector Machine, a classifier trained to separate feature examples.","The page 23 pipeline prepares the image, computes gradients, tallies their directions, normalises nearby regions, combines a feature description, then uses a linear SVM. A sliding window tests different image regions for an object."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"SIFT and the known-book example","text":["SIFT means Scale Invariant Feature Transform. It finds distinctive keypoints and describes nearby image structure. A keypoint is a selected image location; a descriptor is a numerical description around it. The lecture gives SIFT a 128-number descriptor.","Match descriptors from the known book to descriptors in the scene. Several consistent matches support the hypothesis that the book is present. SIFT tolerates many changes in scale and rotation, but not unlimited lighting or viewpoint changes."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Other features, and one printed mistake","text":["SURF is another feature method. FAST means Features from Accelerated Segment Test and quickly detects corner-like points. BRIEF means Binary Robust Independent Elementary Features and makes compact descriptors using intensity comparisons; binary means made of zeros and ones.","The slide labels BRIEF a “detector” and says it is slower than itself. BRIEF is a descriptor, and the printed comparison is inconsistent. Do not memorise that sentence. Basic BRIEF does not promise SIFT-like rotation or scale invariance."],"kind":"danger"},{"title":"Worked example and trade-off","text":["A tiny binary descriptor can compare pairs of pixels: is patch value 20 smaller than 60? Store 1. Is 90 smaller than 30? Store 0. A sequence of these comparisons describes local appearance.","Classical features can be interpretable and efficient. Designing them for every changing scene can be difficult, and they do not automatically use large amounts of data as flexibly as learned representations. Speed depends on the actual algorithm and hardware."],"kind":"readmore"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Which question does classification answer?","choices":["How many metres did a wheel move?","What category does this belong to?","Which TF topic is static?"],"answer":1,"why":"Classification assigns a class such as person or vehicle.","remediate":"Detection asks where; classification asks what."},{"prompt":"In HoG + SVM, what does HoG mainly describe?","choices":["Satellite travel times","Wheel pulses","Distributions of image-gradient directions"],"answer":2,"why":"It converts edge-direction evidence into a numerical feature description.","remediate":"Imagine tallying how many local edges point up, sideways or diagonally."},{"prompt":"What is BRIEF?","choices":["A compact binary feature descriptor","A guaranteed full 3D map","A GPS clock correction"],"answer":0,"why":"It describes a patch using intensity-comparison bits.","remediate":"A detector chooses a place; a descriptor describes the chosen place."}],"research":[{"title":"OpenCV BRIEF tutorial","url":"https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/dc/d7d/tutorial_py_brief.html","addition":"Checked that BRIEF describes patches rather than finding keypoints and clarified the slide’s inconsistent invariance/comparison wording.","anchor":"Week 5 lecture p27"}]},{"id":"w5-s5","title":"Deep detection, YOLO and robustness","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 5.pdf","pages":"29–34"},"cards":[{"title":"Features can be learned","text":["Deep learning learns patterns from training examples through a network of successive calculations. A CNN, or Convolutional Neural Network, learns image features using operations across local image regions. Training adjusts parameters; inference uses the trained model on new input.","The lecture moves from classical hand-designed features to region-based detectors and then YOLO. Stay with these named families; learning their basic purpose is enough before detailed model training."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"The region-based family","text":["R-CNN proposes roughly 2,000 candidate regions using selective search, processes region features and classifies them. Selective search is an algorithm that proposes plausible image regions.","Fast R-CNN computes shared image features and uses RoI pooling to obtain fixed-size features for proposed regions. RoI means Region of Interest; pooling reduces or summarises features. Selective search supplies proposals; the pooling layer does not generate them. Faster R-CNN learns proposals through a Region Proposal Network, or RPN."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"YOLO: boxes and classes in one pass","text":["YOLO means You Only Look Once. The original lecture diagram divides an image into a grid, predicts boxes and confidence, and predicts class information. The results become labelled bounding boxes.","A confidence score expresses model evidence; it is not a guarantee. The page 32 network diagram shows successive learned feature stages and output predictions. You do not need to memorise every layer size to understand the input-to-detection process."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked example: one image result","text":["Suppose the output contains a rectangle around a bicycle and the label “bicycle”. The rectangle answers where; the label answers what. A second box might describe a person. A missed person remains a real person even when no box is produced.","The lecture shows that training examples, translation (moving an image pattern) and rotation can change results. A model can be confidently wrong or fail on unfamiliar conditions; test examples beyond the easy training scenes."],"kind":"danger"},{"title":"Dates and language clarified","text":["The slide calls Faster R-CNN a 2016 development; its first paper submission was in 2015 and a revision appeared in 2016. YOLO’s cited conference paper is 2016, with a 2015 preprint. These date details do not change the slide order.","“Two networks” is a simple description of proposal and detection components; Faster R-CNN shares image features between them. Newer YOLO products may differ from the original lecture architecture."],"kind":"readmore"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What is the key job of Faster R-CNN’s RPN?","choices":["Measuring wheel radius","Generating object-region proposals","Changing the TF frame name"],"answer":1,"why":"The learned proposal component supplies candidate regions for detection.","remediate":"Before classifying a region, the system needs candidate places to inspect."},{"prompt":"What do a box and a class label respectively give you?","choices":["Where and what","Time and sound speed","Wheel speed and battery charge"],"answer":0,"why":"The box locates the region; the class names a category.","remediate":"Point to the rectangle, then read the word beside it."},{"prompt":"A trained model misses a rotated object. What lesson follows?","choices":["Rotation makes the physical object vanish","Training guarantees every viewpoint","Robustness must be checked on changed conditions"],"answer":2,"why":"The lecture illustrates sensitivity to transformations and unfamiliar inputs.","remediate":"Rotate a familiar photograph: appearance changes even though the object remains."}],"research":[{"title":"Fast R-CNN original paper","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1504.08083","addition":"Checked the region-based method so RoI pooling is not mistaken for proposal generation.","anchor":"Week 5 lecture p29"},{"title":"Faster R-CNN original paper","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.01497","addition":"Verified the learned proposal network, shared features and 2015 first-submission date.","anchor":"Week 5 lecture p29"},{"title":"Original YOLO paper","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.02640","addition":"Verified whole-image box/class prediction and the preprint/conference date distinction.","anchor":"Week 5 lecture pp30–32"}]},{"id":"w5-s6","title":"Extracting lines and fitting simple models","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 5.pdf","pages":"35–36"},"cards":[{"title":"A wall can look like a line","text":["A laser scan or point cloud contains many measured points. A point cloud is a collection of spatial points. Line extraction means describing suitable groups of points with straight-line models.","The lecture asks: how many lines are there; which points belong together; and what line best fits each group? Segmentation is dividing data into suitable groups. Fitting is choosing model parameters to describe a group."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Three named approaches","text":["Split-and-merge splits a group when one line fits it poorly, then merges compatible pieces. RANSAC tries models from random small samples and counts support. A Hough transform lets points vote for possible model parameters.","These are alternatives for extracting structure. Knowing which points support a line matters as much as calculating the line’s slope."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Regression: a line plus errors","text":["The slide’s model is Yᵢ = α + βXᵢ + εᵢ. Xᵢ and Yᵢ are the coordinates of point i. α is the line’s intercept, β is slope, and εᵢ is the vertical error or residual.","Least squares chooses α and β to minimise Σ εᵢ², the sum of squared residuals. Σ means add all of them. The slide models vertical errors as N(0,σ²): a normal, bell-shaped distribution with mean zero and variance σ²."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked example: compare fits","text":["Points (0,1), (1,3), (2,5) lie on Y = 1 + 2X. Predictions are 1, 3, 5, so residuals are 0, 0, 0 and squared-error sum is 0.","Try Y = 1 + X instead: predictions 1, 2, 3; residuals 0, 1, 2; squared-error sum 0² + 1² + 2² = 5. For these points, the first line fits better."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Other model forms on page 36","text":["The straight-line normal form is x cos α + y sin α − r = 0. Here α is the direction of the normal, meaning a perpendicular to the line, and r is its signed offset from the origin. This α has a different role from the regression intercept above.","The slide prints a circle as (x+x_c)²+(y+y_c)²−r²=0. With that written sign, the centre is (−x_c,−y_c). If x_c and y_c mean the actual centre coordinates, the usual equation is (x−x_c)²+(y−y_c)²=r². Keep sign meanings explicit; do not reuse r as a scan range without checking the model."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What does segmentation decide in line extraction?","choices":["The file extension","Which points belong to which structure","The colour of the terminal"],"answer":1,"why":"Fitting assumes a group; segmentation decides that group.","remediate":"Sorting points into wall groups comes before fitting each wall."},{"prompt":"For points (0,1),(1,3),(2,5), which line fits exactly?","choices":["Y = 1 + 2X","Y = X","Y = 5 − X"],"answer":0,"why":"Substitute each X and you recover each measured Y.","remediate":"At X=0 you need 1; each step in X raises Y by 2."},{"prompt":"Residuals 0,1,2 give which squared-error sum?","choices":["3","2","5"],"answer":2,"why":"Square each residual and add: 0+1+4=5.","remediate":"A square means multiply the number by itself before adding."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w5-s7","title":"RANSAC: try, count, repeat","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 5.pdf","pages":"37–46"},"cards":[{"title":"Outliers can mislead a fit","text":["RANSAC means RANdom SAmple Consensus. An inlier is a point that supports the current model closely enough; an outlier does not. Robust fitting tries to tolerate outliers instead of letting a few bad points dominate.","The method applies to lines, planes, feature matching and other model-fitting tasks. A plane is a flat surface in 3D. The smallest sample needed depends on the model; a 2D line needs two distinct points."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"The exact slide sequence","text":["Pages 38–45 show: start with scattered points; randomly select two; fit a line through them; calculate each point’s error; select points that support that line; repeat with other samples; keep the strongest supported result.","Distance tolerance is the maximum error you will accept for an inlier. A hypothesis is one proposed model. Consensus is the set of data points that support it. More tries can improve the chance of drawing a useful sample, but do not guarantee perfection."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked example: two candidate lines","text":["Suppose candidate A has 3 points within a 0.05 m tolerance, while candidate B has 8. B has stronger consensus, so keep B under this simple inlier-count rule. You can refit the winning line using its inliers.","For a line ax+by+c=0, perpendicular distance from point (x, y) is |ax+by+c| / √(a²+b²). The bars mean absolute value, meaning magnitude without sign. For line y=0 and point (2,0.03), distance is 0.03 m, inside a 0.05 m tolerance."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"How many possible pairs?","text":["N distinct points have N(N−1)/2 distinct unordered pairs. For N=360, there are 360×359/2 = 64,620. Checking every pair becomes costly as the dataset grows.","RANSAC checks random subsets. If many points are inliers, a clean sample is easier to find. If few are inliers, more tries are needed. Because sampling is random, results can differ between runs."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Failure is useful information","text":["A large consensus can still describe the wrong structure if your model or tolerance is inappropriate. One fitted line does not describe a room with four walls. Inspect the result and repeat the same section until you can explain sample → model → errors → inliers → repeat."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Why does a 2D line sample normally need two distinct points?","choices":["Two colours are required","A single point can always define its slope","Two distinct points fix a line direction and location"],"answer":2,"why":"One point lies on infinitely many possible lines.","remediate":"Place one dot on paper: you can draw many lines through it. Add a second dot."},{"prompt":"Candidate A has 3 inliers and B has 8 using the same tolerance. Which has stronger consensus?","choices":["B","A","Neither can be compared"],"answer":0,"why":"More supporting points means stronger consensus under the stated count rule.","remediate":"Use one rule for both: count the points close enough to each line."},{"prompt":"How many unordered point pairs can 360 points form?","choices":["360","64,620","129,240"],"answer":1,"why":"Each point pairs with 359 others, but counting both orders doubles every pair.","remediate":"Multiply 360 by 359, then divide by 2."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w5-s8","title":"Demonstration: camera messages and calibration information","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 5 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"2–3"},"cards":[{"title":"A camera publishes messages","text":["A publisher sends data on a ROS topic; a subscriber receives it. The camera publishes Image messages containing pixel data and metadata. CameraInfo describes calibration and intrinsic parameters: the camera’s own geometric properties.","The slide lists /limo_camera/image, /limo_camera/camera_info_raw, /limo_camera/camera_points, /limo_camera/depth/camera_info, /limo_camera/depth/image_raw and compressed transport variants. A depth image stores distance-related values; a point cloud stores spatial points. These are not all ordinary colour photographs."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Inspect before choosing a topic","text":["Topics depend on the running driver or simulator, so first list and inspect them. A compressed transport topic carries encoded data and may have a different message type; an Image subscriber cannot blindly read any similarly named topic.","The lecture contains compressed, compressedDepth and theora variants. Their presence is not guaranteed in every runtime. Your camera plugin remaps its colour image to /limo_camera/image; check the active graph for actual depth names."],"kind":"friendly","code":"ros2 topic list\nros2 topic info /limo_camera/image --verbose\nros2 interface show sensor_msgs/msg/Image\nros2 interface show sensor_msgs/msg/CameraInfo"},{"title":"Read the Image message fields","text":["header records the acquisition time and camera frame. height is rows; width is columns. encoding describes channel order and type. step is bytes per complete row. data contains the bytes. is_bigendian says how bytes form multibyte values; do not manually guess the layout.","Worked example: a tightly packed 640×480 bgr8 frame has 3 bytes/pixel. step = 640×3 = 1,920 bytes/row; total data = 1,920×480 = 921,600 bytes. Real row storage may include padding, so use the actual step."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Research addition: what the intrinsics mean","text":["CameraInfo includes focal lengths f_x and f_y, and a principal point c_x, c_y, the image location of the camera’s central viewing direction. For an ideal undistorted or appropriately rectified pinhole camera: u=f_x X/Z+c_x and v=f_y Y/Z+c_y. X, Y, Z are optical-frame metres; u, v are pixel coordinates.","Worked example: f_x=f_y=500 pixels, c_x=320, c_y=240, and camera point (X, Y, Z)=(0.2,0,2) m. u=500×0.2/2+320=370, v=500×0/2+240=240. With known Z=2 m, inverse X=(u−c_x)Z/f_x recovers 0.2 m. A pixel alone gives a viewing direction, not distance. This optional clarification expands the slide’s word “intrinsics”; raw distorted images need the appropriate calibration model."],"kind":"readmore"},{"title":"Choose the real runtime topic","text":["Source inspection confirms the colour-image remapping to /limo_camera/image. It does not prove every listed depth or compressed topic is present in the running environment. Inspect topic names, types, encoding and matching camera calibration before processing data.","The source object_localisation.py gives a later colour/depth projection example. It assumes matching geometry and needs readiness, bounds, finite-depth and TF checks. This lesson explains messages first; it does not require running that advanced script."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"In Image, what does step describe?","choices":["Number of detected objects","Complete row length in bytes","Distance to a wall"],"answer":1,"why":"The byte layout may have row padding, so step describes actual storage.","remediate":"One row contains pixel bytes; step says how many bytes to advance to the next row."},{"prompt":"A 640-wide tightly packed bgr8 row needs how many bytes?","choices":["640","480","1,920"],"answer":2,"why":"Each pixel has three 8-bit, one-byte components, so 640×3=1,920.","remediate":"Count three byte-sized colour values for each pixel."},{"prompt":"What does a single pixel coordinate by itself usually tell you?","choices":["A viewing direction, with depth still unknown","Exact global position","The number of wheel turns"],"answer":0,"why":"Many points at different distances can lie on the same viewing ray.","remediate":"Point down a straight road: several locations share the same direction."}],"research":[{"title":"ROS 2 Humble Image message","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ros2/common_interfaces/humble/sensor_msgs/msg/Image.msg","addition":"Verified acquisition header, image dimensions, encoding and full-row byte step so the slide’s message screenshot is understandable.","anchor":"Week 5 demo p3"},{"title":"ROS 2 Humble CameraInfo message","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ros2/common_interfaces/humble/sensor_msgs/msg/CameraInfo.msg","addition":"Added an optional numerical meaning of intrinsics and pixel projection; this shows why Image alone does not provide 3D location.","anchor":"Week 5 demo p3"}]},{"id":"w5-s9","title":"Demonstration: choose the right rqt tool","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 5 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"4–9"},"cards":[{"title":"Choose the tool for the job","text":["rqt is a Qt-based graphical toolbox. Qt is a software framework for graphical interfaces. rqt_image_view shows image data; rqt_plot graphs numeric data over time; rqt_graph shows node/topic connections.","rqt_console filters ROS log messages, meaning messages written by nodes about their operation. rqt_topic shows topic/debug details such as publishers, subscribers and message values."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Commands from the demonstration","text":["Run each command in a sourced ROS container terminal when its package is available. In rqt_image_view select the actual colour image topic. In rqt_graph find camera publisher → image topic → processing subscriber."],"kind":"friendly","code":"ros2 run rqt_gui rqt_gui\nros2 run rqt_image_view rqt_image_view\nros2 run rqt_plot rqt_plot\nros2 run rqt_graph rqt_graph\nros2 run rqt_console rqt_console\nros2 run rqt_topic rqt_topic"},{"title":"Worked example: no picture","text":["If image_view is blank, first check whether /limo_camera/image exists and has a publisher. Then check the chosen topic and message type. Finally inspect quality-of-service compatibility if messages still do not arrive.","A graphical tool being open does not prove camera data is flowing. ROS 1 wiki links in these slides explain tool ideas, but use the slide’s ROS 2 commands for this Humble environment."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Which tool is meant to show camera images?","choices":["rqt_console","rqt_image_view","rqt_tf_tree"],"answer":1,"why":"It subscribes to image streams and displays them.","remediate":"Choose the tool whose job matches the data: images need an image viewer."},{"prompt":"Which tool helps show publisher/subscriber connections?","choices":["rqt_graph","A colour picker","The wheel circumference formula"],"answer":0,"why":"It draws the ROS computation graph.","remediate":"A graph is a picture of who connects to whom."},{"prompt":"A similarly named compressed topic is safe for any Image subscriber. Is that true?","choices":["Yes, names determine message types","Yes, all compressed data is raw pixels","No, inspect its actual message type and transport"],"answer":2,"why":"The topic name does not convert encoded data into an Image message.","remediate":"Read the label on the package before trying to unpack it."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w5-s10","title":"Demonstration: CvBridge between ROS and OpenCV","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 5 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"10–11"},"cards":[{"title":"Two representations of one picture","text":["ROS Image is a message format used to carry images between nodes. OpenCV operations in Python commonly work on NumPy arrays. NumPy is a Python library for numerical arrays. CvBridge converts between these representations.","Encoding describes how pixels are stored, such as bgr8 for three 8-bit BGR channels. Passthrough keeps the message’s existing encoding; it does not force a particular colour order."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Message → array → message","text":["The slide shows imgmsg_to_cv2 to convert a ROS message into an array and cv2_to_imgmsg for the reverse. Choose bgr8 when the following operations expect BGR colour. Preserve a useful input header when republishing a processed image."],"kind":"friendly","code":"from cv_bridge import CvBridge\nbridge = CvBridge()\nbgr_image = bridge.imgmsg_to_cv2(image_message, desired_encoding='bgr8')\nprocessed_message = bridge.cv2_to_imgmsg(bgr_image, encoding='bgr8')\nprocessed_message.header = image_message.header"},{"title":"Your source-only bridge example","text":["The actual file is src/example_codes/example_codes/opencv_bridge.py. It subscribes to /camera, while the course simulator normally publishes /limo_camera/image. A ROS remapping changes which topic that subscription uses without rewriting the script.","The example displays colour, grayscale blur and Canny windows. Grayscale discards colour; resize changes displayed dimensions. These are image-processing stages, not object recognition by themselves."],"kind":"friendly","code":"python3 src/example_codes/example_codes/opencv_bridge.py --ros-args -r /camera:=/limo_camera/image"},{"title":"Worked example: order matters","text":["A bgr8 pixel (255,0,0) is blue because the first value is B. In rgb8, (255,0,0) is red because the first value is R. Same three numbers; different meaning.","If conversion fails, stop that callback before using an undefined image. Check the actual encoding, image availability and matching subscription quality of service. Source inspection is evidence about code; these lessons do not claim a live camera test occurred."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What does CvBridge help convert?","choices":["Wheel distance into time","ROS Image messages and OpenCV/NumPy images","GPS satellites into pixels"],"answer":1,"why":"It bridges the message representation and processing representation.","remediate":"A message is an envelope for sending data; an array is useful for calculating on it."},{"prompt":"What colour is bgr8 (255,0,0)?","choices":["Red","Green","Blue"],"answer":2,"why":"BGR stores blue first.","remediate":"Read the letters left to right: B, G, R."},{"prompt":"How can the supplied /camera subscription receive /limo_camera/image?","choices":["Use a ROS topic remapping","Delete the camera","Rename every package"],"answer":0,"why":"Remapping changes the topic name used at runtime.","remediate":"The subscriber can keep its code name while ROS routes that name to the real topic."}],"research":[{"title":"ROS 2 Humble CvBridge source","url":"https://github.com/ros-perception/vision_opencv/blob/humble/cv_bridge/python/cv_bridge/core.py","addition":"Verified passthrough preserves pixel encoding while explicit bgr8 asks for BGR conversion.","anchor":"Week 5 demo pp10–11"}]},{"id":"w5-s11","title":"Demonstration: RGB, HSV, colour masks and contours","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 5 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"12–14"},"cards":[{"title":"RGB and HSV describe colours differently","text":["RGB adds red, green and blue light. In an 8-bit RGB example, (0,0,0) is black and (255,255,255) is white. HSV splits colour into Hue (colour type), Saturation (how vivid versus grey), and Value (brightness).","HSV can make colour selection easier than directly combining RGB limits, but it does not remove all lighting problems. OpenCV’s standard 8-bit HSV uses H from 0–179 and S and V from 0–255. A website hue in degrees normally needs dividing by two before using these OpenCV limits."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Thresholding creates a mask","text":["Convert BGR to HSV with cv2.cvtColor. cv2.inRange keeps pixels whose three values lie within chosen bounds. The result is a mask: a single-channel selection image, usually 255 for selected and 0 for rejected.","Worked example: with lower=(40,100,50) and upper=(80,255,255), pixel HSV=(60,200,180) passes all three bounds. Pixel (60,20,180) fails because saturation 20 is below 100."],"kind":"friendly","code":"hsv = cv2.cvtColor(bgr_image, cv2.COLOR_BGR2HSV)\nmask = cv2.inRange(hsv, (40, 100, 50), (80, 255, 255))"},{"title":"Find each boundary","text":["cv2.findContours finds boundaries of selected regions in the binary mask. RETR_TREE preserves nested contour relationships. CHAIN_APPROX_SIMPLE stores fewer redundant boundary points.","Each contour is an array of boundary points, typically (x, y) image coordinates. Area from cv2.contourArea is in pixel units, not square metres. A bounding box also lives in image coordinates unless more geometry is applied."],"kind":"friendly","code":"contours, hierarchy = cv2.findContours(mask, cv2.RETR_TREE, cv2.CHAIN_APPROX_SIMPLE)\nfor contour in contours:\n    area = cv2.contourArea(contour)\n    if area > 100:\n        cv2.drawContours(bgr_image, [contour], -1, (0, 0, 255), 2)"},{"title":"Your actual colour exercise","text":["The file src/example_codes/example_codes/colour_contours.py subscribes to /limo_camera/image and uses the green HSV interval above. Its drawing tuple (0,0,255) is red in BGR, despite its comment saying blue. Use [contour] when drawing a single contour.","The source comment claiming hue is 0–255 is incorrect for this conversion. Red hue can wrap around the start/end of the hue scale, so detecting all red shades can need two masks joined together. The supplied colour_contours_detector.py covers only a high-hue band."],"kind":"danger","code":"python3 src/example_codes/example_codes/colour_contours.py"},{"title":"What you have learned, and what is next","text":["Camera message → BGR array → HSV array → mask → contours → area filter → drawing. Explain each arrow before using it. A coloured region is evidence of a colour, not a guaranteed object identity.","Lecture reading pointers: Introduction to Autonomous Mobile Robots, chapter 4.3–4.5 and 4.7; Elements of Robotics, chapter 12; Robotics, Vision and Control, chapters 11–12; Introduction to Intelligent Robot System Design, chapter 8.4. These are named readings on page 47, not claims that their full books were supplied."],"kind":"readmore"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"HSV (60,20,180) with bounds (40,100,50) to (80,255,255): does it pass?","choices":["Yes, hue alone decides","No, saturation is too low","No, every value exceeds 255"],"answer":1,"why":"All three channels must be inside their corresponding interval.","remediate":"Check H, then S, then V; one failing channel rejects the pixel."},{"prompt":"A contour area of 150 means what in this image-processing step?","choices":["150 square metres","150 satellite measurements","An area measured in image pixel units"],"answer":2,"why":"The operation knows image geometry, not real-world distance.","remediate":"A photograph’s outline size is counted in pixels until camera/depth geometry adds scale."},{"prompt":"What does a selected colour mask guarantee?","choices":["Those pixels satisfy the chosen colour limits","That every region is the target object","That lighting cannot matter"],"answer":0,"why":"Thresholds select colour values; object meaning needs more evidence.","remediate":"A green wall and a green toy can both pass the same colour test."}],"research":[{"title":"OpenCV inRange tutorial","url":"https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/d97/tutorial_threshold_inRange.html","addition":"Verified HSV channel meaning and half-degree hue scaling to avoid copying unsuitable colour-picker limits.","anchor":"Week 5 demo pp12–13"},{"title":"OpenCV contours tutorial","url":"https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/d73/tutorial_py_contours_begin.html","addition":"Verified binary-mask contour input, two-result API, list wrapping for drawing one contour and point compression.","anchor":"Week 5 demo p14"}]}]},{"number":6,"title":"Enhancement Week","support":true,"note":"No Week 6 teaching file was supplied. This is a labelled review of Weeks 1–5, matching the outline slot.","sections":[{"id":"w6-s1","title":"Enhancement Week · Rebuild the foundation","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 1 Student.pdf","pages":"10"},"cards":[{"title":"This is a review slot, not an unseen lecture","text":["The original outline calls Week 6 Enhancement Week. No separate Week 6 lecture or demonstration was downloaded. This activity reviews Weeks 1–5 in that slot; it does not claim to reproduce missing lecturer content.","Pause here. Review any sections you passed by guessing. You want to explain the ideas in your own words before starting probabilistic localisation."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Five things you can now explain","text":["Week 1: see, think, act and the three navigation questions. Week 2: how the wheel arrangement limits motion. Week 3: average wheel speed moves the centre; wheel-speed difference turns it.","Week 4: sensors give noisy measurements in named coordinate frames. Week 5: image-processing steps turn pixels into edges, masks or detections."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Connect one tiny chain","text":["A laser beam measures distance in laser_link. To draw its endpoint in odom, transform the coordinates. A camera colour mask gives pixels; those pixels are not yet metre coordinates in a map.","A Twist command specifies speed; it is not proof the robot travelled the intended distance. Odometry estimates travel, but it drifts. These gaps motivate Week 7."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"A practical review you can do without motion","text":["Open the original sources for each week. Draw your workspace tree and the see–think–act chain. Read one Python example without running it. Write down inputs, outputs and missing assumptions.","If you cannot name a message’s fields or its frame, return to the earlier demonstration. Do not solve confusion by copying a later launch command."],"kind":"friendly"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Why is this Week 6 activity labelled review/support?","choices":["The outline includes a review slot, but no separate Week 6 file was supplied","It replaces Week 7","Week 6 teaches a new unseen algorithm"],"answer":0,"why":"The activity uses supplied earlier material while preserving the original outline slot.","remediate":"The title in the outline is evidence of the slot, not evidence of a missing lecture’s detailed contents."},{"prompt":"A red region covers200 pixels. Can you immediately report its physical area as200 m²?","choices":["Yes","No, physical scale and geometry are needed","Only if the robot is moving"],"answer":1,"why":"Pixels count image samples, not square metres. Camera geometry or a justified scale is needed.","remediate":"A photo of a coin can use100 or1000 pixels depending on distance and resolution; the real coin did not grow."},{"prompt":"A0.2 m/s command for 5 s proves what?","choices":["Exact measured distance is 1 m","All sensors are calibrated","A nominal1 m motion was requested; actual travel still needs checking"],"answer":2,"why":"Speed×time describes intended ideal motion; slip and timing may change actual motion.","remediate":"Distinguish the instruction from what really happened."},{"prompt":"A laser endpoint has coordinates but no frame label. What is missing?","choices":["The reference coordinate system","Its file extension","A faster motor"],"answer":0,"why":"Coordinates only make sense relative to a named frame.","remediate":"“Two metres ahead”needs you to know ahead of which object."}],"practice":{"prompt":"On paper, explain one Week 1–5 concept without looking at the answer, then check its original section.","expected":"You can state the meaning, the units if relevant, one worked example, and one common mistake."},"research":[]}]},{"number":7,"title":"Mobile Robot Localization I","sections":[{"id":"w7-s1","title":"Where am I? Position, heading and pose","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 7.pdf","pages":"2–7"},"cards":[{"title":"Pose means two things","text":["Localization means working out where the robot is relative to its surroundings. Position says where. Orientation says which way it faces. Together they are its pose.","For a robot moving on a floor, write pose as (x, y, θ). x and y are distances along two map directions, in metres. θ, pronounced theta, is the heading angle."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Three localization problems","text":["Position tracking starts with a known pose and follows changes from there. Global localization starts without knowing the pose and searches across a known map.","The kidnapped robot problem happens when someone moves the robot without its motion sensors noticing. Its old estimate can then be confidently wrong."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked example","text":["Pose (2 m, 3 m, 90°) means two metres along the x direction, three along y, facing a quarter turn from the x direction.","Starting there and tracking wheel movement is position tracking. Starting somewhere in the same room without knowing where is global localization."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Your estimate is a guess with evidence","text":["The robot does not directly know its true pose. It estimates it from sensors and movement. If its own pose is uncertain, the positions it assigns to nearby objects become uncertain too."],"kind":"friendly"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What does pose include?","choices":["Only battery level","Position and orientation","Only distance travelled"],"answer":1,"why":"Two robots at the same spot can face different ways; pose distinguishes them.","remediate":"Imagine standing at a door. Your position is the door; your orientation is whether you face inside or outside."},{"prompt":"A robot starts at a measured pose and counts wheel movement. Which problem is this?","choices":["Position tracking","Global localization","Path planning"],"answer":0,"why":"A known starting pose lets it track changes instead of searching the whole map.","remediate":"Start with one known place, then add each movement."},{"prompt":"Someone lifts a running robot into another room. What problem results?","choices":["A perfect map","A longer wheel","The kidnapped robot problem"],"answer":2,"why":"Its old movement history does not contain that unmeasured jump.","remediate":"The computer still believes the old place; new observations must help it recover."}],"practice":{"prompt":"Describe your own pose in a room using position and facing direction.","expected":"Give both a location and a direction, not only one."},"research":[]},{"id":"w7-s2","title":"Why wheel estimates drift","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 7.pdf","pages":"8–12"},"cards":[{"title":"Noise means imperfect information","text":["Sensor noise is variation in readings. Effector noise is variation in what movement actually happens. A wheel can rotate while slipping, so rotation is not the same as distance across the floor.","Sensor aliasing means different places look similar to a sensor, like two identical corridors."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Two kinds of error","text":["A systematic error repeats in a predictable way, such as an incorrect wheel diameter. Calibration means measuring and correcting that predictable mismatch.","A random error varies, such as occasional slip. We describe its uncertainty rather than pretending to remove it completely."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked example: a small wrong heading","text":["The lecture considers a 10 m movement with a heading error Δθ. Δ means change or error. For an added example of Δθ = 1°, sideways error is about 10 × sin(1°) = 0.175 m.","That is roughly 17.5 cm of sideways error from a one-degree mistake. Longer journeys can turn small heading mistakes into large position errors."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Odometry and dead reckoning","text":["Odometry integrates measured movement, commonly from wheel encoders. Integrating means adding many small movements over time. A velocity model instead uses speed, direction and elapsed time.","GPS can help outside, but ordinary GPS accuracy and indoor reception can be inadequate. Combining sensors helps; it does not make every accumulated error disappear."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"A wheel spins on a slippery floor. What can go wrong?","choices":["Wheel distance can differ from actual movement","The map must shrink","The robot gets perfect localization"],"answer":0,"why":"The sensor measures wheel rotation, while slip changes the relationship to floor movement.","remediate":"Count steps on ice: your feet move, but your body may move a different distance."},{"prompt":"Two identical corridors give similar laser readings. This is…","choices":["Calibration","Sensor aliasing","A battery fault"],"answer":1,"why":"The observation does not uniquely identify a location.","remediate":"One clue can fit two places; keep both possibilities until more clues arrive."},{"prompt":"Why can a tiny heading error matter over 10 m?","choices":["Heading never affects position","Only colour changes","The sideways error grows with travel distance"],"answer":2,"why":"Each forward step follows the wrong direction, accumulating lateral error.","remediate":"Draw two almost parallel lines from the same start and compare their separation far away."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w7-s3","title":"The rotation–translation–rotation motion model","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 7.pdf","pages":"13–16"},"cards":[{"title":"Break one move into three small pieces","text":["The lecture models a movement as turn towards the next position, move straight, then turn to the final heading. Translation means a change of position. Rotation means a change of angle.","The symbols δrot1, δtrans and δrot2 name those three changes. A bar over x, y or θ in the slide denotes an odometry estimate."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Read the three formulas gently","text":["δtrans = √((x′ − x)² + (y′ − y)²). Subtract coordinates, square both differences, add them and take the square root to get the straight-line distance.","δrot1 = atan2(y′ − y, x′ − x) − θ. atan2 finds the direction of the movement. δrot2 = θ′ − θ − δrot1 supplies the remaining final turn."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked example: three metres and four metres","text":["Start at (0 m, 0 m, 0°), end at (3 m, 4 m, 90°). Distance = √(3² + 4²) = √25 = 5 m.","The travel direction is atan2(4, 3) ≈ 53.13°. First turn ≈ 53.13°; last turn = 90° − 0° − 53.13° ≈ 36.87°. These example numbers are added for practice."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Noise spreads the possible endpoints","text":["Gaussian noise is a bell-shaped model of error. The slide samples many possible movements: noise in distance and turns spreads the endpoints, sometimes into a curved banana shape.","The true motion is not assumed to have zero mean; the zero-mean Gaussian describes the error around the motion. Normalize angle differences across ±180° or ±π radians."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"In this model, what happens between the two rotations?","choices":["Map deletion","Translation","An unknown fourth turn"],"answer":1,"why":"The middle step changes position along the chosen travel direction.","remediate":"Turn to face the destination, walk, then face the requested final direction."},{"prompt":"A displacement of 3 m in x and 4 m in y has what straight-line length?","choices":["7 m","1 m","5 m"],"answer":2,"why":"The right-triangle distance is √(9 + 16), which is 5 m.","remediate":"Draw a right triangle with sides 3 and 4; its diagonal is the distance."},{"prompt":"Why can possible endpoints form a banana shape?","choices":["Uncertain turns rotate the displacement direction","The robot always travels on a banana","Gaussian errors mean perfect certainty"],"answer":0,"why":"Small angle variations bend the collection of possible endpoint locations.","remediate":"Hold a pen at the end of a ruler and rotate the ruler slightly: its tip traces an arc."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w7-s4","title":"Belief, probability and the Gaussian bell","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 7.pdf","pages":"17–21"},"cards":[{"title":"Belief is a distribution of possibilities","text":["State x means the quantities we estimate. Control u means an action or movement input. Observation z means measured sensor evidence.","A belief assigns confidence to possible states. A continuous representation allows any coordinate value. A discrete representation uses bins such as grid cells. Single hypothesis means one main candidate; multiple hypotheses means several candidate locations."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Mean and uncertainty","text":["A Gaussian uses a mean μ, the central estimate, and a variance σ², its squared spread. σ, pronounced sigma, is the standard deviation, in the same units as the quantity.","For several quantities we use a mean vector, a list of means, and a covariance matrix, a table showing spreads and how errors vary together. Filtering means updating this stored uncertainty as evidence arrives."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"The formula is a description, not a spell","text":["The lecture Gaussian formula is p(x) = [1/(σ√(2π))] × exp(−(x − μ)²/(2σ²)). exp is the exponential function. π is the circle constant.","For a continuous quantity p(x) is probability density. Probability belongs to an interval and is the area under the curve, not the height at one exact coordinate. About 68% of a Gaussian lies within one standard deviation of its mean."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked example and limits","text":["If μ = 2 m and σ = 0.1 m, the one-standard-deviation interval is 1.9 m to 2.1 m. A wider bell means less certainty.","The Kalman filter gives an optimal estimate under appropriate linear and Gaussian assumptions. The extended Kalman filter, EKF, approximates nonlinear motion, such as turning. One Gaussian cannot faithfully represent two distant equally plausible rooms. The slide phrase “discrete never lost” is an idealization; recovery still needs suitable models and evidence."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"A wider probability bell usually means…","choices":["More uncertainty","A bigger wheel","Exact certainty"],"answer":0,"why":"More possible values fit the estimate, so confidence in one narrow region is lower.","remediate":"A small circle around your guessed location is more precise than a large circle."},{"prompt":"What is μ in a Gaussian?","choices":["Its filename","Its mean or centre","Its battery voltage"],"answer":1,"why":"The curve is centred on μ, the estimated average value.","remediate":"Find the middle of the bell; that is the mean."},{"prompt":"For a continuous position, the height of p(x) at one point is…","choices":["Always the complete probability","Always zero height","A density; interval area gives probability"],"answer":2,"why":"A density curve spreads probability over a continuum of values.","remediate":"Shade a small range under the curve to represent its probability."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w7-s5","title":"ACT predicts; SEE corrects","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 7.pdf","pages":"22–24"},"cards":[{"title":"ACT: use what movement might have happened","text":["The prediction is written b̄el(xₜ) = ∫ p(xₜ | uₜ, xₜ₋₁) bel(xₜ₋₁) dxₜ₋₁. A subscript t means now; t−1 means the previous step. A vertical bar means “given”.","Read it as: consider each possible old pose, consider how the action could move it, and add all those possibilities. ∫ is a continuous sum. With grid cells we use Σ, an ordinary sum."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"SEE: use what the sensor actually sees","text":["The measurement update is bel(xₜ) = η p(zₜ | xₜ, M) b̄el(xₜ). M is the map. η, pronounced eta, rescales the result so total probability is one.","Read it as: predicted confidence × how well the observation fits this location, then normalize. The Markov assumption says the current state summarizes the past needed by the model."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked example: two possible locations","text":["Before a scan, A and B each have probability 0.5. The scan likelihood is 0.8 at A and 0.2 at B. Multiply: A = 0.5 × 0.8 = 0.4; B = 0.5 × 0.2 = 0.1.","Total = 0.5. Divide each by 0.5: A becomes 0.8, B becomes 0.2. The evidence strengthens A without making it completely certain."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Do the two stages in order","text":["Prediction moves and often spreads the belief. Correction uses sensor evidence to reweight it. Do not replace all previous evidence with one noisy reading."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Which stage uses the motion model?","choices":["SEE only","ACT prediction","A filename check"],"answer":1,"why":"The motion model links the previous pose and movement to possible new poses.","remediate":"ACT asks where a movement might have taken you."},{"prompt":"After multiplying probabilities, why normalize?","choices":["To restore a total probability of one","To change the robot colour","To remove every alternative"],"answer":0,"why":"The products no longer necessarily sum to one until divided by their total.","remediate":"If two scores are 4 and 1, divide by 5 to get 0.8 and 0.2."},{"prompt":"For predicted weights 0.5/0.5 and likelihoods 0.8/0.2, the updated weights are…","choices":["0.4 and 0.1, already final","1 and 1","0.8 and 0.2"],"answer":2,"why":"The raw products 0.4 and 0.1 total 0.5; dividing by that total gives 0.8 and 0.2.","remediate":"Multiply first, add the results, then divide each result by the sum."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w7-s6","title":"How sensor fusion balances two imperfect estimates","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 7.pdf","pages":"25–30"},"cards":[{"title":"Follow the original coloured diagrams","text":["The lecture ACT diagram starts with a known position, then shows progressively wider bells after movement. Proprioceptive sensors measure the robot itself, such as wheel encoders.","SEE uses an exteroceptive sensor, which measures the outside world, such as a rangefinder looking at a mapped pillar. The fused green bell combines the blue prediction and red observation."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"A tiny one-dimensional Kalman example","text":["Additional teaching example: predicted position x⁻ = 2.0 m with variance P⁻ = 0.04 m²; measured position z = 2.4 m with measurement variance R = 0.01 m². A minus superscript means before the new observation.","Kalman gain K = P⁻/(P⁻ + R) = 0.04/0.05 = 0.8. Gain tells us how much of the measurement disagreement to apply."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Calculate the corrected estimate","text":["Corrected x = x⁻ + K(z − x⁻) = 2.0 + 0.8(2.4 − 2.0) = 2.32 m. Corrected variance P = (1 − K)P⁻ = 0.2 × 0.04 = 0.008 m².","The result is closer to the more precise measurement and has smaller uncertainty. These formulas assume a scalar measurement of the same position, independent Gaussian errors and a correct model."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"A landmark bounds drift","text":["A landmark is a recognizable external reference. Looking at a known landmark can correct accumulated relative motion error.","A correction does not guarantee truth if the landmark is misidentified. Uncertainty need not decrease when a measurement is uninformative or the model is wrong. A global map reference can support tracking without restarting a full global search every time."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Which is an exteroceptive measurement?","choices":["A wheel encoder count","A laser distance to a wall","A motor command"],"answer":1,"why":"The laser observes something outside the robot.","remediate":"Inside the robot: wheel rotation. Outside: wall distance."},{"prompt":"In the example, K = 0.8 gives corrected x…","choices":["2.32 m","20 m","0.8 m"],"answer":0,"why":"Apply 80% of the 0.4 m disagreement: 2 + 0.32 = 2.32 m.","remediate":"First find the disagreement, then multiply it by the gain, then add it to the prediction."},{"prompt":"Why does fusion prefer the measurement here?","choices":["Its filename is shorter","It is always newer","Its variance is smaller"],"answer":2,"why":"R = 0.01 is smaller than P⁻ = 0.04, so the sensor is treated as more precise.","remediate":"A smaller spread means more confidence, provided the uncertainty model is honest."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w7-s7","title":"Original lecture check: movement, wall and fused belief","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 7.pdf","pages":"31–39"},"cards":[{"title":"ACT question on page 32","text":["The robot begins at x₀ with probability 1 and moves forward twice, to estimated x₁ and x₂. The selected diagram shows the movement estimates widening as odometry uncertainty accumulates.","The sensor category is proprioceptive: wheel odometry measures its own movement. This is a qualitative question; the slide supplies no distances for numerical calculation."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"SEE question on page 33","text":["The laser reports a distance x to a mapped wall. The observation suggests a position xmeas with nonzero uncertainty; a bell-shaped measurement estimate is appropriate, rather than an infinitely precise spike.","The sensor category is exteroceptive. The marked option is the third diagram, showing the sensor-based bell at xmeas alongside the earlier odometry bell."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Fusion question on page 34","text":["The selected posterior, meaning the updated belief after evidence, sits between xodo and xmeas and is narrower in the illustrated Gaussian case.","It is formed by multiplying the prior and likelihood and normalizing, not averaging picture heights or creating an arbitrary second peak."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Read and prepare for the demonstration","text":["The lecture reading list is Chapter 5, sections 5.1–5.4 and 5.6.8 on Kalman filter localization. The demonstration introduces motion models and robot_localization; the workshop topic is fusion of LIMO sensors with an EKF.","Before continuing, explain aloud why movement broadens a belief and a reliable external observation can narrow it."],"kind":"friendly"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"In the original ACT exercise, which sensor category is used?","choices":["Exteroceptive camera","No sensor","Proprioceptive wheel odometry"],"answer":2,"why":"The motion estimate comes from measuring the robot itself.","remediate":"The wheel sensor watches the wheel, not the wall."},{"prompt":"Does the original wall rangefinder have zero uncertainty?","choices":["No: the observation has a spread","Yes: laser means perfect","Yes: every map is exact"],"answer":0,"why":"The exercise explicitly states nonzero rangefinder uncertainty.","remediate":"A precise-looking number can still have measurement error."},{"prompt":"Which posterior matches the illustrated fusion?","choices":["A wider bell far outside both estimates","A narrower bell between the estimates","Two unrelated final peaks"],"answer":1,"why":"The shown compatible Gaussian estimates multiply into a more concentrated compromise.","remediate":"Imagine two imperfect clues agreeing on an overlapping region."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w7-s8","title":"Demonstration: smoothing odometry with robot_localization","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 7 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"1–3"},"cards":[{"title":"What this package does","text":["robot_localization is a ROS package that combines measurements into a state estimate. A ROS message is a structured packet of information carried on a topic, a named data channel.","It can combine wheel, IMU, laser or visual odometry and appropriately integrated GPS-based measurements. GPS supplies external positioning when reception and accuracy are adequate. IMU means inertial measurement unit: a sensor measuring quantities such as rotational velocity and acceleration."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"One update loop","text":["Predict the next state, receive a new measurement, then update the estimate and uncertainty. Repeat with each new data step.","Loosely coupled fusion means the filter consumes sensor estimates/messages, rather than jointly processing every raw camera pixel or laser point inside one estimator."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"What the state contains","text":["The slide calls the model six-dimensional for its three positions and three orientations. The full filter state contains 15 variables once velocity and acceleration are included; the next demonstration page lists them.","For a floor robot, a planar configuration can ignore out-of-plane movement. Planar means constrained to the floor plane."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"What success looks like","text":["Combined odometry should be smoother and locally accurate. It can still drift because combining relative sources does not automatically provide a fixed global position."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What is the main demonstration goal?","choices":["Delete every sensor","Fuse odometry sources into a smoother estimate","Create a path without sensors"],"answer":1,"why":"The filter uses multiple measurements to improve local state estimation.","remediate":"Several imperfect movement clues can be combined with their confidence."},{"prompt":"Does smoother odometry guarantee no long-term drift?","choices":["Yes, always","Only with a blue line","No, relative sources can still drift"],"answer":2,"why":"Smoothness describes variation in the output; it is not proof of global correctness.","remediate":"A smooth line can slowly move away from the truth."},{"prompt":"What is an IMU?","choices":["An inertial measurement unit","A map image","A keyboard"],"answer":0,"why":"It provides inertial measurements such as acceleration and angular velocity.","remediate":"Think of a device that senses acceleration and turning."}],"research":[{"title":"robot_localization Humble configuration","url":"https://github.com/cra-ros-pkg/robot_localization/blob/humble-devel/doc/configuring_robot_localization.rst","addition":"Verified the 15-value mask order, planar two_d_mode, and avoiding duplicate correlated encoder information.","anchor":"Week 7 demonstration pages 3 and 6"}]},{"id":"w7-s9","title":"Demonstration: probabilistic motion and laser scan matching","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 7 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"4–5"},"cards":[{"title":"Follow the demonstration numbers","text":["Page 4 illustrates dx = 0.2, dy = 0.35, previous heading 30° and final heading 10°. dx and dy mean position changes along x and y.","As an explanatory calculation, distance = √(0.2² + 0.35²) ≈ 0.403 m. Travel direction ≈ atan2(0.35, 0.2) = 60.26°. First turn ≈ 30.26°; final turn ≈ −50.26°."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Laser_scan_matcher","text":["The demonstration introduces incremental laser scan registration: align the current scan to the previous scan to estimate movement. Incremental means one small update after another.","ICP means Iterative Closest Point. It repeatedly pairs nearby points and adjusts their alignment. The lecture describes subscribing to /scan and publishing estimated /odom."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked idea","text":["If a wall appears to shift 0.1 m backwards relative to the sensor, scan matching may infer the sensor moved 0.1 m forwards. Repeated geometry and noisy scans can make the alignment ambiguous."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Inspect the actual interface","text":["Topic names can be remapped, meaning renamed in a launch configuration. Treat /scan and /odom here as the lecture description; check the actual running node before wiring its output into a filter."],"kind":"danger","code":"ros2 topic list -t\nros2 node list\nros2 topic info /scan"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What does scan matching compare?","choices":["Two battery levels","Only the goal","Consecutive sensor scans"],"answer":2,"why":"Changes in observed geometry can reveal relative movement.","remediate":"Align yesterday’s sketch with today’s sketch to see how the viewpoint shifted."},{"prompt":"For dx = 0.2 and dy = 0.35, the distance is approximately…","choices":["0.403 m","0.55 m exactly","35 m"],"answer":0,"why":"The displacement length is √(0.04 + 0.1225), about 0.403.","remediate":"Use the diagonal distance formula rather than adding the two axes."},{"prompt":"Why inspect the running topics?","choices":["To change all filenames","Launch files can remap the names","The lecture never uses messages"],"answer":1,"why":"The implementation can publish under configured names, which must match the filter inputs.","remediate":"A data channel’s name on your machine is the one your subscriber must use."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w7-s10","title":"Demonstration: the 15 switches in an EKF sensor mask","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 7 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"6"},"cards":[{"title":"The order matters","text":["The state order is x, y, z, roll, pitch, yaw; vx, vy, vz, roll-rate, pitch-rate, yaw-rate; ax, ay, az. v means velocity, a means acceleration, and a rate means change per second.","Roll is rotation around x, pitch around y, yaw around z. On a floor, yaw is the heading. Each sensor configuration has 15 true/false switches in this exact order."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"The original illustrated mask","text":["The demonstration selects x, y, yaw, vx and yaw-rate. true means use this measured variable; false means do not use it.","The slide shows ROS 1 XML syntax. For ROS 2 the equivalent settings belong under the node name and ros__parameters in YAML, a readable configuration format."],"kind":"friendly","code":"ekf_filter_node:\n  ros__parameters:\n    two_d_mode: true\n    odom0: /YOUR_VERIFIED_ODOMETRY_TOPIC\n    odom0_config: [true, true, false,\n                   false, false, true,\n                   true, false, false,\n                   false, false, true,\n                   false, false, false]"},{"title":"Worked mask reading","text":["The sixth entry is yaw, so true there includes measured heading. The seventh entry is vx, so true there includes forward velocity. The twelfth entry is yaw-rate.","Covariance tells the filter how uncertain a measurement is. Incorrectly tiny covariance can make it overtrust a bad sensor."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Before using the template","text":["YOUR_VERIFIED_ODOMETRY_TOPIC is a placeholder, not a real topic to copy unchanged. Match actual topics and coordinate frames. Do not assume all variables in an encoder-derived pose and velocity are independent."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What does true in the mask mean?","choices":["Force the robot to that value","Include that measured variable","Create a new sensor"],"answer":1,"why":"The mask chooses which fields of this sensor’s message enter the filter.","remediate":"A switch selects information; it does not invent a measurement."},{"prompt":"Which entry selects yaw-rate?","choices":["Entry 1","Entry 15","Entry 12"],"answer":2,"why":"The ordering has six pose variables, then six velocity variables; yaw-rate is the last velocity entry.","remediate":"Count groups of six, six and three."},{"prompt":"Why can a tiny covariance be harmful when wrong?","choices":["It tells the filter to overtrust the sensor","It always stops drift","It changes the topic name"],"answer":0,"why":"Covariance acts as a confidence statement; false confidence distorts fusion.","remediate":"A bad clue labelled extremely reliable can dominate a good clue."}],"research":[]}],"note":"Follows the lecture before the demonstration. The original pages 31–34 contain a graphical quiz, not an unseen numerical worksheet. Additional small numerical examples below are teaching aids."},{"number":8,"title":"Mobile Robot Localization II","sections":[{"id":"w8-s1","title":"Map-based navigation and global localization","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 8.pdf","pages":"1–5"},"cards":[{"title":"Return to the three problems","text":["Position tracking knows its start. Global localization searches for an initially unknown pose. Kidnapping breaks a previously good estimate without a matching odometry movement.","This week uses a known map and sensor observations to maintain multiple possible poses when needed."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Two navigation styles","text":["Behavior-based navigation can say “follow the left wall until the fourth door.” It can fail if a door is missed or the goal is not recognized.","Map-based navigation estimates the robot’s location in a map repeatedly, so movement and observations can be checked against a world reference."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked example","text":["Two corridors have the same doors. A single scan might fit both. Move to a junction: if only one corridor has that junction, the new observation can remove the other location hypothesis. A hypothesis means a possible explanation."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Two representations to choose","text":["We need a representation for the environment and a representation for confidence in the robot’s pose. They are related but different: a map says what is there; a belief says where the robot might be."],"kind":"friendly"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What extra reference does map-based localization use?","choices":["A known environment map","Only elapsed time","Only a wheel colour"],"answer":0,"why":"It compares sensed surroundings with stored environment structure.","remediate":"Use a floor plan to interpret what you can see."},{"prompt":"Why keep two corridor hypotheses initially?","choices":["To waste memory","They both fit the current evidence","To avoid observing"],"answer":1,"why":"A nonunique observation cannot honestly choose one location with certainty.","remediate":"If two doors look identical, wait for a more distinctive clue."},{"prompt":"Which strategy is most vulnerable to missing one counted door?","choices":["Map-based pose estimation","An EKF covariance","Follow the wall to the fourth door"],"answer":2,"why":"A missed count changes where the behavior stops.","remediate":"Counting objects without checking location can shift the target by one."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w8-s2","title":"Ways to represent the map","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 8.pdf","pages":"6–11"},"cards":[{"title":"Continuous lines and exact cells","text":["A line-based map represents walls as lines, much like an architectural plan. It preserves geometry but can require costly calculations.","Exact cell decomposition divides free space into polygon regions that fit obstacle boundaries. A polygon is a closed shape with straight edges; decomposition means splitting into parts."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Fixed and adaptive grids","text":["An occupancy grid divides the environment into equal cells and stores occupancy information in each one. Fidelity means how well the representation preserves the real geometry.","A coarse grid can erase a narrow passage. Adaptive decomposition uses small cells where detail matters and larger cells elsewhere; the page 9 picture shows different cell sizes."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Topological maps","text":["A topological map is a graph of places and their connections. A node represents a place; an edge connects places that can be reached from each other.","Example: Kitchen — Hall — Bedroom preserves connectivity without needing exact centimetre coordinates. Pure topology need not encode scale; distances can optionally be attached as extra information."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked resolution example","text":["An added example: a 0.4 m doorway represented with 0.5 m cells can disappear in the grid. With 0.05 m cells it spans about eight cells, preserving more detail. Smaller cells require more memory."],"kind":"friendly"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What can a coarse fixed grid lose?","choices":["Every heading definition","Narrow passages","All computer speed"],"answer":1,"why":"A small gap may be merged into occupied cells at coarse resolution.","remediate":"Draw a thin gap with a thick marker: the gap can disappear."},{"prompt":"In a topological map, an edge normally represents…","choices":["A connection between places","A sensor voltage","An angle covariance"],"answer":0,"why":"The graph describes which locations connect.","remediate":"Rooms are dots; doorways or corridors connect the dots."},{"prompt":"Adaptive decomposition uses…","choices":["Only equal large cells","No cells ever","Different cell sizes where appropriate"],"answer":2,"why":"Detail is concentrated where geometry requires it.","remediate":"Use fine paper near a doorway and coarse paper in an empty room."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w8-s3","title":"Why a full pose grid becomes expensive","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 8.pdf","pages":"12–14"},"cards":[{"title":"Count position and heading separately","text":["A floor pose uses x, y and θ, so a pose grid needs bins for both position and direction. One physical cell can contain many candidate headings.","The lecture room is 10 m × 10 m with 0.1 m cells and 1° heading bins. Each side has 10/0.1 = 100 cells. A full turn has 360 heading bins."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Original calculation","text":["States = 100 × 100 × 360 = 3,600,000 = 3.6 × 10⁶. The six means multiply by one million.","A naive prediction comparing every old state with every new state can require (3.6 × 10⁶)² = 1.296 × 10¹³ pair operations. This is a worst-case illustration, not the necessary cost of every implementation."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Reduce the burden","text":["Adaptive cells reduce unnecessary detail. A limited motion model only considers reachable neighbouring states. Randomized sampling keeps a representative subset of possible poses.","Particles are samples of possible poses. Sensor fusion, changing environments and extracting useful information remain challenges even with a compact representation."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked change in resolution","text":["Halving cell width from 0.1 m to 0.05 m doubles both row and column counts. Position cells multiply by four, so the same angular bins give 14.4 million states.","More detail is useful only if the extra computation and sensor accuracy support it."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"How many pose states are in the lecture example?","choices":["36,000","360","3,600,000"],"answer":2,"why":"There are 100 × 100 position cells and 360 heading bins per cell.","remediate":"Multiply the x count, y count and direction count."},{"prompt":"Halving cell width in both planar directions changes cell count by…","choices":["Four times","Half","No change"],"answer":0,"why":"Each direction doubles its bins, giving 2 × 2 = 4 times as many cells.","remediate":"Count squares in a 2×2 grid versus a 4×4 grid."},{"prompt":"Which reduces full-grid computation?","choices":["Adding every impossible transition","Sampling representative poses","Ignoring all observations"],"answer":1,"why":"Samples approximate the belief with fewer candidates.","remediate":"Keep a useful sample instead of an exhaustive list of millions of possibilities."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w8-s4","title":"Markov localization and different belief shapes","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 8.pdf","pages":"15–16"},"cards":[{"title":"Same predict–correct loop, many candidates","text":["For each candidate pose: predicted belief = Σ(previous belief × motion probability). Corrected belief = η × observation likelihood × predicted belief.","The Markov assumption means we condition the model on the previous state and current input; the stored belief already summarizes earlier evidence."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Four pictured representations","text":["The lecture compares a single continuous Gaussian, a continuous distribution with several peaks, a discrete metric grid, and a distribution over topological places.","Multimodal means several peaks. This is useful when several rooms fit the same observation. A single Gaussian would blur the rooms into an often impossible midpoint."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked map belief","text":["Suppose A, B and C have predicted probabilities 0.2, 0.3, 0.5. A scan has likelihoods 0.1, 0.8, 0.2. Products are 0.02, 0.24, 0.10; total 0.36.","Updated probabilities are about 0.056, 0.667, 0.278. B becomes most likely because the observation fits it much better."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Do not confuse the two grids","text":["The occupancy grid represents environmental obstacles. The pose belief grid represents where and in which direction the robot might be. A belief can also use particles while the environment still uses an occupancy grid."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What does multimodal mean?","choices":["Multiple probability peaks","One perfect sensor","Only one map cell"],"answer":0,"why":"Several separated hypotheses remain plausible.","remediate":"Two rooms can each have a high probability peak."},{"prompt":"In the worked A/B/C update, which is most likely afterwards?","choices":["A","B","C"],"answer":1,"why":"B’s product 0.24 is largest even though C had the largest predicted probability.","remediate":"A strong matching observation can change which guess leads."},{"prompt":"Is an occupancy grid the same thing as a pose-belief grid?","choices":["Yes, always","Only if red","No, they represent different information"],"answer":2,"why":"One stores environment occupancy; the other stores robot pose confidence.","remediate":"Ask whether a value describes the wall or your possible location."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w8-s5","title":"Particles: a handful of possible robots","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 8.pdf","pages":"17–18"},"cards":[{"title":"One particle is one pose hypothesis","text":["A particle contains a candidate pose (x, y, θ) and a weight w. The weight says how strongly current evidence supports that candidate.","A particle filter approximates a probability distribution using many such samples. Monte Carlo names this use of random samples."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Why use this representation?","text":["A particle cloud can represent several separate location clusters and nonlinear movement without forcing one bell shape. Nonlinear means a simple straight-line relationship is insufficient.","More particles can cover possibilities better, but they cost computation and do not guarantee better localization if the models or observations are wrong."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked weighting","text":["Three candidate poses receive raw scan scores 6, 3 and 1. Total = 10. Normalized weights are 0.6, 0.3 and 0.1.","The first candidate currently has the best sensor match. The others remain possible; a future observation can change their ranking."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Estimate carefully","text":["The slide mentions highest-weight particle or averaging particles as a pose estimate. Averaging two distant clusters can put the estimate inside a wall. Prefer an appropriate dominant cluster when the belief has multiple separated peaks."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"A particle represents…","choices":["A tiny real motor","A candidate robot pose and weight","A new physical robot"],"answer":1,"why":"Particles are computational hypotheses, not separate machines.","remediate":"Imagine pins on a floor plan showing where the robot might be."},{"prompt":"Scores 6, 3, 1 normalize to…","choices":["0.6, 0.3, 0.1","6, 3, 1 unchanged","1, 1, 1"],"answer":0,"why":"Divide each score by their sum, 10.","remediate":"All final weights must add to one."},{"prompt":"Why can averaging two far-apart clusters be wrong?","choices":["Averages cannot be calculated","All particles are identical","The midpoint may be an impossible location"],"answer":2,"why":"Two plausible rooms can have an implausible wall between them.","remediate":"A location halfway between two good guesses is not automatically another good guess."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w8-s6","title":"Predict, update, resample and recover","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 8.pdf","pages":"19–22"},"cards":[{"title":"Predict and update do different jobs","text":["Prediction moves each particle according to measured motion, with random variation for movement uncertainty.","Update changes each particle’s weight according to how well the new observation would fit the map at that pose. A better matching scan means more support."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Resampling concentrates useful guesses","text":["Resampling draws a new set of particles in proportion to their weights. High-weight regions tend to get more copies; low-weight regions may lose particles.","With weights 0.6, 0.3 and 0.1 and ten new samples, expected counts are six, three and one. Actual random counts can vary."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Kidnapping needs fresh possibilities","text":["If every particle is near the old location after a jump, no local rearrangement alone can create the correct distant pose. Random recovery particles or global reinitialization can restore wider possibilities.","AMCL adapts particle count and can use average sensor-fit changes to support recovery."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Configuration affects recovery","text":["In the supplied example_codes/params/amcl.yaml both recovery_alpha_fast and recovery_alpha_slow are 0.0. Do not promise automatic random-particle kidnapping recovery with that configuration.","Reinitializing across free map space is a separate explicit recovery option. These observations concern the read-only supplied template, not a running robot."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Which operation mainly changes particle weights from a scan?","choices":["Predict","Update","Rename"],"answer":1,"why":"The observation model rates how well each hypothesis explains the sensor data.","remediate":"Motion shifts guesses; observation scores them."},{"prompt":"After resampling, high-weight regions tend to have…","choices":["Fewer guesses always","No guesses","More particle copies"],"answer":2,"why":"Sampling probability is proportional to the particle weights.","remediate":"More raffle tickets for a stronger guess means it is drawn more often."},{"prompt":"Why inject global recovery particles?","choices":["To consider locations absent from the old cloud","To guarantee the old location is correct","To replace the map with nothing"],"answer":0,"why":"Kidnapping can move the robot outside the current cloud’s represented region.","remediate":"You cannot find another room if every guess stays in the first room."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w8-s7","title":"Follow the original 10,000-particle storyboard","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 8.pdf","pages":"23–43"},"cards":[{"title":"The first image is uncertainty everywhere","text":["Page 23 shows 10,000 random hypotheses spread across free map space. The robot uses a laser rangefinder. Red samples are pose guesses; the scan fan is a geometric observation.","Page 24 illustrates a first promising scan match and motion prediction. A strongest candidate is evidence, not certainty."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Watch the repeated cycle","text":["Pages 25–26 illustrate resampling and reduced diversity. Pages 27–29 obtain another observation, weigh particles and resample with 10,000 hypotheses.","Pages 30–34 repeat prediction and observation. The slide notes that prediction spreads/smooths particles because movement is uncertain."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"The cloud converges","text":["Pages 35–39 show a small cluster following the robot as repeated observations make its location more confident. Convergence means the distribution settles around a consistent estimate.","The fixed 10,000 in this teaching storyboard is not a requirement for every AMCL configuration. It demonstrates the algorithm visually."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Reading and workshop","text":["The lecture assigns Chapter 5, sections 5.5–5.7. The demonstration introduces AMCL; the workshop topic is probabilistic map-based localization.","Before continuing, explain each red-cloud change as prediction, weight update or resampling."],"kind":"friendly"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Why are the first 10,000 hypotheses widely spread?","choices":["The robot initially lacks a known global pose","The robot occupies all rooms physically","The laser is a motor"],"answer":0,"why":"Global localization needs candidate positions across the map.","remediate":"Many starting guesses represent lack of location knowledge."},{"prompt":"Which stage usually spreads the guesses due to uncertain movement?","choices":["Deleting the map","Prediction","A file rename"],"answer":1,"why":"Noise in the motion model distributes possible endpoints.","remediate":"A step with uncertain distance and direction produces several possible destinations."},{"prompt":"Does the lecture storyboard require all AMCL runs to use 10,000 particles?","choices":["Yes forever","Only outside","No, it is an illustrative example"],"answer":2,"why":"AMCL particle limits depend on configuration and computational needs.","remediate":"Separate a demonstration number from a compulsory setting."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w8-s8","title":"Demonstration: what AMCL needs and produces","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 8 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"1–3"},"cards":[{"title":"AMCL means Adaptive Monte Carlo Localization","text":["It estimates a floor robot’s pose in a known two-dimensional map using a particle filter. A laser scan is compared to the stored map.","Odometry predicts changes between scans. Map matching supplies a global correction to reduce accumulated relative motion drift."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Adaptive means changing the number of guesses","text":["When the belief covers many plausible states, the algorithm can use more particles. When the estimate is well determined, it can use fewer, within configured limits.","This balances computation and representation quality; it does not remove all uncertainty."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked contrast","text":["An EKF that fuses wheels and IMU can give smooth local odometry. AMCL can then use the map and laser to estimate where that local track belongs in the global map.","AMCL normally localizes against a pre-existing map. Building an unknown map is the SLAM problem taught next week."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Check the inputs","text":["A usable map, valid coordinate transforms, laser observations and appropriate motion model are required. Setting an initial pose in RViz supplies a starting guess, including uncertainty."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What does AMCL normally require?","choices":["No environment reference","A known map and suitable sensor data","Only a goal coordinate"],"answer":1,"why":"The scan likelihood is calculated by comparison with the map.","remediate":"You need the floor plan before matching the laser view to it."},{"prompt":"What does adaptive refer to here?","choices":["Every obstacle moves","The robot changes colour","Particle count can change"],"answer":2,"why":"The representation size can be adjusted to the uncertainty.","remediate":"Use more guesses when many possibilities remain."},{"prompt":"Is AMCL itself the mapping lesson?","choices":["No, it localizes in a known map","Yes, it always builds a new map","It never estimates pose"],"answer":0,"why":"SLAM handles the coupled unknown-map problem separately.","remediate":"AMCL asks where on this map; SLAM asks where and what map."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w8-s9","title":"Demonstration: ROS 1 slide names versus Humble interfaces","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 8 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"4–6"},"cards":[{"title":"Inputs described by the lecture","text":["The conceptual inputs are scan, coordinate transforms, initialpose and map. initialpose supplies a pose guess and covariance. Covariance communicates uncertainty.","Humble uses sensor_msgs/msg/LaserScan, tf2_msgs/msg/TFMessage, geometry_msgs/msg/PoseWithCovarianceStamped and nav_msgs/msg/OccupancyGrid."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Outputs and frame relationship","text":["AMCL publishes amcl_pose, the estimated map pose with covariance. Humble publishes particle_cloud with nav2_msgs/msg/ParticleCloud; the slide’s particlecloud/PoseArray is a ROS 1 interface.","The map → odom transform tells us how the smooth local odometry frame sits in the global map. AMCL does not replace the odom → robot-base movement transform."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Services in Humble","text":["A service is a named request-and-response operation. Humble uses reinitialize_global_localization to spread guesses through free map space and request_nomotion_update to request a scan update without requiring movement.","The slide’s global_localization and static_map call pattern is legacy. Humble AMCL subscribes to the configured map topic. Check exact live topic/service names and types."],"kind":"friendly","code":"ros2 topic list -t\nros2 node info /amcl\nros2 service list -t\nros2 topic info /particle_cloud"},{"title":"Read-only checks come first","text":["The commands above inspect interfaces; they do not command robot motion. If the node is namespaced, use the actual full name shown by ros2 node list. Namespacing means adding a shared path prefix to related nodes/topics."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Which particle topic is verified in Humble source?","choices":["particlecloud with the ROS 1 type","cmd_vel","particle_cloud with nav2_msgs/msg/ParticleCloud"],"answer":2,"why":"The Humble AMCL publisher declares that topic and message type.","remediate":"Read the robot’s actual interface, not a legacy screenshot."},{"prompt":"Which transform does AMCL supply for global correction?","choices":["map → odom","odom → every laser point","battery → map"],"answer":0,"why":"It aligns the locally continuous odometry frame with the map.","remediate":"The local track keeps its smoothness while its placement on the map is corrected."},{"prompt":"How should you find exact service names on your system?","choices":["Guess from an old diagram","Use ros2 service list -t","Rename all services"],"answer":1,"why":"The CLI reports the running services and their types.","remediate":"Ask the system what exists before calling it."}],"research":[{"title":"Nav2 AMCL Humble implementation","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ros-navigation/navigation2/humble/nav2_amcl/src/amcl_node.cpp","addition":"Verified ROS 2 particle topic/type, global reinitialization service, map subscription, frame alignment and configurable recovery settings; corrected legacy slide names.","anchor":"Week 8 demonstration pages 4–9"},{"title":"ROS 2 tf2 TFMessage definition","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ros2/geometry2/humble/tf2_msgs/msg/TFMessage.msg","addition":"Verified the ROS 2 transform message package, replacing the slide’s ROS 1 tf/tfMessage spelling.","anchor":"Week 8 demonstration pages 4–5"}]},{"id":"w8-s10","title":"Demonstration: tune filter, laser and motion parameters","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 8 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"7–9"},"cards":[{"title":"Three families, in the slide order","text":["Overall filter settings control particle count, resampling, initial confidence and update thresholds. Laser settings control how scans are scored. Odometry settings describe uncertainty in measured movement.","Parameters are named settings. A model is the mathematical description used to interpret a sensor or movement."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Read the actual supplied template","text":["example_codes/params/amcl.yaml contains min_particles: 500, max_particles: 2000, max_beams: 60, laser_model_type: likelihood_field, and robot_model_type: nav2_amcl:: DifferentialMotionModel.","The alpha1–alpha5 parameters express motion-noise assumptions; larger noise values indicate less confidence in the relevant movement component. Do not copy ROS 1 odom_model_type into Humble."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked update thresholds","text":["The supplied update_min_d = 0.25 m and update_min_a = 0.2 rad are movement thresholds that can trigger updates; 0.2 rad is about 11.5°.","A scan with max_beams = 60 uses selected beams for localization scoring rather than requiring every raw range to be used. Change one setting at a time and observe the effect."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Template limitations to understand","text":["The YAML has use_sim_time: False, while the example launch applies simulated time. Verify the effective node setting rather than assuming the template text wins. base_frame_id is base_footprint; match the actual frame tree.","The supplied launch templates refer to limo_localisation, a workshop package the student must create; this package is absent from the untouched companion src tree. That is an exercise template, not a guaranteed ready-to-run launch."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Which family contains the laser observation model?","choices":["Only odometry settings","Laser parameters","Only filename settings"],"answer":1,"why":"These parameters describe how expected and measured ranges are compared.","remediate":"A movement model describes steps; a laser model describes wall-distance clues."},{"prompt":"With min_particles = 500 and max_particles = 2000, allowed adaptive counts are…","choices":["Within those configured bounds","Exactly 10,000 always","Always zero"],"answer":0,"why":"Adaptive sampling changes representation size subject to limits.","remediate":"Think of minimum and maximum as the ends of the permitted range."},{"prompt":"Why verify the effective use_sim_time parameter?","choices":["Its colour is important","All YAML is ignored","Launch configuration can also set it"],"answer":2,"why":"Multiple configuration sources can affect the running node.","remediate":"Inspect the final setting in the running system, not only one source file."}],"research":[]}],"note":"AMCL demonstration pages 4–9 contain ROS 1 names and parameters. Course concepts are preserved; ROS 2 Humble differences are explicitly marked."},{"number":9,"title":"Simultaneous Localization and Mapping (SLAM)","sections":[{"id":"w9-s1","title":"Estimate your place and draw the map together","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 9.pdf","pages":"1–7"},"cards":[{"title":"The SLAM question","text":["SLAM means Simultaneous Localization and Mapping. Simultaneous means together. Localization asks where the robot is; mapping asks what the surroundings contain.","If the map is known, localization can match against it. If the pose is known, mapping can place observations. When both are unknown, they must be estimated together."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"The chicken-and-egg difficulty","text":["A wall observation belongs in the map only if we know the robot’s viewpoint. But the wall’s map position can help us infer that viewpoint.","Imagine drawing a building while walking through it with uncertain step lengths. Both your drawn walls and your believed position influence each other."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Full and online SLAM","text":["Full SLAM estimates the whole history of robot poses and the map. Online SLAM focuses on the latest pose and map while summarizing older evidence.","Marginalization means integrating out quantities we no longer explicitly keep, while preserving their influence in the remaining estimate."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"The original full/online equations","text":["Page 7 writes full SLAM as p(x₀:ₜ, m | z₁:ₜ, u₁:ₜ). Read this as probability of the pose history and map, given all observations and controls so far. x means pose, m map, z observation, u control and t the current step.","The colon means the whole sequence. Online SLAM keeps p(xₜ, m | z₁:ₜ, u₁:ₜ) by integrating the earlier uncertain poses out: ∫…∫ p(x₁:ₜ, m | z₁:ₜ, u₁:ₜ) dx₁…dxₜ₋₁, with the initial reference pose treated as fixed. The repeated integral sums over old pose possibilities."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"When it is useful","text":["SLAM helps when there is no prior map or suitable external positioning reference, such as indoor exploration, underground spaces or obstructed environments.","The lecture motivates autonomy, but autonomy does not always require SLAM: a robot can use a known map and localization. SLAM also need not run without any human driving or initial setup."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"SLAM estimates which pair together?","choices":["Map and robot pose","Battery and keyboard colour","Only wheel diameter"],"answer":0,"why":"Mapping and localization depend on each other when the environment is initially unknown.","remediate":"Draw the world while working out where you stand in that drawing."},{"prompt":"Full SLAM includes…","choices":["Only one current velocity","The complete path history and map","Only a file extension"],"answer":1,"why":"The full formulation estimates all past poses, not only the latest.","remediate":"Think of a whole breadcrumb trail rather than just its newest crumb."},{"prompt":"Can a robot navigate autonomously using a known map and localization?","choices":["No, every autonomous robot must remap","Only with 10,000 wheels","Yes; SLAM is not always necessary"],"answer":2,"why":"The mapping problem may already be solved before navigation starts.","remediate":"If someone gives you a good floor plan, you can locate yourself on it without drawing it again."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w9-s2","title":"SLAM methods and where people use them","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 9.pdf","pages":"8–10"},"cards":[{"title":"Several ways to solve the same problem","text":["EKF SLAM keeps robot and landmark estimates in a joint Gaussian approximation. FastSLAM uses particle-based alternatives with landmark estimation.","Grid-based SLAM can combine pose particles and map estimates. Graph-based SLAM stores poses/relations and optimizes them. Topological SLAM emphasizes recognizing places and connections."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Translate the names","text":["Rao–Blackwellized particle filtering means splitting an estimation problem so some parts use particles and others use conditional analytical estimation. You need the idea here, not a new derivation.","Graph optimization adjusts connected estimates to better agree with measured relationships. A constraint is one relationship those estimates should satisfy."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked use-case choice","text":["A 2D laser-equipped floor robot in rooms can use a grid/pose-graph map. Recognizable posts or trees can be landmarks in a feature map. A corridor route can be represented topologically.","The choice depends on sensors, environment and task, not on one method being best everywhere."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Lecture applications","text":["The slides include home cleaning and lawn mowing, aircraft surveillance, underwater exploration, mines and space terrain mapping.","These examples show the broad usefulness of map-and-pose estimation; they do not mean the same 2D LIMO configuration handles every environment."],"kind":"friendly"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Which approach directly stores connected poses and relationships?","choices":["Only a compass","Graph-based SLAM","Keyboard teleoperation alone"],"answer":1,"why":"A graph expresses pose nodes and measurement constraints between them.","remediate":"Picture dots for viewpoints connected by measured relationships."},{"prompt":"A recognizable tree trunk can be used as…","choices":["A landmark feature","A wheel command","A map file extension"],"answer":0,"why":"A feature is a detectable environmental structure that can be re-observed.","remediate":"Recognizable objects become references."},{"prompt":"Should one SLAM configuration be assumed best for air, sea and indoor floors?","choices":["Yes, always","Only if renamed","No, sensor and motion assumptions differ"],"answer":2,"why":"Different tasks need different state, sensor and map models.","remediate":"A floor map does not represent all the degrees of motion of an aircraft."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w9-s3","title":"Occupancy maps are built from many observations","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 9.pdf","pages":"11–18"},"cards":[{"title":"A grid map is a mosaic","text":["Discretizing means dividing continuous space into cells. A grid gives each cell occupancy information, describing whether an obstacle might be present.","The lecture’s binary explanation says free or occupied. Practical maps also preserve uncertainty and unknown, meaning there is not yet enough evidence."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Add each observation in the right place","text":["A laser ray generally provides evidence for free space before a detected return and an obstacle near the return, within the sensor model.","Page 14 combines repeated sensor observations into an increasingly complete map. Pages 15–17 compare ultrasound, laser and a campus-scale grid."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked memory example","text":["Added example: a 10 m × 10 m area at 0.05 m resolution contains 200 × 200 = 40,000 cells. At 0.025 m it contains 400 × 400 = 160,000 cells.","The grid is non-parametric here: instead of a short list of named landmark coordinates, it stores many cell values. Fine grids require substantial memory."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Raw odometry can distort a map","text":["Page 18 shows a distorted map from mapping with raw drifting odometry. A scan placed using a wrong pose can put the same wall in several inconsistent places.","Unknown is not free. An unseen room cannot safely be treated as empty just because it lacks obstacle measurements."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Why does wrong robot pose distort a map?","choices":["It changes the laser’s colour","It places observations at wrong map locations","It deletes all cell boundaries"],"answer":1,"why":"Mapping transforms sensor observations from the robot viewpoint into map coordinates.","remediate":"If you draw from the wrong place, the wall goes in the wrong place."},{"prompt":"Does an unknown cell prove there is no obstacle?","choices":["No, it lacks enough information","Yes, unknown means empty","Only for yellow cells"],"answer":0,"why":"No observation is not the same as an observation of free space.","remediate":"A room behind a closed door remains unknown."},{"prompt":"Halving grid cell width from 0.05 to 0.025 m gives how many cells in this example?","choices":["40,000","80,000","160,000"],"answer":2,"why":"Both grid dimensions double, so total count becomes four times 40,000.","remediate":"Count rows times columns, not only one dimension."}],"research":[{"title":"ROS 2 Humble OccupancyGrid message","url":"https://docs.ros.org/en/ros2_packages/humble/api/nav_msgs/index.html","addition":"Clarified that occupancy grids represent occupancy information; practical unknown space differs from confirmed free space.","anchor":"Week 9 lecture pages 12–14"}]},{"id":"w9-s4","title":"Feature SLAM: the robot sees landmark A","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 9.pdf","pages":"19–23"},"cards":[{"title":"Controls and relative observations go in","text":["The lecture denotes movement inputs U₁:ₖ = {u₁,…, uₖ} and observations Z₁:ₖ = {z₁,…, zₖ}. The colon means the range from first to kth step.","Wanted outputs are landmark map m = {m₁,…, mₙ} and robot path X₁:ₖ. Landmarks are stored in a common coordinate frame, while the sensors initially measure them relative to the robot."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Start the original A/B/C sequence","text":["Page 21 chooses a start with zero robot uncertainty for the simplified illustration. Pages 22–23 show the first measurement of feature A and an uncertainty ellipse around A.","An uncertainty ellipse is a stretched region showing where the feature might be. Zero uncertainty at the chosen start is a modelling simplification and fixes a reference frame; it is not proof of a GPS position."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked landmark position","text":["Added planar example: robot at (1 m, 2 m), facing 0°, sees a landmark 2 m straight ahead. Landmark estimate is (3 m, 2 m).","General conversion: landmark x = robot x + r cos(θ + β); landmark y = robot y + r sin(θ + β). r is measured range, β is bearing relative to robot heading, and cos/sin convert direction into axis components."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Repeat the three steps","text":["At every frame, meaning new sensor time step: predict movement, measure features, update robot and landmark estimates.","A feature seen once is uncertain. Do not store it as an exact pin merely because it has a name."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Does a range-and-bearing sensor directly give absolute map coordinates?","choices":["Yes, without robot pose","No, it first measures relative to the robot","Only when it is red"],"answer":1,"why":"The robot pose is needed to transform the measurement into the map frame.","remediate":"Two metres ahead depends on where you stand and which way you face."},{"prompt":"Robot (1,2), heading 0°, landmark 2 m ahead: its map position is…","choices":["(3,2)","(1,4)","(−1,2)"],"answer":0,"why":"Forward at heading zero adds 2 m to x and nothing to y.","remediate":"Add the forward displacement to the robot’s own position."},{"prompt":"In the simplified starting illustration, zero uncertainty means…","choices":["Every future movement is exact","No sensor errors ever","The chosen initial reference is treated as known"],"answer":2,"why":"The example anchors the starting pose while later measurement and motion uncertainty remain.","remediate":"A fixed starting pin does not make every later step perfect."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w9-s5","title":"Feature SLAM: move, observe B and C, keep correlations","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 9.pdf","pages":"24–27"},"cards":[{"title":"Movement grows the robot ellipse","text":["Page 24 moves the robot and broadens its possible pose. Page 25 measures new features B and C.","Page 26 gives B and C their own uncertainty, based on sensor error plus uncertainty in the observing robot. Page 27 moves again, broadening the pose uncertainty further."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Correlated means linked errors","text":["If the robot’s x position was wrong by 0.2 m when it mapped B and C, both landmark x estimates may share that 0.2 m error. Their errors are related.","A joint covariance records these relationships between the robot and landmarks. Treating them all as independent would throw away information needed for later correction."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked simple variance calculation","text":["Added one-dimensional example: landmark position = robot position + measured relative distance. If robot variance is 0.04 m² and independent distance variance is 0.01 m², landmark variance is 0.05 m².","Its standard deviation is √0.05 ≈ 0.224 m. This simple sum assumes the two input errors are independent; general covariance calculations also include cross terms."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Joint state grows with landmarks","text":["For two-dimensional point landmarks, a simple EKF SLAM state can be [robot x, robot y, robot θ, Ax, Ay, Bx, By, Cx, Cy]. Three robot quantities plus two for each of three landmarks gives nine entries.","This state and covariance belong to the SLAM estimate, not the fixed 15-variable robot_localization odometry state."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Why does uncertainty in B include robot-pose uncertainty?","choices":["The map filename is long","B must be moving","B is placed using the uncertain observing pose"],"answer":2,"why":"An uncertain viewpoint makes the absolute position inferred from a relative observation uncertain.","remediate":"An imprecise starting ruler position makes the endpoint imprecise too."},{"prompt":"With independent variances 0.04 and 0.01, the sum is…","choices":["0.05 m²","0.03 m²","0.4 m"],"answer":0,"why":"Independent additive errors have additive variances.","remediate":"Add squared spreads as variances, not their standard deviations."},{"prompt":"What does correlated mean here?","choices":["All landmarks are exact","Errors can vary together","Every sensor is broken"],"answer":1,"why":"A shared robot pose error can shift several mapped features together.","remediate":"Draw two landmarks from the same wrong viewpoint: both move together."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w9-s6","title":"Re-observe A: loop closure improves the shared map","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 9.pdf","pages":"28–30"},"cards":[{"title":"The familiar feature returns","text":["Page 28 re-observes A after moving and measuring B and C. This is loop-closure detection: recognizing previously mapped structure.","Page 29 uses that recognition to correct the robot estimate and reduce uncertainty in other correlated map features too."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Why the whole picture improves","text":["Old A links the new viewpoint to the earlier viewpoint. A correction can adjust their shared relationships, instead of treating the latest scan as an unrelated new object.","In a pose graph, loop closure adds a constraint between poses that recognize the same place. The optimizer adjusts estimates to better satisfy the combined constraints."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked intuitive example","text":["You walk a supposed rectangular route and your step counts end 0.5 m away from the start. Recognizing the same starting pillar gives evidence that the accumulated route estimate needs correction.","The correction is distributed according to model uncertainty; it is not necessarily a single 0.5 m jump assigned only to the last step."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"False loop closure is a serious mistake","text":["Two similar pillars can be confused. A wrong match can distort the map. Loop closure reduces uncertainty only when the correspondence and measurement models are appropriate."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What is loop closure?","choices":["Recognizing a previously mapped place or feature","Driving a circle without sensing","Deleting an old observation"],"answer":0,"why":"The key is a measured connection back to known structure.","remediate":"Returning to a place matters when you recognize it."},{"prompt":"Why can re-observing A improve B and C?","choices":["A physically pushes them","Their estimates share correlations through the robot state","Their names are alphabetical"],"answer":1,"why":"The joint estimate stores relationships that let one correction affect other quantities.","remediate":"Correcting a shared viewpoint can correct all objects placed from it."},{"prompt":"What can a false loop closure cause?","choices":["Guaranteed accuracy","Only a colour change","A distorted map"],"answer":2,"why":"A wrong correspondence supplies an incorrect constraint.","remediate":"Matching one corridor to a different similar corridor bends the estimate the wrong way."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w9-s7","title":"Original SLAM examples, interfaces and assigned reading","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 9.pdf","pages":"31–37"},"cards":[{"title":"Victoria Park and the tennis court","text":["Pages 31–32 show an outdoor vehicle using recognizable tree landmarks in Victoria Park. The map/path plots show feature estimates and the travelled route.","Page 33 compares drifting wheel odometry with an EKF-estimated repeated tennis-court trajectory. The corrected plot is structured because environmental features constrain the pose estimate."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Line features and SLAM Toolbox","text":["Page 34 replaces many raw scan points with detected line features. Page 35 introduces SLAM Toolbox and RViz visualization. RViz is ROS’s visual inspection application.","The illustrated inputs are laser scans and valid odom-to-base transforms. Outputs include an occupancy map and estimated pose with covariance."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked comparison task","text":["Look at the original tennis-court figures. Ask which plot preserves repeated court structure and which wanders. You do not need to infer exact centimetre error from a small image to see the qualitative difference."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Reading checkpoint","text":["The lecture assigns Chapter 5, section 5.8. Explain how the A/B/C example demonstrates shared uncertainty before opening the SLAM Toolbox demonstration.","SLAM Toolbox is graph-based; using it in the workshop does not mean it implements the same EKF algorithm as the earlier conceptual example."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Which outdoor landmarks appear in the Victoria Park example?","choices":["Keyboard buttons","Trees","Warehouse barcodes only"],"answer":1,"why":"The images show tree features used in the vehicle’s environment map.","remediate":"Distinct structures can provide re-observable landmarks."},{"prompt":"Does SLAM Toolbox have to use EKF SLAM because the lecture illustrated EKF?","choices":["No, it uses a graph-based approach","Yes, all SLAM methods are EKF","It cannot build maps"],"answer":0,"why":"The lecture compares concepts and then demonstrates a different practical implementation.","remediate":"Different tools can solve the same coupled map-and-pose problem."},{"prompt":"The tennis-court comparison illustrates…","choices":["Perfect raw wheel odometry","No need for observations","Environmental observations can constrain drift"],"answer":2,"why":"Feature-based estimates preserve the route structure better than the shown drifting odometry.","remediate":"Repeated recognizable court features help correct movement errors."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w9-s8","title":"Demonstration: SLAM Toolbox and processing modes","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 9 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"1–3"},"cards":[{"title":"A practical two-dimensional laser tool","text":["SLAM Toolbox estimates a map and pose using laser observations, an odometry transform and scan matching. Its graph-based approach can use loop closure.","The lecture mentions many possible SLAM sensors generally; this workshop’s SLAM Toolbox is specifically for 2D laser SLAM."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Synchronous and asynchronous","text":["Synchronous mapping processes accepted scans in sequence and can accumulate lag if computation falls behind. Asynchronous mapping uses a best-effort approach to stay responsive.","These names describe processing policy. They are not a universal rule that synchronous must be offline and asynchronous must be online; either is chosen for task and hardware."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Continue a saved mapping session","text":["A serialized pose graph preserves internal mapping state for later continuation. Serialization means storing structured internal data in files; deserialization loads it again.","Saving only a picture of the occupancy map is useful for display or AMCL, but it does not preserve the full resumable SLAM graph."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked decision","text":["For an added example, a slow computer receiving scans faster than it can process them may benefit from asynchronous mapping. For careful recorded-data processing, accepting lag may make synchronous processing suitable.","Lifelong mapping means continuing to refine a map over sessions; it still depends on valid matching and sensible configuration."],"kind":"friendly"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What is this SLAM Toolbox workshop primarily based on?","choices":["Only GPS indoors","2D laser scans and odometry transforms","A motor temperature graph"],"answer":1,"why":"The package’s practical mapping approach is planar laser SLAM.","remediate":"Its core observation is the horizontal laser view."},{"prompt":"What should you save to continue the internal mapping session?","choices":["Only a screenshot","Only a goal coordinate","The serialized pose graph"],"answer":2,"why":"The graph contains state that a flat image alone does not preserve.","remediate":"A drawing records appearance; the serialized graph records how the estimate was built."},{"prompt":"Must synchronous mode always be offline?","choices":["No, it is a processing policy","Yes, by definition","Only if renamed"],"answer":0,"why":"Live synchronous operation is possible if the processing and lag requirements are acceptable.","remediate":"The question is how scans are handled, not merely whether they were recorded."}],"research":[{"title":"SLAM Toolbox Humble README","url":"https://github.com/SteveMacenski/slam_toolbox/blob/humble/README.md","addition":"Verified graph-based 2D laser SLAM, processing modes, serialized graph versus map image, required transforms and matching of configured node names.","anchor":"Week 9 demonstration pages 2–5"}]},{"id":"w9-s9","title":"Demonstration: configuration and expected messages","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 9 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"4–5"},"cards":[{"title":"Read the illustrated launch","text":["Page 4 creates a ROS 2 node from package slam_toolbox, executable async_slam_toolbox_node, name slam, with parameters loaded from params/slam.yaml.","The supplied example_codes/params/slam.yaml begins with slam: then ros__parameters:, matching that node name. If the node name changes, its parameter namespace must match."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Frames and scan topic","text":["odom_frame is the local movement frame, map_frame is the world map frame, and base_frame is the robot body frame. scan_topic names the laser channel.","The node needs a valid odom → base_frame transform at scan time. It publishes map and pose estimates, and supplies map → odom alignment when enabled."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Parameters in the final slide","text":["solver_plugin selects the optimizer. mode selects mapping or localization. map_file_name refers to a serialized map to load. map_start_pose or map_start_at_dock specifies the starting relationship.","tf_buffer_duration stores transform history; stack_size_to_use relates to serialization/deserialization memory; enable_interactive_mode allows editing graph poses in RViz."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked read-only check","text":["Before a workshop launch, inspect the scan type, timing and transforms. The source comment saying transform_publish_period = 0 “never publishes odometry” is imprecise: it disables publishing that map-to-odom transform.","These checks inspect data; they do not start motion."],"kind":"danger","code":"ros2 topic list -t\nros2 topic info /scan\nros2 run tf2_ros tf2_echo odom base_link\nros2 param get /slam use_sim_time"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Why does the YAML top-level name slam match the launch name?","choices":["The node must receive its own parameter namespace","Every ROS node must be called slam","Only filenames matter"],"answer":0,"why":"Node-scoped parameters are applied under the configured node name.","remediate":"Put a letter in the recipient’s labelled mailbox."},{"prompt":"Which transform is required to relate scans to movement?","choices":["battery → keyboard","odom → robot base","goal → document"],"answer":1,"why":"The laser’s viewpoint must be tied to the robot pose in the odometry frame.","remediate":"The system needs to know how the robot body moved when the scan was taken."},{"prompt":"What is map_file_name used for in this context?","choices":["A screenshot caption","A velocity command","A serialized SLAM graph to load"],"answer":2,"why":"Continuation/localization needs the saved internal map state described by the package.","remediate":"Loading a graph restores relationships, not just picture pixels."}],"research":[]}],"note":"The lecture’s worked example is a visual EKF SLAM sequence with landmarks A, B and C. Added numerical examples are labelled as explanatory practice, not original worksheet answers."},{"number":10,"title":"Planning and Navigation I","sections":[{"id":"w10-s1","title":"Choose actions that can actually reach the goal","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 10.pdf","pages":"1–7"},"cards":[{"title":"Motion planning in one sentence","text":["Motion planning chooses feasible movements from a known start state to a desired goal state. Feasible means the robot can physically perform them.","A path describes where to go; a trajectory also describes movement over time, including speeds. A movement policy describes how to choose an action as circumstances change."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Goals and constraints","text":["We want to reach the goal without collision, often minimizing distance, time or energy. A constraint is a rule the movement must satisfy, such as maximum speed or turning limits.","Optimization means choosing the best candidate according to a stated cost while respecting those rules. The shortest drawn line may cross a wall or require an impossible turn."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Original route comparison","text":["Page 6 illustrates Path A reaching the destination at 150 seconds and Path B at 180 seconds. If both are feasible and travel time is the objective, A has the lower cost.","If the goal or terrain cost changes, another route could become preferable. “Best” always depends on what is being optimized."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Two challenges","text":["Plan despite uncertain actions, and react quickly to unexpected objects. Good planning needs a useful world representation, a robot motion model, current pose and goal pose."],"kind":"friendly"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What does feasible mean?","choices":["Drawn in a bright colour","Physically executable under the robot’s constraints","Always straight"],"answer":1,"why":"A robot must respect its actual movement limits and obstacle geometry.","remediate":"A car cannot instantly move sideways just because a line is drawn there."},{"prompt":"With travel-time cost, 150 s versus 180 s makes which path preferable if both are feasible?","choices":["The 150 s path","The 180 s path","Neither can ever be compared"],"answer":0,"why":"Lower travel time is better under the specified objective.","remediate":"First name the objective, then compare its numbers."},{"prompt":"A trajectory adds what to a geometric path?","choices":["Only the file name","Only the colour","Timing and motion information"],"answer":2,"why":"It specifies how motion proceeds over time, not only its shape.","remediate":"A route says which street; a trajectory says how fast and when."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w10-s2","title":"Global strategy and local reflexes","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 10.pdf","pages":"8–11"},"cards":[{"title":"The historical approaches","text":["The slides move from exact-model classical planning to sensor-reactive planning, then hybrid/hierarchical systems and probabilistic methods.","Reactive means responding directly to observations. A hierarchy splits a larger task into levels. Probabilistic methods represent uncertainty in sensing and action."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"The two-layer architecture","text":["A global planner considers the larger map and produces a route or subgoals. A local planner/controller considers immediate sensing and current motion to choose near-term commands.","The lecture diagrams show global planning at lower frequency and local collision avoidance at higher frequency. Frequency means how many updates happen per second."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked corridor example","text":["The global route says go through the corridor to the door. A person steps into the corridor. The local controller adjusts speed or direction, or stops; a global replan may be needed if the route becomes blocked.","Both levels still work toward the same final goal."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Obstacle avoidance needs context","text":["A local choice should consider the goal, current speed, robot kinematics, sensor limits and collision risk. Kinematics describes allowed motion geometry.","A controller that avoids a nearby wall can still fail to reach the final goal if it has no useful global guidance."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Which level responds most directly to a newly seen nearby obstacle?","choices":["The lecture filename","The local controller","Only the map author"],"answer":1,"why":"It uses current local sensing to change immediate commands.","remediate":"Strategy chooses the route; reflexes avoid what appears in front of you."},{"prompt":"What does a global planner mainly supply?","choices":["A route or subgoals through the wider map","Only motor temperature","Only wheel diameter"],"answer":0,"why":"It considers the overall destination and environment connectivity.","remediate":"Choose the hallway sequence before deciding the next wheel speed."},{"prompt":"Does collision avoidance alone guarantee arrival?","choices":["Yes, always","Only with a fast processor","No, it can avoid obstacles yet get stuck"],"answer":2,"why":"Local actions can lack the long-range information needed to escape traps.","remediate":"Avoiding every wall does not tell you which exit leads to the goal."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w10-s3","title":"Bug2: go straight, follow the boundary, leave when progress is possible","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 10.pdf","pages":"12–14"},"cards":[{"title":"Draw the m-line","text":["Bug2 connects the start and goal with a straight reference line called the m-line. Move toward the goal along it while clear.","When an obstacle blocks progress, follow its boundary consistently on the chosen left or right side."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"When to leave the obstacle","text":["At a new m-line crossing closer to the goal than the hit point, leave the boundary if the path toward the goal is clear. A hit point is where boundary following began.","If the robot returns around the boundary to the hit point without a valid leave point, the classical algorithm can report that this obstacle prevents reaching the goal."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Follow the original six pictures","text":["Page 13 shows a blue starting point, orange goal and blue polygon obstacle. The robot advances, reaches the obstacle, follows its boundary and leaves towards the goal when the line becomes useful again.","No full obstacle map is needed, but localization relative to start/goal and boundary sensing are still required."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Completeness has assumptions","text":["Complete means it finds a solution when one exists within the stated ideal problem assumptions, or detects failure. The lecture calls Bug2 complete but suboptimal: its route can be longer than necessary.","Ideal point-robot and reliable boundary-following assumptions do not imply guaranteed success for a noisy real robot in all environments."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What is the m-line?","choices":["The start-to-goal reference line","The battery wire","The map boundary"],"answer":0,"why":"Bug2 uses this line to define direct progress toward the target.","remediate":"Draw one line joining the starting dot to the goal dot."},{"prompt":"When is a boundary departure useful?","choices":["At every random point","At a closer valid m-line crossing with clear goal direction","Only when the wheel stops"],"answer":1,"why":"The departure must make progress rather than repeat the original blockage.","remediate":"Rejoin the goal line after getting past the obstacle’s blocking part."},{"prompt":"Does complete mean shortest route?","choices":["Yes, by definition","Only for circles","No, Bug2 can be complete and suboptimal"],"answer":2,"why":"Completeness is about finding a solution, not minimizing its length.","remediate":"Finding an exit and finding the shortest exit route are different goals."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w10-s4","title":"Dynamic Window Approach: test possible speeds","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 10.pdf","pages":"15–17"},"cards":[{"title":"Search commands instead of all future journeys","text":["DWA means Dynamic Window Approach. It samples achievable velocities over a short horizon, meaning a short future interval.","v is forward speed in m/s; ω, pronounced omega, is angular speed in rad/s. The dynamic window limits choices using current velocity and acceleration/deceleration capabilities."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Predict short arcs","text":["If v and ω stay constant briefly, a differential-drive robot follows a circular arc; ω = 0 gives a straight line. Radius r = v/ω when ω is nonzero.","Added example: v = 0.2 m/s and ω = 0.4 rad/s gives r = 0.5 m. Over one second the heading changes 0.4 rad and travelled arc length is 0.2 m."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"The five-step lecture loop","text":["Sample possible control velocities; simulate each short trajectory; score goal progress, global-path tracking, clearance and speed; discard illegal candidates; send the chosen velocity and repeat.","The slide’s dx, dy, dtheta are a general control-space description. A nonholonomic differential-drive robot cannot choose arbitrary sideways velocity; the sample space must respect its motion model."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Stopping is part of admissibility","text":["A candidate must allow the robot to brake before a modelled obstacle, given assumptions about sensing and braking. Added ideal formula: stopping distance = v²/(2a), for constant braking magnitude a.","At v = 0.4 m/s and a = 0.2 m/s², braking distance is 0.16/0.4 = 0.4 m. Real systems also need response-time and uncertainty allowance; the formula alone is not a complete safety guarantee."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What does DWA sample?","choices":["Only whole building maps","Feasible short-term velocity commands","Only landmark names"],"answer":1,"why":"It evaluates the trajectories generated by candidate controls.","remediate":"Try several nearby speed-and-turn combinations before choosing one."},{"prompt":"At v = 0.2 and ω = 0.4, the arc radius is…","choices":["0.5 m","2 m","0.08 m"],"answer":0,"why":"Radius is v/ω = 0.2/0.4.","remediate":"Forward speed divided by turn rate gives the turning radius."},{"prompt":"A differential-drive robot’s candidates must…","choices":["Include any sideways jump","Ignore acceleration","Respect its actual movement constraints"],"answer":2,"why":"Sampling impossible controls does not create executable trajectories.","remediate":"Only test moves the real platform can make."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w10-s5","title":"DWA scores, local traps and the missed doorway","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 10.pdf","pages":"18–26"},"cards":[{"title":"Strengths and limitations","text":["The lecture lists quick reactions, modest computation and practical use. It also warns about suboptimal routes, local minima and assuming static obstacles in the basic model.","A local minimum is a situation where immediate choices look unhelpful even though a longer route exists. Static means not moving during the prediction; moving people can violate that assumption."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"The displayed score expression","text":["Pages 19–25 display NF = α·vel + β·nf + γ·Δnf + δ·goal. α, β, γ and δ are weights multiplying component scores; NF is the combined navigation score.","The slides do not fully define each nf/Δnf/goal term or their units. Treat the equation as an illustrated weighted combination, not a complete executable controller specification."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Original doorway sequence","text":["The robot travels quickly along a horizontal corridor. At c₀ it should turn into the south-facing corridor, but it is too fast to make that entry.","It overshoots, the preferred direction flips, and it can return to a similar failure. Page 26 shows the corresponding real map doorway issue: the robot did not slow early enough."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked score idea","text":["Added example, not lecture coefficients: score = 2×goal-progress + 1×clearance + 0.5×speed. Candidate A with normalized values (0.8,0.2,0.9) scores 2.25; B with (0.7,0.8,0.4) scores 2.4.","Choose B only if both candidates satisfy collision and braking constraints. Never compensate for a collision by assigning a high speed score."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What happens in the original doorway problem?","choices":["The robot moves too fast to enter the turn","The goal disappears","The laser becomes a wheel"],"answer":0,"why":"The controller does not slow sufficiently early for the required change in direction.","remediate":"You can miss a doorway if you rush past before turning."},{"prompt":"What does a local minimum mean here?","choices":["The smallest map file","Immediate choices fail to reveal a route to the goal","The robot has arrived"],"answer":1,"why":"Short-horizon scoring can get stuck even when a larger-scale solution exists.","remediate":"You may need to move away first to later reach the goal."},{"prompt":"Can a high score make a colliding trajectory acceptable?","choices":["Yes, if fast","Yes, if nearest the goal","No, reject illegal trajectories first"],"answer":2,"why":"Safety/feasibility constraints are prerequisites before comparing valid candidates.","remediate":"Crossing a wall is not a good shortcut regardless of its score."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w10-s6","title":"Nav2 connects navigation components","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 10.pdf","pages":"27–31"},"cards":[{"title":"What Nav2 supplies","text":["Nav2 is the ROS 2 navigation framework. It receives a current pose and goal pose, plans a route and controls movement toward the goal.","Its modular servers include planning and control, allowing algorithms to be selected through plugins. A plugin is a replaceable algorithm component loaded behind a defined interface."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Read the original architecture diagram","text":["The map server supplies /map. AMCL uses map and /scan to align map and odom. The planner server computes a path using the global costmap.","The controller server follows the path using the local costmap and odometry, producing /cmd_vel movement commands for the base controller. The BT navigator coordinates the navigation request."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Behavior trees and lifecycle","text":["A behavior tree organizes decisions and actions using success, failure and running states. It can coordinate planning, following and recovery.","Lifecycle nodes move through managed states such as unconfigured, inactive and active, so the navigation system can start components in a controlled order."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Reading and implementation boundary","text":["The lecture assigns Chapter 6, sections 6.1–6.2 and 6.4. Nav2 supports application logic in Python, but do not assume its compiled planner/controller plugin interfaces are Python classes.","The diagram’s 10 Hz /cmd_vel label is an example frequency, not a fixed universal setting."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Which server mainly computes the global path?","choices":["The map image","The planner server","The keyboard driver"],"answer":1,"why":"Planning searches a route using the global environment representation.","remediate":"The planner answers which route; the controller answers which immediate command."},{"prompt":"What does the controller ultimately generate?","choices":["Movement velocity commands","Only a new README","Only a landmark list"],"answer":0,"why":"It translates path-following decisions into motion commands for the base.","remediate":"The wheels need a speed and turn command, not just a line on the screen."},{"prompt":"What do managed lifecycle states help with?","choices":["Only screen brightness","Changing the goal colour","Controlled setup and activation"],"answer":2,"why":"Components can be configured before being enabled to process navigation work.","remediate":"Prepare the system before asking it to act."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w10-s7","title":"Demonstration: modular Nav2 and behavior-tree coordination","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 10 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"1–2"},"cards":[{"title":"The navigator is a coordinator","text":["A behavior-tree navigator delegates jobs to planner, controller and behavior servers. Its logic decides when to plan, follow, retry or recover.","A server handles requests for a specific capability. A plugin supplies the algorithm inside a server."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"A simple tree example","text":["Added explanatory example: plan a path; follow it; if following fails, run a suitable recovery and try again under bounded rules.","A sequence normally progresses through children in order. A fallback can try an alternative after a child fails. These are core tree ideas, not a replacement Week 11 behavior-tree lecture."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Runtime selection and extensibility","text":["The demonstration emphasizes modular algorithms, runtime selection and support for different robot types. The chosen algorithms still require parameters and motion assumptions suited to the robot.","Replaceable modules help experimentation, but their input/output contracts must continue to match."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Check the provided launch spelling","text":["The local example launch names its node behaviour_server, while bt_nav.yaml has a behavior_server parameter block. British and American spelling differ here. Unless rewritten, node-scoped settings may not apply as intended.","The launch is a workshop template to inspect and adapt, not evidence that this exact configuration has been run successfully."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What is the BT navigator’s role?","choices":["Only to measure wheel rotation","To replace every sensor","Coordinate navigation tasks and decisions"],"answer":2,"why":"It organizes the use of the specialist servers.","remediate":"A coordinator chooses which specialist acts next."},{"prompt":"What is a plugin?","choices":["A replaceable algorithm component","A different physical wheel","A map screenshot"],"answer":0,"why":"It implements one capability behind a defined interface.","remediate":"Swap the route-search algorithm while keeping the same planning server contract."},{"prompt":"Why can behaviour_server versus behavior_server matter?","choices":["It only changes visual style","Parameter names must match the configured node","Both names are always automatically identical"],"answer":1,"why":"A parameter block for a different node name may not configure the intended node.","remediate":"Labels must match the mailbox receiving the configuration."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w10-s8","title":"Demonstration: global planner, footprint and costmap","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 10 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"3–4"},"cards":[{"title":"The global planner considers the larger route","text":["The planner works toward the final goal using the global costmap. A costmap is a grid assigning travel cost to regions, including obstacles and nearby risk.","The stored occupancy map says where obstacles are known. Costmap layers can combine that map with sensor-observed obstacles and inflation."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"The robot has a body","text":["A footprint is the robot’s ground outline; a robot radius can approximate a circular footprint. Inflation increases costs near obstacles to discourage paths too close to them.","A point-robot simplification works only when obstacle expansion or collision checking correctly accounts for the real body."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Read the supplied planner template","text":["example_codes/params/planner.yaml selects NavfnPlanner with use_astar: true. Its global costmap includes static_layer, obstacle_layer and inflation_layer, with resolution 0.05 m.","This actual template demonstrates why a global costmap is not necessarily purely static: it also includes an obstacle sensor layer."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked corridor check","text":["Added example: a 0.4 m-wide circular robot needs a corridor wider than 0.4 m, plus appropriate margin, to fit. A centre line alone cannot prove clearance.","The slide describes blue regions as nontraversable in its display. Cost colours depend on visualization; inflated cost is not always a hard forbidden region."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What does a footprint represent?","choices":["The robot body’s ground outline","The robot’s filename","The last mouse click"],"answer":0,"why":"Collision checks must account for physical size.","remediate":"A centre point is smaller than the real machine around it."},{"prompt":"Can a global costmap include live obstacle sensing?","choices":["Never","Yes, through an obstacle layer","Only when printed"],"answer":1,"why":"The supplied template explicitly combines static, obstacle and inflation layers.","remediate":"A global representation can include both the stored map and fresh obstacle information."},{"prompt":"Does every blue or inflated cell always mean the same forbidden cost?","choices":["Yes in all configurations","Only for small maps","No, inspect costs and display settings"],"answer":2,"why":"Colour is a rendering choice, and inflation often creates graded costs.","remediate":"Read the cost meaning, not only the pigment."}],"research":[{"title":"Nav2 Costmap 2D configuration","url":"https://docs.nav2.org/rolling/configuration_and_development/configuration_guide/core_servers/costmap_2d/","addition":"Clarified layered global/local costmaps, robot footprint/radius, resolution and inflation costs. Current documentation is used only for these shared concepts.","anchor":"Week 10 demonstration pages 3–6"}]},{"id":"w10-s9","title":"Demonstration: local controller and rolling surroundings","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 10 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"5–6"},"cards":[{"title":"The controller turns a path into motion","text":["The controller uses pose, odometry and local environment information to generate immediate velocity commands along the planned path.","A local costmap focuses on the surroundings relevant to the next movements. A rolling window can move with the robot rather than storing the entire building."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Fresh obstacles need fresh sensing","text":["A person or chair absent from the stored map can still appear in the current laser data. The obstacle layer can update the local costmap so the controller adapts.","A local costmap is a representation of observations; it does not guarantee every object is detectable by the available sensors."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Read the supplied DWB configuration","text":["The controller template selects dwb_core:: DWBLocalPlanner. DWB is Nav2’s DWA-style controller with configurable trajectory critics, which are scoring/checking components.","The template has max_vel_x: 0.1 m/s and max_vel_theta: 0.5 rad/s. Its local costmap uses global_frame: map, a 2 m × 2 m rolling window and sensor-derived obstacles. Local costmaps can also be configured in odom, but that is not this supplied template’s setting."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Version-aware configuration","text":["The supplied Humble template uses progress_checker_plugin, singular. Newer documentation uses progress_checker_plugins, plural; do not copy the new syntax into an old setup without checking compatibility.","A progress checker detects lack of meaningful movement; a goal checker decides whether the requested final pose is close enough."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What should detect a chair newly placed ahead?","choices":["Only the old map screenshot","Fresh sensing reflected in the local costmap","Only the README"],"answer":1,"why":"The controller needs current obstacles, not only previous map knowledge.","remediate":"Update your view when the room changes."},{"prompt":"Which plugin is selected in the supplied controller template?","choices":["dwb_core:: DWBLocalPlanner","AMCL as a velocity generator","A screenshot plugin"],"answer":0,"why":"That configuration chooses the DWB local path-following algorithm.","remediate":"Read the FollowPath plugin entry in controller.yaml."},{"prompt":"For Humble, should you blindly replace singular progress_checker_plugin with the latest plural form?","choices":["Yes, always","Only if the text is blue","No, preserve the distribution-compatible interface"],"answer":2,"why":"The documented schema changed across distributions.","remediate":"Instructions for a newer version may name settings an older version does not understand."}],"research":[{"title":"Nav2 DWB Controller documentation","url":"https://docs.nav2.org/jazzy/configuration_and_development/configuration_guide/controller_plugins/dwb_controller/","addition":"Verified trajectory-critic role and the explicit Humble singular progress_checker_plugin note; avoided newer schema changes.","anchor":"Week 10 lecture pages 15–18 and demonstration page 5"}]},{"id":"w10-s10","title":"Demonstration: navigation cycle and careful tuning","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 10 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"7–8"},"cards":[{"title":"Read the original five-stage cycle","text":["Goal: provide destination and orientation. Plan: compute a global route. Drive: controller issues commands. Sense: sensors update nearby obstacles. Adapt: adjust movement to avoid new hazards.","Sensing and adapting repeat during movement; the system can also replan when needed."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Which settings change behaviour?","text":["Global/local costmap size and resolution affect represented space and computation. Inflation affects obstacle proximity cost. Velocity and acceleration limits affect how quickly the robot moves and changes movement.","Acceleration is change in velocity per second. Deceleration is reducing speed. A cautious configuration commonly uses conservative limits and sufficient clearance."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked timing and size","text":["At 0.1 m/s, an ideal straight 1 m journey takes 1/0.1 = 10 seconds, before turns, braking or obstacles. A 3 m × 3 m local window at 0.05 m resolution has 60 × 60 = 3,600 cells.","These are added arithmetic examples; parameter choice still depends on the real robot, sensors and processor."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Tune for evidence","text":["Change one parameter, observe path tracking and collision clearance in simulation, then compare. A larger velocity limit is not automatically better if the doorway problem returns.","Read the effective parameters and keep node names consistent before concluding that a YAML edit changed the running behaviour."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"After sensors reveal an obstacle, which cycle stage adjusts movement?","choices":["File download","Adapt","Ignore"],"answer":1,"why":"The controller uses updated surroundings to revise its immediate commands.","remediate":"New information should change the next action."},{"prompt":"At 0.1 m/s, an ideal 1 m straight movement takes…","choices":["10 seconds","1 second","100 minutes"],"answer":0,"why":"Time equals distance divided by speed: 1/0.1 = 10.","remediate":"Ask how many one-tenth-metre steps fit into one metre."},{"prompt":"Why change one tuning parameter at a time?","choices":["To avoid using numbers","To make files longer","To attribute observed changes to a specific setting"],"answer":2,"why":"Several simultaneous changes make cause and effect difficult to identify.","remediate":"One experiment should isolate one main change."}],"research":[]}],"note":"This week teaches local collision avoidance before the Nav2 demonstration. Lecture formulas added in examples are clearly separated from the slide’s own undefined score terms."},{"number":11,"title":"Planning and Navigation II","sections":[{"id":"w11-s1","title":"Path planning: start, goal, world and allowed moves","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 11.pdf","pages":"1–5"},"cards":[{"title":"The building-route question","text":["The lecture asks how to reach SAN 413 from the building entrance. Planning needs a world representation, start, goal, allowed actions and a way to compare routes.","In the two-layer diagram, this week focuses on global path planning, after last week’s local collision avoidance."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"The grid-world simplification","text":["The lecture assumes the robot occupies one grid cell, knows its location and can move to any of eight neighbouring cells. These are four side neighbours and four diagonals.","Omnidirectional means it can move in any planar direction under this simplified model. A real differential-drive robot may need turning and forward movement instead."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked neighbour count","text":["For an interior cell (2,2), side neighbours are (1,2), (3,2), (2,1), (2,3). Diagonals are (1,1), (1,3), (3,1), (3,3).","At a map boundary or near obstacles, some moves are unavailable. The graph must include only legal transitions."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Planning assumptions become physical limits","text":["A point-cell model does not automatically account for body size, corner clearance or turning limits. The planner and collision model must match the actual robot before executing a route."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Which layer is this week mainly about?","choices":["Only wheel encoders","Global path planning","Only local velocity sampling"],"answer":1,"why":"The lecture extends last week’s local avoidance to route search.","remediate":"Choose the route through the map after learning the immediate reflexes."},{"prompt":"How many neighbours does the simplified interior grid cell have?","choices":["Eight","Two","Fifteen"],"answer":0,"why":"There are four side-adjacent and four diagonal cells.","remediate":"Draw a 3×3 square and count the cells surrounding its centre."},{"prompt":"Does an eight-neighbour point model automatically guarantee executable differential-drive motion?","choices":["Yes, always","Only if green","No, physical motion and clearance must be checked"],"answer":2,"why":"A grid transition can require movement a real platform cannot perform directly.","remediate":"A drawing’s shortcut still has to fit the machine."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w11-s2","title":"Turn the world into a graph","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 11.pdf","pages":"6–10"},"cards":[{"title":"Graph vocabulary","text":["A graph contains vertices, also called nodes, and edges. In a grid graph a free cell is a state/vertex and a permitted move is an edge.","A directed edge allows movement in its stated direction. A transition function tells us which next state results from an action."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"State, action and cost","text":["The lecture writes cost C: X × A → positive real values. X is the set of states; A is the set of actions; × means choose a state-action pair.","A route’s cost adds the costs of its chosen edges. Equal-cost grid moves allow simple breadth-first search; unequal edge costs need cost-aware methods."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Two representation approaches","text":["Combinatorial planning explicitly constructs connectivity and searches it. Sampling-based planning probes configurations and checks collisions while building its representation.","Discretization loses detail. Grid resolution affects accuracy, path shapes, memory and scaling to many dimensions. A dimension is an independent quantity needed to describe state."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked graph","text":["Added example: start S connects to A and B; both can connect onward to goal G. Edges S→A cost 1, A→G cost 5, S→B cost 2, B→G cost 2.","S→A→G totals 6. S→B→G totals 4. Two paths with the same number of edges can have different travel cost."],"kind":"friendly"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"In the grid-to-graph conversion, a free cell is a…","choices":["Sensor noise term","Vertex/state","Velocity command only"],"answer":1,"why":"The graph state represents a location the robot can occupy in the simplified problem.","remediate":"Dots represent possible states; connections represent moves."},{"prompt":"Which route has lower cost in the example?","choices":["S→B→G, total 4","S→A→G, total 6","Both cost 2"],"answer":0,"why":"Compare summed edge costs, not only the number of transitions.","remediate":"Add each route’s numbers before choosing."},{"prompt":"What can happen when grid resolution is too coarse?","choices":["All physical limits disappear","Sensor data become exact","Important geometry or passage detail is lost"],"answer":2,"why":"A discretized representation can merge distinct regions or remove narrow spaces.","remediate":"Big squares cannot preserve every small gap."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w11-s3","title":"Lattice, visibility and sampled graphs","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 11.pdf","pages":"11–13"},"cards":[{"title":"A lattice uses feasible motion pieces","text":["A motion primitive is a small allowed movement, such as a short forward arc. A state lattice chains these primitives into a graph.","The lecture’s car diagram shows branches made from possible turning movements, rather than arbitrary sideways jumps."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Visibility graph: connect visible obstacle corners","text":["In the illustrated two-dimensional polygon world, vertices include obstacle corners plus start and goal. Connect pairs whose straight-line segment is not blocked, then search the graph.","Visibility means an unobstructed straight connection. For a robot with size, obstacles must be expanded or full footprint collision checked; blindly touching a real corner is unsafe."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Randomly sampled graphs","text":["Instead of using every grid cell, sample configurations and connect suitable pairs with valid local paths. Random sampling can help explore complicated high-dimensional spaces.","A configuration describes the robot’s arrangement: for a floor robot it may be pose; for an arm it includes joint angles."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked choice","text":["A car with limited steering suits feasible motion primitives. A point robot among polygons can use a visibility graph. A multi-joint arm can benefit from sampling.","Every connection still needs collision checking; two valid endpoints can have a blocked segment between them."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Why use motion primitives in a lattice?","choices":["To represent feasible movement pieces","To remove all obstacles","To make every robot omnidirectional"],"answer":0,"why":"Allowed arcs preserve the robot’s movement constraints.","remediate":"Build the route from moves the robot can actually make."},{"prompt":"What makes a visibility-graph edge valid?","choices":["Its colour","An unobstructed connecting segment","Its alphabetical order"],"answer":1,"why":"A line passing through an obstacle cannot be traversed.","remediate":"You must be able to connect the two viewpoints without crossing a wall."},{"prompt":"Do collision-free endpoints guarantee a collision-free connection?","choices":["Always","Only with random sampling","No, the segment between them can cross an obstacle"],"answer":2,"why":"Validity must cover the whole motion, not just its start and end.","remediate":"Two rooms can be free even though the wall between them is solid."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w11-s4","title":"Uninformed search: BFS and Dijkstra","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 11.pdf","pages":"14"},"cards":[{"title":"Uninformed does not mean random","text":["Uninformed search uses problem structure without a special estimate of remaining distance to the goal. It systematically expands candidate states.","Breadth-first search, BFS, explores by number of actions from the start. Dijkstra expands by accumulated nonnegative cost. Uniform-cost search follows the same cost-ordering idea."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"What Dijkstra remembers","text":["Keep a tentative best cost from the start to each node. Select the smallest unsettled cost, inspect its outgoing edges and improve neighbour costs when a cheaper route is found.","Settled means the cheapest known distance has been finalized under the nonnegative-edge assumption. A parent pointer records how to reconstruct the selected route."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked weighted example","text":["Using S→A = 1, A→G = 5, S→B = 2, B→G = 2: start S at 0; discover A at 1 and B at 2; after A, G is tentatively 6; after B, improve G to 4.","The final route is S→B→G. BFS minimizes number of moves when all moves cost the same; Dijkstra handles differing nonnegative costs."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Choose the method by the objective","text":["A route with two expensive steps can cost more than a route with three cheap steps. Dijkstra is not a heuristic prediction of goal distance; it orders actual accumulated cost."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What does Dijkstra prioritize?","choices":["Only remaining straight-line distance","Accumulated cost from the start","Only number of letters in node names"],"answer":1,"why":"It expands the cheapest unsettled route cost, without a goal heuristic.","remediate":"Ask what the journey has cost so far."},{"prompt":"What is G’s final cost in the worked example?","choices":["4","6","1"],"answer":0,"why":"The route through B costs 2 + 2 and improves the earlier value 6.","remediate":"A tentative answer can be improved by a cheaper discovered route."},{"prompt":"When is BFS optimal for route cost?","choices":["With arbitrary negative edges","Always in every graph","When action costs are equal"],"answer":2,"why":"Only then does fewer actions directly mean lower cost.","remediate":"If each step costs one token, the fewest steps spend the fewest tokens."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w11-s5","title":"Breadth-first wavefront and search limits","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 11.pdf","pages":"15–20"},"cards":[{"title":"The original wavefront procedure","text":["Label the goal cell 0. Label each reachable unlabelled neighbour 1, then its reachable neighbours 2, and keep spreading out. Stop once the start is labelled.","Read a route from start by descending labels to 0. Cross only permitted connections; the original diagrams include walls on cell boundaries."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Follow the lecture route","text":["Pages 16–19 show an eight-column, five-row world with green start at bottom-left and red goal at bottom-right. The drawn route detours upward and across to pass the cell-boundary walls.","The start-to-goal route descends 17, 16, 15, …, 1, 0: seventeen unit-cost moves. A straight line along the bottom is blocked by the drawn walls."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Breadth-first versus depth-first","text":["BFS expands levels, so with equal action costs the first reached goal is shallowest/cheapest. Depth-first search, DFS, follows one branch deeply before backing up; it need not find the shortest route and can fail in unbounded search.","b means branching factor, the number of children per state; d is goal depth; m is maximum search depth. O(·) describes how required work grows with problem size."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Correct the printed complexity notation","text":["Page 20 prints O(bd) for BFS time/space and O(bm) for DFS time. Standard tree-search bounds are exponential: BFS roughly O(b^d); DFS time O(b^m) and stack space O(bm), with conventions affecting a one-level factor.","For b = 2, depth 5 has 2^5 = 32 nodes at that level, not 2×5 = 10. Finite graph implementations that mark visited nodes have different bounds in terms of vertices and edges."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"In the wavefront method, what label does the goal get?","choices":["17","0","−100"],"answer":1,"why":"All other labels count moves away from that zero-distance reference.","remediate":"Start the distance counting at the destination."},{"prompt":"How do you follow the wavefront route from the start?","choices":["Follow descending labels on legal connections","Always choose the largest label","Cross any wall"],"answer":0,"why":"Each decreasing step moves one unit-cost action nearer the goal.","remediate":"Count down until you reach zero."},{"prompt":"With branching factor 2, how many nodes can be at depth 5 in a full binary tree?","choices":["10","5","32"],"answer":2,"why":"Repeated branching multiplies: 2×2×2×2×2 = 32.","remediate":"Every level doubles the possible nodes, so use a power rather than one multiplication."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w11-s6","title":"A*: cost so far plus an optimistic estimate","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 11.pdf","pages":"21–24"},"cards":[{"title":"Informed search adds a clue","text":["A heuristic is an estimate of remaining goal cost. Informed search uses this extra information to prioritize promising candidates.","A* uses f(n) = g(n) + h(n). n is a node; g is actual cost from start so far; h estimates remaining cost; f estimates a full route through that node."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Distance heuristics","text":["Manhattan distance is |xgoal − x| + |ygoal − y|, counting axis-aligned changes. Absolute value bars mean ignore the sign of a difference.","Euclidean distance is √((xgoal − x)² + (ygoal − y)²), the straight-line distance. Both need to be scaled and selected consistently with movement costs."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked priority choice","text":["Added example: candidate A has g = 4, h = 3, so f = 7. Candidate B has g = 2, h = 6, so f = 8. A has the smaller estimated total and is considered first, subject to the search queue and tie rules.","A* compares a frontier of discovered candidates, not only the immediate-looking best neighbour."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"When the shortest-path claim holds","text":["An admissible heuristic never overestimates the true remaining cost. A consistent heuristic also respects edge costs across neighbours. Optimal A* requires appropriate assumptions and correct graph-search handling, including reopening improved nodes when needed.","Page 24’s point-grid illustration chooses moves around blocked cells. Its phrase “most intelligent choice at each step” must not be confused with guaranteed success from greedy local choices alone."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What is A*’s f(n)?","choices":["Only h(n)","g(n) + h(n)","g(n) × the filename length"],"answer":1,"why":"It combines spent cost and estimated remaining cost.","remediate":"Add what you already paid to what you expect to pay next."},{"prompt":"Candidate A has g=4, h=3; B has g=2, h=6. Which has smaller f?","choices":["A, with f=7","B, with f=8","Both have f=6"],"answer":0,"why":"7 is less than 8, so A has the better estimated total.","remediate":"Calculate both sums before comparing."},{"prompt":"An admissible heuristic must…","choices":["Always overestimate","Ignore the goal","Never overestimate true remaining cost"],"answer":2,"why":"Optimism prevents the search from wrongly dismissing a genuinely cheaper route under the algorithm’s assumptions.","remediate":"A lower-bound estimate can be too small but must not claim a larger unavoidable cost than reality."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w11-s7","title":"Original A* worked grid and its heuristic mismatch","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 11.pdf","pages":"25–27"},"cards":[{"title":"Copy the original problem faithfully","text":["Page 25 has four columns and five rows. Count columns from left, rows from top. Start is green at (1,5); goal is yellow at (4,1). Black blocked cells are (3,4) and (3,5).","The slide asks for a Manhattan heuristic, but its worked sequence includes diagonal moves with cost 1.4. Axis-aligned moves cost 1."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Follow the selected red boxes","text":["The illustrated route is (1,5) → (2,4) → (3,3) → (4,2) → (4,1). Added step-by-step reading of the printed values: at (2,4), g=1.4, h=5, f=6.4; at (3,3), g=2.8, h=3, f=5.8.","At (4,2), g=4.2, h=1, f=5.2. At the goal, g=5.2, h=0, f=5.2. Total cost is three diagonals plus one straight move: 3×1.4 + 1 = 5.2."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Why the Manhattan choice needs a caution","text":["Manhattan h at start is |4−1| + |1−5| = 3+4 = 7, while the illustrated path costs 5.2. Therefore it overestimates and is not admissible for these allowed diagonal costs.","For four-neighbour unit-cost motion, Manhattan is suitable. For eight-neighbour motion with cardinal cost 1 and diagonal cost 1.4, an appropriate obstacle-free lower bound is 1.4×min(dx, dy) + |dx−dy|; here it gives 1.4×3+1 = 5.2."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Corner clearance and general A* limits","text":["The diagonal (2,4) → (3,3) passes the corner of blocked cell (3,4), a permissive point-grid simplification in this example. A physical robot needs corner/footprint collision rules.","Keep the original 5.2 example visible, but do not present it as a proof that Manhattan always guarantees an optimal diagonal path. Page 26 links a search visualizer; page 27 emphasizes heuristic selection and representation limits."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What is the cost of the slide’s three diagonals plus one straight move?","choices":["7","5.2","4"],"answer":1,"why":"The printed diagonal cost is 1.4, giving 4.2 + 1.","remediate":"Count each diagonal as 1.4, not as two straight moves."},{"prompt":"Why is Manhattan h=7 not admissible for this example?","choices":["A shown legal path already costs only 5.2","The goal is yellow","Every heuristic is wrong"],"answer":0,"why":"A lower bound cannot exceed the cost of an available valid route under the same model.","remediate":"If you can get there for 5.2, saying at least 7 is too pessimistic."},{"prompt":"Before using the illustrated diagonal on a real robot, check…","choices":["Only the text colour","Only the goal label","Corner rules and physical footprint clearance"],"answer":2,"why":"A point can pass geometry that a body cannot safely pass.","remediate":"Expand the point into the real robot body and check the full motion."}],"practice":{"prompt":"Redraw the 4×5 lecture grid and write g, h, f at each selected route cell.","expected":"Reproduce the given 5.2 path and explain separately why its Manhattan heuristic does not have the usual admissibility guarantee."},"research":[]},{"id":"w11-s8","title":"RRT: grow a tree by testing sampled connections","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 11.pdf","pages":"28–31"},"cards":[{"title":"Why sample instead of filling every cell?","text":["A robot arm with many joints has a high-dimensional configuration space. A dense full grid can become enormous. Sampling-based planning probes a subset of configurations and checks connections.","RRT means Rapidly-exploring Random Tree. Begin with the starting configuration, repeatedly sample a target, find the nearest tree node and extend a short feasible motion toward the target."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Read the original pseudocode","text":["Page 30 starts the tree at qstart. With some probability β it chooses qgoal as target; otherwise it samples a random target. It finds qnear, constructs qnew a step towards that target and adds the edge only if the motion lies in free space.","Goal bias means sometimes sampling the goal directly. A distance measure chooses the nearest existing node. A local planner decides how to connect configurations feasibly."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked extension","text":["Added simple point-space example: qnear=(0,0), target=(3,4), step length 1. Distance to target is 5, so its unit direction is (3/5,4/5)=(0.6,0.8). qnew=(0.6,0.8).","Check the whole segment from (0,0) to (0.6,0.8) for obstacles before adding it. If blocked, reject that extension. Page 31’s images show progressively growing branches and a found red route."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Guarantees need the correct version and assumptions","text":["Basic RRT aims to find a feasible path; it is not a shortest-path guarantee. Probabilistic completeness is an asymptotic statement under suitable sampling and robust-path assumptions, not a promise to finish every finite trial.","Do not claim every geometric straight segment is dynamically feasible for a car or arm. The local motion model must enforce the relevant constraints."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"After sampling an RRT target, where does the extension begin?","choices":["At a new random physical robot","At the nearest existing tree node","Always at the goal"],"answer":1,"why":"The tree grows by connecting new valid motion to already connected states.","remediate":"Attach a new branch to the closest suitable part of the existing tree."},{"prompt":"For target (3,4) and unit step from (0,0), qnew is…","choices":["(0.6,0.8)","(3,4) always","(1,1)"],"answer":0,"why":"Divide the target direction by its length 5 to get a unit direction.","remediate":"A one-metre step along a five-metre vector uses one fifth of each component."},{"prompt":"Does basic RRT guarantee the shortest path it finds?","choices":["Yes, always","Only with many obstacles","No, its basic goal is feasibility"],"answer":2,"why":"Optimal variants are different algorithms; sampling alone does not guarantee shortest cost.","remediate":"A found passage can be useful without being the best passage."}],"research":[{"title":"OMPL RRT reference","url":"https://ompl.kavrakilab.org/classompl_1_1geometric_1_1RRT.html","addition":"Verified random-target, nearest-node, bounded-extension and goal-bias roles in the RRT lecture pseudocode.","anchor":"Week 11 lecture pages 29–31"},{"title":"LaValle Planning Algorithms, chapter 5","url":"https://lavalle.pl/planning/ch5.pdf","addition":"Clarified sampling-based feasibility and asymptotic completeness assumptions rather than finite-run or shortest-path guarantees.","anchor":"Week 11 lecture pages 28–31"}]},{"id":"w11-s9","title":"Nav2 planner plugins and the reading checkpoint","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 11.pdf","pages":"32–34"},"cards":[{"title":"Algorithms fit inside the planner server","text":["The lecture ends with Nav2’s planner plugin list. Nav2 provides a planning interface; different plugins implement different route-search methods.","Your supplied example_codes/params/planner.yaml chooses nav2_navfn_planner/NavfnPlanner and use_astar: true. This connects this week’s A* concept to the practical configuration."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Compare the algorithms you now know","text":["BFS explores equal-cost levels. Dijkstra orders accumulated cost. A* adds a suitable remaining-cost heuristic. RRT incrementally samples and grows feasible connections.","Different assumptions make each appropriate. A good route must also match the map representation and robot motion model."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"A tiny decision exercise","text":["For a uniform four-neighbour maze, BFS is enough to minimize move count. For unequal positive edge costs, Dijkstra works without needing a heuristic. For a helpful admissible goal estimate, A* can guide the search.","For a many-joint configuration space, sampling may avoid an impractical dense grid. This comparison follows the lecture topics; it is not a new curriculum week."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Assigned reading and outline discrepancy","text":["The lecture assigns Chapter 6, section 6.3, Path Planning. The actual Week 11 deck title is Planning and Navigation II; its content is the graph-search lesson you just completed.","The earlier outline’s Week 11 behavior-tree label conflicts with the supplied deck. Behavior trees appear in Week 10 Nav2 material; no missing replacement lecture is invented."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Which algorithm adds h to actual spent cost g?","choices":["BFS","A*","A wheel encoder"],"answer":1,"why":"A* prioritizes the estimated total route cost f=g+h.","remediate":"Remember spent cost plus optimistic remaining cost."},{"prompt":"Which supplied setting enables A* in NavfnPlanner?","choices":["use_astar: true","max_particles: 2000","scan_topic: scan"],"answer":0,"why":"That planner parameter selects the A* search mode.","remediate":"Look in the planner plugin settings rather than localization settings."},{"prompt":"What should we do about the outline/deck mismatch?","choices":["Invent a replacement lecture","Skip the supplied deck","Flag it and follow the actual supplied lecture order"],"answer":2,"why":"The user asked to preserve the material, so the discrepancy is made explicit instead of silently changing it.","remediate":"Teach the pages we have and label what their outline called them."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w11-s10","title":"Demonstration: Python Simple Commander","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 11 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"1–4"},"cards":[{"title":"Navigation as a library","text":["An API, application programming interface, is a set of functions your program can call. Simple Commander wraps navigation requests so Python applications can use Nav2 more easily.","BasicNavigator is its Python navigation helper. goToPose sends one goal; goThroughPoses sends a route through poses; followWaypoints visits poses with waypoint tasks."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Initialize, set a pose, wait for readiness","text":["rclpy.init() prepares the ROS 2 Python system. BasicNavigator() creates the helper. setInitialPose receives a PoseStamped. A stamped pose includes a coordinate frame and timestamp alongside position/orientation.","waitUntilNav2Active() waits for the chosen navigation/localization nodes to be active. An active ROS node is ready to perform its managed task; this is different from merely seeing a process listed."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"The required local terminal context","text":["First enter the existing course container on the host. Then source the ROS environment and built workspace inside it. Sourcing loads the package paths into that terminal.","These preparation commands preserve the established src/build/install/log workspace structure."],"kind":"friendly","code":"# On the host:\ndistrobox enter ros-humble-limo\n\n# Inside that container:\nsource /opt/ros/humble/setup.bash\ncd \"$HOME/robotics_ws\"\nsource install/setup.bash\nros2 pkg executables nav2_simple_commander\nros2 pkg executables example_codes"},{"title":"Names and script availability","text":["The original demo lists example_nav_to_pose.py, example_nav_through_poses.py and example_waypoint_follower.py. The local assessment setup.py registers console entrypoints for these examples.","Inspect code before a simulation run: the local example_nav_to_pose.py has a commented TransformListener and may send an invalid default initial pose; it also changes its target after 18 seconds. Treat it as an example to understand, not a guaranteed ready-to-run robot mission."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What does PoseStamped add to position/orientation?","choices":["Only a screenshot","Coordinate frame and timestamp","Only a battery percentage"],"answer":1,"why":"The system needs to know what frame the coordinates use and when they apply.","remediate":"A location number needs a map reference and a time."},{"prompt":"Which method waits for managed Nav2 readiness?","choices":["waitUntilNav2Active()","getFeedback() only","The filename alone"],"answer":0,"why":"It waits for the relevant lifecycle nodes to activate.","remediate":"Wait until the navigation service is ready before giving it a mission."},{"prompt":"Where does distrobox enter ros-humble-limo run?","choices":["Only inside an already running ROS node","Inside a PDF","On the host before the in-container commands"],"answer":2,"why":"The command enters the container; the later environment setup belongs inside it.","remediate":"First enter the classroom, then use the tools inside the classroom."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w11-s11","title":"Demonstration: send goals, read feedback, check the result","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 11 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"5–6"},"cards":[{"title":"A navigation request takes time","text":["goToPose(goal_pose) requests movement toward a goal. Treat the method as issuing a request, not proving arrival. Feedback gives ongoing progress while the action runs.","An action is a long-running request that can supply feedback and a final result. Goal acceptance, progress and success are different stages."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Feedback and completion","text":["The lecture lists current pose, elapsed/estimated remaining time, distance remaining and recovery count. Feedback may be absent briefly, so check it exists before reading its fields.","Poll isTaskComplete(); then use getResult(). Crucial correction to page 4: complete can mean succeeded, failed or canceled. It does not mean only “robot reached the goal”."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Humble-compatible checking pattern","text":["Illustrative API pattern below; it is teaching code for an already prepared simulation, not a command to move a live robot. Duration.from_msg converts the ROS duration message into a duration object.","Humble NavigateToPose feedback uses navigation_time; the slide code’s navigation_duration numeric comparison is not the matching field/type. TaskResult names the outcome."],"kind":"friendly","code":"from nav2_simple_commander.robot_navigator import TaskResult\nfrom rclpy.duration import Duration\n\n# After a simulation goal has been sent:\nwhile not navigator.isTaskComplete():\n    feedback = navigator.getFeedback()\n    if feedback is not None:\n        elapsed = Duration.from_msg(feedback.navigation_time)\n        if elapsed > Duration(seconds=600.0):\n            navigator.cancelTask()\n\nresult = navigator.getResult()\nif result == TaskResult.SUCCEEDED:\n    print(\"Arrived\")\nelif result == TaskResult.CANCELED:\n    print(\"Canceled\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Failed or unknown result\")"},{"title":"Worked application and limits","text":["A warehouse application can request a shelf pose, wait for success and then perform its delivery task. If navigation fails, it should report failure or recover, not claim the delivery arrived.","One fixed goToPose request does not itself perform ongoing tracking of a moving person. The lecture mentions dynamic-object following as a use case, but the application needs new target observations and appropriate updates."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"isTaskComplete() is True. What must you do before claiming arrival?","choices":["Assume every completed task succeeded","Inspect getResult() for success","Delete the map"],"answer":1,"why":"Failed and canceled actions are also complete.","remediate":"Finished means it stopped running; success means it achieved the goal."},{"prompt":"Which Humble feedback field is used for elapsed navigation time?","choices":["navigation_time","navigation_duration as a plain number","filename_length"],"answer":0,"why":"The verified Humble action and local example use navigation_time, a duration message.","remediate":"Use the version’s actual field and convert its duration type."},{"prompt":"Does one fixed goal automatically follow a moving person indefinitely?","choices":["Yes, by itself","Only when goal is blue","No, ongoing tracking needs updated target information"],"answer":2,"why":"A fixed goal represents one pose, not an unlimited stream of moving positions.","remediate":"Chasing a moving target requires observing where it moved next."}],"research":[{"title":"Nav2 Simple Commander Humble source","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ros-navigation/navigation2/humble/nav2_simple_commander/nav2_simple_commander/robot_navigator.py","addition":"Verified completion includes failure/cancelation; getResult distinguishes outcomes. Confirmed Humble methods and readiness flow instead of newer API additions.","anchor":"Week 11 demonstration pages 3–5"},{"title":"Nav2 Simple Commander current documentation","url":"https://docs.nav2.org/rolling/configuration_and_development/simple_commander_api/simple_commander_api/","addition":"Checked request/poll/result usage and explicit cancelation when changing task type. Newer signatures were not imported into Humble examples.","anchor":"Week 11 demonstration pages 3–5"},{"title":"Nav2 NavigateToPose Humble action definition","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ros-navigation/navigation2/humble/nav2_msgs/action/NavigateToPose.action","addition":"Verified navigation_time is a duration message; corrected the slide snippet’s navigation_duration numeric comparison.","anchor":"Week 11 demonstration pages 4–5"}]},{"id":"w11-s12","title":"Demonstration: frontier exploration combines SLAM and navigation","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 11 Demonstration.pdf","pages":"7–8"},"cards":[{"title":"A frontier is the edge of what you know","text":["Exploration actively moves to gather information in an unknown or partly known environment. A frontier separates known free space from unknown space.","It is not simply the outer wall of a room. Moving towards a reachable frontier can expose more surroundings to the sensors."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"The lecture’s repeated loop","text":["Keep SLAM updating the map. Detect candidate frontiers. Choose a suitable reachable candidate as a goal. Navigate towards it. Reconsider when progress, map changes or a replanning trigger demands it.","SLAM and navigation run alongside each other; new map information changes the next useful goals."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Worked reachable-choice example","text":["Added example: frontier A is 2 m away but across a blocked doorway; frontier B is 3 m away with a free reachable approach. B is a usable goal despite being farther in straight-line distance.","Choose an approach pose in known reachable free space near the frontier, rather than blindly commanding an unobserved occupied/unknown cell."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Read the supplied exploration parameters","text":["example_codes/params/explore.yaml uses /global_costmap/costmap and its update topic. It includes planner_frequency: 0.2, progress_timeout: 15.0 and min_frontier_size: 0.6.","0.2 updates per second means a nominal five-second period. An exploration node is an additional component; these parameters alone do not instantiate it. “Find all boundaries” is an ideal objective, limited by reachability, sensing and stopping rules."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What is a frontier?","choices":["Any wall with a name","Boundary between known free and unknown space","Only the goal pose"],"answer":1,"why":"It marks an opportunity to obtain information beyond currently mapped free space.","remediate":"Stand at the edge of the area you have already explored."},{"prompt":"Frontier A is closer but blocked; B is farther and reachable. Which is a usable next target?","choices":["B, with a reachable approach","Always A because it is closer","Neither can ever be used"],"answer":0,"why":"Reachability is necessary before distance preferences matter.","remediate":"A nearby door through a solid wall is not a usable route."},{"prompt":"At planner_frequency 0.2 Hz, the nominal period is…","choices":["0.2 seconds","Two hours","5 seconds"],"answer":2,"why":"Period is 1/frequency = 1/0.2.","remediate":"One update every five seconds is one fifth of an update per second."}],"practice":{"prompt":"Explain the full course connection in your own words: sensors → belief/pose → map or SLAM → global path → local commands → exploration goals.","expected":"Keep the roles distinct and explain how feedback loops update them. On a failed section quiz, reread that section from its first card and retry it before continuing."},"research":[]}],"note":"The supplied outline calls Week 11 behavior trees, but this original lecture is graph/path planning; the demonstration is Simple Commander and exploration. Preserve this actual sequence. Page 25’s Manhattan/diagonal example and page 20’s printed complexity notation need the explicit cautions below."},{"number":12,"title":"Recap/Assessment support","support":true,"note":"No Week 12 deck or current assessment brief was supplied. These are labelled recap/support activities anchored to the supplied lectures and code audit.","sections":[{"id":"w12-s1","title":"Recap · One complete robot loop","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 1 Student.pdf","pages":"10, 19–29"},"cards":[{"title":"Week 12 is recap and assessment support","text":["The original outline names Week 12 Recap/Assessment support. No separate Week 12 file or current assignment brief was supplied. This recap uses the lectures and code already read.","The chain is sense→estimate→plan→control→check. Earlier lessons introduced the parts in order; now explain their connections."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Follow one obstacle through the system","text":["A LiDAR beam measures a range. Its frame tells us where that beam starts. Pose estimation relates the robot to the map. A costmap marks obstacle risk and robot clearance.","A planner proposes a route. A controller chooses feasible velocities. Sensors check what changed. A navigation action reports feedback and its final result."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Follow one suspected pothole","text":["The camera gives an image. An image-processing method proposes a region. Depth or calibrated geometry is needed to estimate physical position and dimensions. A timestamped transform relates that estimate to the map.","Repeated views need to be associated with the same physical pothole. A picture alone does not prove an accurate map location, area or distinct-object count."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Separate a starter from an achieved result","text":["The supplied assessment detector contains aTODO and can publish a default marker at the origin. Its waypoint clients include unfinished initial-pose handling. A node appearing in a graph does not prove it completed the road-inspection task.","Inspect each assumption and keep evidence when it works. These are findings from reading the supplied code, not robot tests performed during game creation."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"What connects a sensor point to a map position?","choices":["Only a colour threshold","A valid pose/frame transform at the appropriate time","A larger screenshot"],"answer":1,"why":"Measurements live in sensor frames; the robot/map relationship is needed to express them in the map.","remediate":"Name both coordinate systems and the relationship between them."},{"prompt":"A detector repeatedly publishes a marker at(0,0). Does that prove pothole detection?","choices":["No, the code may be publishing a default placeholder","Yes, one pothole always lives there","Yes, if the marker is red"],"answer":0,"why":"A marker is an output display. You must verify actual image evidence and valid geometry behind it.","remediate":"Open the code and find where the measured location replaces the default pose."},{"prompt":"A navigation action is finished. What next?","choices":["Assume success","Reinstall ROS","Check its result status and evidence"],"answer":2,"why":"Completion includes failure or cancellation; the outcome must be inspected.","remediate":"“The attempt ended”is different from“the attempt achieved the goal.”"}],"practice":{"prompt":"Draw your end-to-end system, labelling each arrow with the actual message type and frame where relevant.","expected":"You can trace observations through pose estimation and planning to/cmd_vel, and explain what proves completion."},"research":[]},{"id":"w12-s2","title":"Assessment support · Test one claim at a time","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 1 Student.pdf","pages":"12–15"},"cards":[{"title":"Start with the supplied task","text":["The Week 1 lecture describes autonomous surveying, pothole detection/localisation/geometry estimation, and reporting potholes on a map with quantities. This support translates those stated aims into evidence you can collect.","It is a study checklist, not an unseen marking rubric. Verify final submission requirements against your current course brief."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"A small test grows into a complete test","text":["First confirm the simulator and observations. Then confirm one valid navigation goal. Next test several waypoints and coverage. Separately check a known visible pothole against an annotated image and known map location.","Finally combine them. Record conditions, inputs, outputs and failure cases. A single successful screenshot cannot establish performance across the whole area."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Measure what your claim means","text":["For detection, record true detections, misses and false detections. For location, compare estimated map coordinates with reference coordinates. For area, report the unit and explain the pixel-to-metre method.","For counting, test repeated views and close neighbouring potholes. For autonomy, record whether manual intervention was needed. Keep raw observations and timestamps so another person can inspect the claim."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Be honest about evidence","text":["This game does not execute robot tests. Goals, waypoints and performance measurements need their own practical evidence. Do not describe a proposed algorithm or starter script as a demonstrated result.","If a test fails, save the failure explanation and repeat after a justified change. That is useful engineering evidence."],"kind":"danger"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Which evidence supports a localisation-accuracy claim?","choices":["A visually attractive icon","Only a/cmd_vel message","Estimated positions compared with reference positions"],"answer":2,"why":"Accuracy concerns the difference between an estimate and a suitable reference.","remediate":"A dot on a map must be compared with where the object actually is."},{"prompt":"Why test repeated views of the same pothole?","choices":["To check it is not counted as several objects","To increase the count intentionally","To change the image resolution"],"answer":0,"why":"The same physical object can appear in many frames. Counting detections is not automatically counting objects.","remediate":"Seeing one chair in five photos does not mean you own five chairs."},{"prompt":"Does this study checklist establish the official marking rubric?","choices":["Yes","No, it is derived support based on the supplied lecture aims","Only if printed"],"answer":1,"why":"The current assignment brief was not supplied; lecture aims guide learning but do not replace official requirements.","remediate":"Separate what the lecture states from extra suggestions for testing it."}],"research":[]},{"id":"w12-s3","title":"Assessment support · Explain, demonstrate, and reflect","source":{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 1 Student.pdf","pages":"6, 12–15"},"cards":[{"title":"The assessment split in your supplied lecture","text":["Week 1 page 12 states a 3500-word research report worth65%, and a robot demonstration worth35%. It describes five minutes plus questions, and a submitted demonstration video.","Those are sourced lecture statements. Deadlines, the current detailed brief, and its grading rubric are not in these files; check the course’s current instructions before submission."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"A report tells a reasoned engineering story","text":["Explain the problem and chosen methods. Define the relevant assumptions. Show how the system was built and tested. Present results with units and useful figures. Discuss what failed, limitations, and what the evidence supports.","If outside research informed a choice, cite it and say what you used from it. Do not present copied code or another source’s claims as your own measurements."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"A demonstration shows the behaviour","text":["Practise a concise sequence: mission and setup, autonomous movement, detection/map output, then one clear result and one limitation. Include the required evidence and video according to the current brief.","A reset/recovery plan is useful, but do not conceal manual intervention while claiming autonomy. The five-minute figure comes from the lecture; timing of your actual assessment must follow the brief."],"kind":"friendly"},{"title":"Robotics has human consequences","text":["The learning outcomes include ethics and social implications, software engineering, and independent enquiry. Ask what happens when perception misses a defect or movement becomes unsafe.","Explain uncertainty and limits honestly. Use simulation for learning, then validate hardware separately. Your blue/red/yellow notes are reminders; they are not a safety certification."],"kind":"friendly"}],"quiz":[{"prompt":"Which assessment figures are stated on Week 1 page 12?","choices":["Report 35%, demo65%","Report 65%, demo35%","Only a multiple-choice exam"],"answer":1,"why":"The supplied lecture states65%report and 35%demonstration.","remediate":"Read the source page; keep its statement separate from any later official changes."},{"prompt":"What should your report say about external research?","choices":["Identify/cite it and explain how it informed your work","Hide it","Call it your own measurement"],"answer":0,"why":"Source attribution makes the reasoning inspectable and distinguishes research from your results.","remediate":"Readers should know which idea came from a source and which result came from your test."},{"prompt":"Why disclose a detector’s limitations?","choices":["Because all robots are useless","Because limitations replace testing","Because users and reviewers need to understand where its evidence applies"],"answer":2,"why":"Ethical and engineering judgement includes communicating uncertainty and failure conditions.","remediate":"Someone relying on road-inspection results needs to know what the system might miss."}],"practice":{"prompt":"Give a 60-second explanation of your proposed system using only words you can define.","expected":"You can state the mission, the sensor/pose/planning/control flow, the evidence collected, and one honest limitation."},"research":[]}]}],"inventory":[{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 1 Student.pdf","description":"Original teaching file read, including essential diagrams: 33 pages."},{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 1 Demonstration.pdf","description":"Original teaching file read, including essential diagrams: 37 pages."},{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 2 2026 lecture.pdf","description":"Original teaching file read, including essential diagrams: 35 pages."},{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 2 Demonstration.pdf","description":"Original teaching file read, including essential diagrams: 27 pages."},{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 3 2026_v3.pptx","description":"Original teaching file read, including essential diagrams: 29 slides."},{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 3 Demonstration.pdf","description":"Original teaching file read, including essential diagrams: 20 pages."},{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 4.pdf","description":"Original teaching file read, including essential diagrams: 29 pages."},{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 4 Demonstration.pdf","description":"Original teaching file read, including essential diagrams: 25 pages."},{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 5.pdf","description":"Original teaching file read, including essential diagrams: 48 pages."},{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 5 Demonstration.pdf","description":"Original teaching file read, including essential diagrams: 14 pages."},{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 7.pdf","description":"Original teaching file read, including essential diagrams: 39 pages."},{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 7 Demonstration.pdf","description":"Original teaching file read, including essential diagrams: 6 pages."},{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 8.pdf","description":"Original teaching file read, including essential diagrams: 43 pages."},{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 8 Demonstration.pdf","description":"Original teaching file read, including essential diagrams: 9 pages."},{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 9.pdf","description":"Original teaching file read, including essential diagrams: 37 pages."},{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 9 Demonstration.pdf","description":"Original teaching file read, including essential diagrams: 5 pages."},{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 10.pdf","description":"Original teaching file read, including essential diagrams: 31 pages."},{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 10 Demonstration.pdf","description":"Original teaching file read, including essential diagrams: 8 pages."},{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 11.pdf","description":"Original teaching file read, including essential diagrams: 34 pages."},{"file":"Robotics & Automation - Week 11 Demonstration.pdf","description":"Original teaching file read, including essential diagrams: 8 pages."}]};
